/* Built by tools/build-split.py from data.js — do not edit. e3b, SDLs 30, 31, 32, 33, 34, 35, 36 */
(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3b.deb52d4d72.js"]={"30":[{"id":"sdl30_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has an inulin clearance of 90 mL/min and a para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) clearance of 300 mL/min. What is the filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"0.20","B":"3.3","C":"0.25","D":"0.70","E":"0.30"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Filtration fraction is the fraction of renal plasma flow filtered across the glomerular capillaries: GFR (inulin clearance) divided by RPF (PAH clearance), here 90/300 = 0.30. 0.20 is the normal value, at which 20% of RPF is filtered and 80% leaves through the efferent arterioles into the peritubular capillaries. 3.3 inverts the ratio, dividing RPF by GFR. 0.70 is the fraction of plasma flow that is not filtered. 0.25 does not follow from these clearances.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Filtration fraction = GFR/RPF = inulin clearance/PAH clearance, normally about 0.20; a higher filtration fraction raises peritubular capillary protein concentration and proximal reabsorption. COMLEX: A rising filtration fraction with a falling RPF suggests efferent constriction, as with angiotensin II in volume depletion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — PAH clearance estimates effective renal plasma flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"A ureteral stone acutely raises Bowman space hydrostatic pressure. Which change in renal hemodynamics results?","choices":{"A":"GFR falls, RPF falls, and filtration fraction is unchanged","B":"GFR falls, RPF rises, and filtration fraction falls","C":"GFR, RPF, and filtration fraction remain unchanged","D":"GFR falls, RPF is unchanged, and filtration fraction falls","E":"GFR is unchanged, RPF rises, and filtration fraction falls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Increased Bowman space pressure opposes filtration and reduces net ultrafiltration pressure, so GFR falls, but plasma flow through the glomerulus is not altered, so RPF is unchanged and the filtration fraction, GFR divided by RPF, decreases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: postrenal obstruction lowers GFR by raising PBS without changing RPF. COMLEX: think hydronephrosis with a fall in GFR after ureteral obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar changes govern glomerular capillary pressure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"Which change would increase the net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Ureteral constriction","B":"Rising plasma protein concentration","C":"Dilation of the afferent arteriole","D":"Afferent arteriolar constriction","E":"Reduced glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Afferent dilation increases glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, which is the main force favoring filtration, and raises net ultrafiltration pressure and GFR. Ureteral obstruction raises Bowman space pressure, higher plasma protein raises capillary oncotic pressure, and afferent constriction and a fall in capillary hydrostatic pressure all reduce net filtration pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PBS and capillary oncotic pressure oppose filtration, and only PGC favors it. COMLEX: use the Starling equation to predict the effect of any single change.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar changes govern glomerular capillary pressure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Approximately what is the lower limit of mean arterial pressure below which renal blood flow autoregulation fails?","choices":{"A":"40 mm Hg","B":"60 mm Hg","C":"100 mm Hg","D":"120 mm Hg","E":"80 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal blood flow and GFR remain nearly constant when mean arterial pressure lies between about 80 mm Hg and the upper autoregulatory limit. Below about 80 mm Hg (BRS and Costanzo; Guyton and the course notes give a range of about 75 to 160 mm Hg), autoregulation fails and both flow and filtration fall with pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoregulation of RBF spans roughly 80 to 200 mm Hg in BRS and Costanzo (75 to 160 mm Hg in Guyton), and hypotension below 80 mm Hg reduces GFR. COMLEX: severe hypotension can therefore cause acute tubular injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback maintain constant flow and filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of intrinsic mechanisms accounts for autoregulation of renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin II and aldosterone secretion","B":"Myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback","C":"Sympathetic tone and vasopressin secretion","D":"Prostaglandin and bradykinin release","E":"Natriuretic peptide and renin release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The myogenic mechanism causes afferent arterioles to contract when stretched, and tubuloglomerular feedback causes afferent constriction when the macula densa senses increased NaCl delivery. Both are intrinsic to the kidney, whereas the other pairs are neurohumoral or paracrine mediators that modulate flow rather than autoregulate it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoregulation is intrinsic and works by changing renal vascular resistance. COMLEX: this is why renal blood flow remains stable with modest changes in systemic pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback maintain constant flow and filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Macula densa cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus respond directly to which stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Stretch of the renal arteriolar wall","B":"Plasma renin concentration in the peritubular blood","C":"Colloid osmotic pressure of peritubular capillary blood","D":"NaCl delivery to the early distal tubule","E":"Urine flow rate within the bladder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The macula densa senses NaCl delivery to the early distal tubule. Increased delivery signals afferent constriction through tubuloglomerular feedback, and reduced delivery promotes afferent dilation and renin release from the granular cells. Wall stretch is sensed by smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole in the myogenic response. Peritubular capillary oncotic pressure governs proximal reabsorption, not macula densa signaling, and bladder urine flow lies downstream of the nephron.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the macula densa is the sensor and the afferent arteriole is the effector of tubuloglomerular feedback. COMLEX: loop diuretics blunt this sensing by blocking NKCC2 at the macula densa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback maintain constant flow and filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":1,"stem":"Which substance is freely filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR?","choices":{"A":"Uric acid","B":"Glucose","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Potassium","E":"Inulin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inulin is filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so the amount excreted equals the amount filtered and its clearance equals GFR. Glucose and bicarbonate are filtered and almost completely reabsorbed, and potassium and uric acid are both reabsorbed and secreted, so their clearances differ from GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: relative clearances run PAH greater than inulin, urea, sodium, and glucose. COMLEX: creatinine approximates inulin clinically, with slight secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — saturable carrier kinetics define transport maximum, threshold, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":1,"stem":"What does the clearance of PAH measure at low concentrations?","choices":{"A":"Effective renal plasma flow","B":"True glomerular filtration rate","C":"Total renal blood flow","D":"Filtration fraction","E":"Renal vascular resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PAH is filtered and secreted by the proximal tubule, so nearly all of the PAH reaching the kidney is cleared, and its clearance measures effective renal plasma flow, which underestimates true RPF by about 10 percent. GFR is measured by inulin, and blood flow requires correction for hematocrit. Filtration fraction is GFR divided by RPF, so it needs inulin clearance as well as PAH clearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RBF equals RPF divided by 1 minus the hematocrit. COMLEX: PAH clearance is valid only at plasma concentrations below its transport maximum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — saturable carrier kinetics define transport maximum, threshold, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":1,"stem":"At approximately what plasma glucose concentration does glucose first appear in the urine?","choices":{"A":"About 100 mg/dL","B":"About 150 mg/dL","C":"About 350 mg/dL","D":"About 200 mg/dL","E":"About 500 mg/dL"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose first appears in the urine at the renal threshold of about 200 mg/dL, before the carriers are fully saturated. All carriers are saturated (Tm, about 375 mg/min) at a plasma glucose of about 350 mg/dL, and the region between the two is splay, caused by nephron heterogeneity and the low affinity of the Na+-glucose cotransporter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: threshold near 200 mg/dL, saturation near 350 mg/dL, and Tm near 375 mg/min frame the glucose titration curve. COMLEX: glucosuria in diabetes reflects a filtered load beyond reabsorptive capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — saturable carrier kinetics define transport maximum, threshold, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is a recognized cause of acquired Fanconi syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Lisinopril","B":"Furosemide","C":"Tenofovir","D":"Amiloride","E":"Metformin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid lists drugs such as ifosfamide, cisplatin, and tenofovir, along with lead poisoning, multiple myeloma, ischemia, and hereditary disorders such as Wilson disease, tyrosinemia, and glycogen storage disease, as causes of Fanconi syndrome, a generalized proximal tubule reabsorption defect with wasting of amino acids, glucose, HCO3-, and phosphate. Lisinopril lowers angiotensin II and can raise K+, but it does not cause generalized proximal wasting. Furosemide blocks NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, a Bartter-like rather than Fanconi-like picture. Amiloride blocks ENaC in principal cells. Metformin is not a listed cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fanconi syndrome: proximal wasting of amino acids, glucose, HCO3- (type 2 RTA), and phosphate, with hypokalemia; causes include Wilson disease, tyrosinemia, glycogen storage disease, multiple myeloma, ifosfamide, cisplatin, tenofovir, and lead. COMLEX: Glucosuria with a normal plasma glucose in a patient on tenofovir warrants checking phosphate and HCO3-.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Fanconi syndrome underlies many cases of type 2 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter is defective in Bartter syndrome?","choices":{"A":"NCC in the distal convoluted tubule","B":"ENaC in the collecting tubule","C":"SGLT2 in the proximal tubule","D":"Aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct","E":"NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bartter syndrome is a reabsorption defect of the thick ascending limb affecting the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, and it resembles chronic loop diuretic use with metabolic alkalosis, hypochloremia, hypokalemia, and hypercalciuria. NCC is affected in Gitelman syndrome and ENaC is overactive in Liddle syndrome. SGLT2 loss causes glucosuria, and aquaporin-2 defects cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; neither gives this loop-diuretic picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bartter equals loop diuretic effect, and Gitelman equals thiazide effect. COMLEX: Bartter syndrome is autosomal recessive.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the thick ascending limb NKCC2 pathway is one of the transporters altered in these syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits Gitelman syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Defective NCC that mimics chronic thiazide use","B":"Defective NKCC2 that mimics chronic loop diuretic use","C":"Overactive ENaC that mimics primary aldosteronism","D":"Defective SGLT2 that mimics chronic SGLT2 inhibitor use","E":"Defective aquaporin-2 that mimics vasopressin deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gitelman syndrome is a NaCl reabsorption defect in the distal convoluted tubule affecting the thiazide-sensitive NCC transporter, and it presents like chronic thiazide use with metabolic alkalosis, hypochloremia, hypomagnesemia, and hypokalemia. It is generally less severe than Bartter syndrome. Overactive ENaC is Liddle syndrome, which causes hypertension with hypokalemic alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gitelman equals thiazide, and it is milder than Bartter. COMLEX: look for hypomagnesemia to point to Gitelman syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the thick ascending limb NKCC2 pathway is one of the transporters altered in these syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":1,"stem":"Using Winter's formula, what Pco2 is expected in simple metabolic acidosis with a plasma HCO3- of 12 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L)?","choices":{"A":"20 mm Hg","B":"26 mm Hg","C":"32 mm Hg","D":"38 mm Hg","E":"44 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Winter's formula predicts an expected Pco2 of 1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8, plus or minus 2, so for an HCO3- of 12 mEq/L the expected Pco2 is 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26 mm Hg. A measured Pco2 above or below the expected range indicates a coexisting respiratory acidosis or alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: compare the measured Pco2 with the Winter's prediction to test whether compensation is appropriate. COMLEX: mixed disorders are found when the measured value falls outside the predicted range.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the renal rules and delta-delta ratio are applied to complete blood gas interpretation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has plasma Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), Cl- 104 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). What is the serum anion gap?","choices":{"A":"16 mEq/L","B":"24 mEq/L","C":"34 mEq/L","D":"20 mEq/L","E":"10 mEq/L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The serum anion gap equals Na+ minus the sum of Cl- and HCO3-, so 138 - (104 + 14) = 20 mEq/L, which exceeds the normal value of about 12 mEq/L and indicates unmeasured anions. The other values do not follow from the formula with the given electrolytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an elevated anion gap points to accumulation of unmeasured anions such as lactate, ketoacids, or toxic alcohol metabolites. COMLEX: calculate the gap on every metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the renal rules and delta-delta ratio are applied to complete blood gas interpretation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis has a plasma HCO3- of 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) and an anion gap of 24 mEq/L (reference range: 8–12 mEq/L). Taking 24 and 12 as the normal values, his delta ratio (delta anion gap divided by delta HCO3-) is 1.5. What does this ratio indicate?","choices":{"A":"High-anion-gap acidosis with no second metabolic disorder","B":"High-anion-gap acidosis with a coexisting metabolic alkalosis","C":"High-anion-gap acidosis with a coexisting normal-anion-gap acidosis","D":"High-anion-gap acidosis with HCO3- partly lowered by respiratory alkalosis","E":"Normal-anion-gap acidosis with a coexisting metabolic alkalosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The delta ratio compares the rise in the anion gap with the fall in HCO3-: here (24 - 12) / (24 - 16) = 12 / 8 = 1.5. A ratio of 1 to 2 means the high-anion-gap acidosis alone accounts for the fall in HCO3-. A ratio below 1 means HCO3- has fallen more than the gap has risen, so a normal-anion-gap acidosis coexists, and a ratio above 2 means HCO3- has fallen less than expected, so a metabolic alkalosis coexists. The ratio does not assess respiratory disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta ratio = delta AG / delta HCO3-; 1 to 2 is a pure high-anion-gap acidosis, below 1 adds a normal-anion-gap acidosis, and above 2 adds a metabolic alkalosis. COMLEX: calculate it in every high-anion-gap acidosis to find a hidden second disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the renal rules and delta-delta ratio are applied to complete blood gas interpretation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":1,"stem":"Which signaling sequence mediates the antidiuretic effect of ADH on principal cells?","choices":{"A":"V1 receptors raise IP3 and drive AQP2 insertion into the basolateral membrane","B":"V2 receptors raise cGMP and drive ENaC insertion into the luminal membrane","C":"V2 receptors raise cAMP and drive AQP2 insertion into the luminal membrane","D":"V2 receptors lower cAMP and drive AQP1 removal from the proximal tubule","E":"V1 receptors raise cAMP and drive urea channel removal from the medulla"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADH binds basolateral V2 receptors on principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct, increasing cAMP and triggering insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the luminal membrane, which increases water permeability. V1 receptors mediate vasoconstriction through IP3, and ADH also stimulates the UT1 urea transporter rather than removing it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: V2 to cAMP to AQP2 is the antidiuretic pathway, and V1 to IP3 is the vasoconstrictor pathway. COMLEX: desmopressin acts on V2 receptors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the corticopapillary gradient and ADH action determine urine osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has urine flow of 10 mL/min, urine osmolarity of 100 mOsm/L, and plasma osmolarity of 300 mOsm/L. What is the free-water clearance?","choices":{"A":"+6.7 mL/min","B":"+3.3 mL/min","C":"+10.0 mL/min","D":"0 mL/min","E":"-2.0 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free-water clearance equals urine flow minus osmolar clearance. Osmolar clearance is 100 x 10 / 300 = 3.3 mL/min, so free-water clearance is 10 - 3.3 = +6.7 mL/min. A positive value indicates that solute-free water is being excreted, as when ADH is absent or ineffective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: positive free-water clearance means dilute urine, and negative means concentrated urine. COMLEX: isosmotic urine gives a free-water clearance of zero.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the corticopapillary gradient and ADH action determine urine osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":1,"stem":"Which condition is associated with a negative free-water clearance?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus from pituitary ADH deficiency","B":"SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion by a lung tumor","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from collecting duct ADH resistance","D":"Primary polydipsia from suppressed ADH release","E":"Loop diuretic treatment from loss of the medullary gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In SIADH excess ADH causes hyperosmotic urine with low urine flow, so free-water clearance is negative. Central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and primary polydipsia typically produce hyposmotic urine with a positive value, and loop diuretics produce isosmotic urine with a free-water clearance near zero.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high ADH gives negative free-water clearance, and low or ineffective ADH gives positive clearance. COMLEX: use urine osmolarity to separate these states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the corticopapillary gradient and ADH action determine urine osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve carries the parasympathetic efferents that contract the detrusor muscle during voiding?","choices":{"A":"Superior hypogastric plexus","B":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion","C":"Pudendal nerve branches","D":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","E":"Genitofemoral nerve trunk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 travel in the pelvic splanchnic nerves and contract the detrusor while relaxing the internal sphincter to produce voiding. The hypogastric nerve carries sympathetic fibers that promote storage, and the pudendal nerve carries somatic fibers to the external sphincter. The inferior mesenteric ganglion is a sympathetic ganglion, not a parasympathetic pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: S2, S3, and S4 keep the bladder emptying, and sympathetic fibers promote storage. COMLEX: sacral lesions therefore impair detrusor contraction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic pathways govern storage and voiding","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has postoperative urinary retention without mechanical obstruction. Which drug would promote bladder emptying by stimulating M3 receptors on the detrusor muscle?","choices":{"A":"Bethanechol","B":"Oxybutynin","C":"Tamsulosin","D":"Mirabegron","E":"Finasteride"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bethanechol is a muscarinic agonist that contracts the detrusor muscle through M3 receptors, the same action as the parasympathetic pelvic nerve during voiding. Oxybutynin is a muscarinic antagonist that relaxes the detrusor and is used for urge incontinence. Tamsulosin is an alpha-1 blocker that relaxes smooth muscle at the bladder neck and prostate. Mirabegron is a beta-3 agonist that relaxes the detrusor. Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase and shrinks the prostate over months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bladder pharmacology: bethanechol (M3 agonist, detrusor contraction), oxybutynin (M3 antagonist), mirabegron (beta-3 agonist, detrusor relaxation), tamsulosin (alpha-1 blocker, bladder neck and prostate relaxation). COMLEX: Exclude outlet obstruction before giving a detrusor stimulant for urinary retention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 4 — parasympathetic M3 contraction of the detrusor during voiding","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic preganglionic neurons that relax the detrusor muscle and contract the internal sphincter during bladder filling originate in which spinal cord segments?","choices":{"A":"S2–S4","B":"L1–L3","C":"T1–T4","D":"T5–T9","E":"C8–T1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the sympathetic innervation of the detrusor muscle and internal sphincter originates in the lumbar spinal cord (L1–L3), reaching the bladder through the hypogastric nerve, while the parasympathetic innervation originates in the sacral cord (S2–S4). S2–S4 is the parasympathetic and somatic (pudendal) outflow. T1–T4 supplies thoracic organs such as the heart, and T5–T9 gives the preganglionic fibers to the adrenal medulla in the greater splanchnic nerve. C8–T1 is not part of the bladder's autonomic supply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bladder: sympathetic L1–L3 via the hypogastric nerve (beta-3 detrusor relaxation, alpha-1 internal sphincter contraction) for storage; parasympathetic S2–S4 via the pelvic nerves (M3 detrusor contraction) for voiding; somatic S2–S4 via the pudendal nerve to the external sphincter. COMLEX: These levels guide where to look for related somatic and viscerosomatic findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 4 — sympathetic beta-3 and alpha-1 actions during storage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":1,"stem":"Which autonomic pathway mediates penile erection?","choices":{"A":"Parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Sympathetic hypogastric nerves from T11-L2","C":"Somatic pudendal nerves from S2-S4","D":"Sympathetic fibers from the celiac ganglion","E":"Parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Erection is a parasympathetic response mediated by the pelvic splanchnic nerves from S2-S4, which release nitric oxide and cause smooth muscle relaxation. Emission is sympathetic through the hypogastric nerve, and expulsion is somatic through the pudendal nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: point, squeeze, and shoot maps parasympathetic erection, sympathetic emission, and somatic expulsion. COMLEX: S2, S3, and S4 keep the penis off the floor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH drives the male hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":1,"stem":"What is the intracellular signaling effect of nitric oxide in penile erectile tissue?","choices":{"A":"Lowered cGMP that relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle","B":"Raised intracellular Ca2+ that contracts arterial smooth muscle","C":"Raised cAMP that contracts cavernosal smooth muscle","D":"Raised cGMP that relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle","E":"Lowered cAMP that relaxes venous smooth muscle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP and activating protein kinase G, which relaxes smooth muscle in the corpora cavernosa, producing vasodilation and engorgement. Norepinephrine raising intracellular Ca2+ causes contraction and detumescence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the NO to cGMP to smooth muscle relaxation pathway is pro-erectile, and norepinephrine is anti-erectile. COMLEX: this is the pathway targeted by PDE5 inhibitors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH drives the male hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":1,"stem":"What is the mechanism of action of PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil?","choices":{"A":"Increasing nitric oxide synthase expression in the corpora","B":"Blocking alpha-1 receptors in the penile arteries","C":"Slowing the breakdown of cGMP in cavernosal smooth muscle","D":"Stimulating norepinephrine release from sympathetic nerves","E":"Slowing the breakdown of cAMP in cavernosal smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PDE5 inhibitors block phosphodiesterase type 5, preventing breakdown of cGMP, so the effect of nitric oxide is prolonged and smooth muscle relaxation is enhanced when sexual stimulation triggers NO release. They do not create the erection without stimulation. Alpha-1 blockade is a separate mechanism, the one by which drugs such as trazodone cause priapism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sildenafil raises cGMP and is contraindicated with nitrates. COMLEX: priapism lasting more than four hours is an emergency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH drives the male hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"In a research study, healthy volunteers receive an infusion of a drug that inhibits the formation of endothelial-derived nitric oxide. Blood pressure, renal hemodynamics, and urinary sodium excretion are measured during the infusion. Which renal effect is expected?","choices":{"A":"Lower renal vascular resistance, with higher GFR and Na+ excretion","B":"Higher renal vascular resistance, with higher GFR and Na+ excretion","C":"Unchanged renal resistance, since NO matters solely in hemorrhage","D":"Higher renal vascular resistance, with lower GFR and Na+ excretion","E":"Lower renal vascular resistance, with lower GFR and Na+ excretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton states that a basal level of endothelial nitric oxide production maintains vasodilation of the kidneys and normal excretion of sodium and water, so drugs that inhibit nitric oxide formation increase renal vascular resistance and decrease GFR and urinary sodium excretion, eventually raising blood pressure; Costanzo adds that nitric oxide, made from L-arginine by renal endothelial cells, dilates renal arterioles and protects against sympathetic vasoconstriction. Lower resistance with higher GFR is the effect of adding a vasodilator, not removing one. Higher resistance with higher GFR would require selective efferent constriction, which is not how loss of a general vasodilator acts. Nitric oxide acts at basal levels, not solely in hemorrhage. Lower resistance cannot follow from removing a dilator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal vasodilators: nitric oxide, PGE2 and PGI2, bradykinin, low-dose dopamine, and ANP (afferent); constrictors: norepinephrine, epinephrine, endothelin, and angiotensin II (efferent-preferential). COMLEX: Endothelial damage with impaired nitric oxide production in hypertension or atherosclerosis can add renal vasoconstriction and raise blood pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — prostaglandins are the other local vasodilator buffer of the afferent arteriole","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with vomiting and volume depletion takes ibuprofen and develops a rising creatinine. Which mechanism best explains the fall in GFR?","choices":{"A":"Lost prostaglandin afferent dilation leaves constrictors lowering capillary pressure","B":"Direct toxicity to podocyte foot processes that lowers the filtration coefficient","C":"Constriction of the efferent arteriole by prostaglandin E2 that lowers RPF","D":"Increased plasma oncotic pressure from prostaglandin inhibition that lowers GFR","E":"Obstruction of the ureter by drug crystals that raises Bowman space pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prostaglandins E2 and I2 dilate afferent arterioles and protect renal blood flow in high vasoconstrictor states, such as volume depletion with high sympathetic tone and angiotensin II. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, removing this protection, so afferent constriction lowers glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs cause prerenal azotemia in volume-depleted patients by removing afferent dilation. COMLEX: avoid NSAIDs in heart failure, cirrhosis, and volume depletion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — prostaglandins protect afferent flow and NSAIDs remove this buffer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has an obstructing ureteral stone that raises Bowman space hydrostatic pressure (PBS) from 10 to 25 mm Hg. At the afferent end of a glomerular capillary in the obstructed kidney, PGC is 45 mm Hg and pi GC is 27 mm Hg. What is the net ultrafiltration pressure at this point?","choices":{"A":"-13 mm Hg","B":"+8 mm Hg","C":"+18 mm Hg","D":"+20 mm Hg","E":"-7 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Net ultrafiltration pressure equals PGC minus PBS minus pi GC, so 45 - 25 - 27 = -7 mm Hg, meaning the net force no longer favors filtration and GFR falls to near zero. The value +8 mm Hg is the result for this capillary with PBS at its prior 10 mm Hg, and the other values result from omitting or mishandling one of the terms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: net pressure equals PGC minus PBS minus pi GC, with pi BS taken as zero. COMLEX: obstruction can abolish filtration when PBS rises enough.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — the Starling equation defines net ultrafiltration pressure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man's mean arterial pressure rises from 100 to 140 mm Hg during a hypertensive episode, yet renal blood flow stays constant. Which response best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Afferent arteriolar constriction raising renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","B":"Efferent arteriolar dilation that lowers renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","C":"Afferent arteriolar dilation that lowers renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","D":"Increased sympathetic activity that reduces renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","E":"Increased circulating ANP that lowers total renal resistance to hold flow constant"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autoregulation maintains constant renal blood flow by changing renal vascular resistance in proportion to arterial pressure. Increased pressure stretches the afferent arteriole, causing myogenic constriction, and increases NaCl delivery to the macula densa, causing tubuloglomerular feedback constriction, so resistance increases to hold flow constant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoregulation changes resistance in the same direction as pressure to keep flow constant. COMLEX: this occurs at the afferent arteriole within the autoregulatory range.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — myogenic stretch and tubuloglomerular feedback both constrict the afferent arteriole as pressure rises","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man receives an infusion of isotonic saline that raises his GFR and the NaCl delivered to the macula densa. Within seconds, the afferent arteriole constricts and GFR returns toward normal. Which mediator, released as a result of the macula densa signal, most likely constricts the afferent arteriole?","choices":{"A":"Nitric oxide released from the endothelium","B":"Renin released from juxtaglomerular cells","C":"Adenosine formed from released ATP","D":"Prostaglandin E2 from the renal medulla","E":"Bradykinin released from the distal tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton describes increased NaCl delivery activating the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of macula densa cells, which depolarizes them and releases ATP; the ATP is converted to adenosine, which constricts the afferent arterioles, lowers glomerular hydrostatic pressure, and returns GFR toward normal. Nitric oxide is a vasodilator and decreases the sensitivity of this feedback. High macula densa NaCl decreases renin release, and renin itself does not constrict the arteriole. Prostaglandin E2 dilates renal arterioles and, with nitric oxide, reduces feedback sensitivity. Bradykinin is a renal vasodilator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tubuloglomerular feedback: high macula densa NaCl → cotransporter entry, depolarization, ATP → adenosine → afferent constriction and less renin; low NaCl → afferent dilation and more renin. COMLEX: Loop diuretics block the macula densa cotransporter and blunt this feedback.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback is one of the two intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man starts a high-protein diet, and his GFR increases. Which mechanism best explains this change?","choices":{"A":"Greater macula densa NaCl delivery constricts the afferent arteriole to raise GFR","B":"Less macula densa NaCl delivery dilates the afferent arteriole through feedback","C":"Lower proximal NaCl reabsorption raises distal delivery and dilates the efferent arteriole","D":"Rising plasma protein concentration lowers capillary oncotic pressure and raises GFR","E":"Greater myogenic stretch of the afferent arteriole increases its diameter and GFR"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A high-protein diet increases proximal Na+ and Cl- reabsorption, decreasing delivery to the macula densa. Tubuloglomerular feedback responds by dilating the afferent arteriole, which raises glomerular capillary pressure and GFR. Increased plasma protein would raise oncotic pressure and reduce GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced macula densa delivery dilates the afferent arteriole and raises GFR. COMLEX: this feedback loop matches filtration to tubular reabsorptive capacity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback adjusts afferent tone from distal delivery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man with untreated type 1 diabetes has a GFR of 120 mL/min, plasma glucose of 400 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), urine glucose of 2500 mg/dL (reference range: normally undetectable), and urine flow of 4 mL/min. What is his glucose reabsorption rate?","choices":{"A":"480 mg/min","B":"100 mg/min","C":"580 mg/min","D":"380 mg/min","E":"280 mg/min"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The filtered load is GFR x plasma glucose, or 120 mL/min x 4 mg/mL = 480 mg/min. The excretion rate is urine concentration times flow, or 25 mg/mL x 4 mL/min = 100 mg/min. Reabsorption is the difference, 480 - 100 = 380 mg/min, which approximates the glucose transport maximum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reabsorption equals filtered load minus excretion, and it saturates at Tm. COMLEX: convert every concentration to consistent units before calculating.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — glucose titration curve, Tm, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman in a renal physiology study receives a freely filtered test substance. Its plasma concentration is 2 mg/mL, its urine concentration is 50 mg/mL, urine flow is 2 mL/min, and her inulin clearance is 120 mL/min. Which statement is correct?","choices":{"A":"Clearance is 50 mL/min, so net secretion occurred","B":"Clearance is 50 mL/min, so net reabsorption occurred","C":"Clearance is 120 mL/min, so it is handled like inulin","D":"Clearance is 25 mL/min, so net reabsorption occurred","E":"Clearance is 200 mL/min, so net secretion occurred"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration, or 50 x 2 / 2 = 50 mL/min. Because this is lower than the inulin clearance of 120 mL/min, the substance was filtered and then net reabsorbed. A clearance greater than inulin would indicate net secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a clearance below inulin implies net reabsorption, and above inulin implies net secretion. COMLEX: use the clearance ratio to infer tubular handling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — clearance ratios relative to inulin reveal tubular handling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has a plasma glucose of 300 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) and glucosuria, even though the average transport maximum for glucose has not been reached. Which explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"The transport maximum has been exceeded uniformly","B":"PAH is competitively inhibiting glucose reabsorption","C":"Nephron heterogeneity and low SGLT2 affinity produce splay","D":"GFR has fallen enough to lower the filtered glucose load","E":"Glucose is secreted into the tubule by the distal nephron"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glucose begins to appear in the urine at a plasma threshold of about 180 to 200 mg/dL, below the plasma level of about 350 mg/dL at which the transport maximum (about 375 mg/min) is reached. In between, glucose is excreted before all carriers are saturated, a region called splay. Splay is explained by heterogeneity among nephrons and the relatively low affinity of the Na+-glucose cotransporter. A falling GFR would lower, not raise, the filtered load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: splay lies between threshold and Tm, and threshold precedes Tm. COMLEX: this is why glucosuria occurs before maximal reabsorption is reached.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — carrier saturation kinetics and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has new hypertension, with a blood pressure of 168/98 mm Hg, and muscle weakness. She eats a large amount of real black licorice every day. Serum K+ is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) and HCO3- is 32 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L); plasma renin activity and aldosterone are both low. Which mechanism best explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"Autonomous aldosterone secretion from an adrenal adenoma","B":"Cortisol activating renal mineralocorticoid receptors","C":"Gain-of-function ENaC with impaired channel degradation","D":"Renin secretion from a juxtaglomerular cell tumor","E":"Loss of NCC function in the distal convoluted tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cortisol can activate mineralocorticoid receptors, and 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase normally protects them by converting cortisol to inactive cortisone; glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice blocks this enzyme, producing an acquired syndrome of apparent mineralocorticoid excess with hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and low aldosterone. An aldosterone-secreting adenoma would give a high aldosterone, and a renin-secreting tumor would give high renin and aldosterone. Gain-of-function ENaC (Liddle syndrome) also gives low aldosterone, but it is an inherited disorder, while her daily licorice explains her picture. Loss of NCC function (Gitelman syndrome) causes hypokalemic alkalosis with normal or low blood pressure, not hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis with low renin and low aldosterone: Liddle syndrome (ENaC gain of function, treat with amiloride) or apparent mineralocorticoid excess (11-beta-HSD deficiency or licorice; treat with K+-sparing diuretics or corticosteroids). COMLEX: Ask about licorice intake before an extensive work-up of low-renin hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — the mineralocorticoid receptor's genomic action in principal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman has muscle cramps, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypochloremia, and metabolic alkalosis, with normal blood pressure. Her urinary calcium excretion is low. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Loss of NKCC2 function in the thick ascending limb","B":"Gain of ENaC function in the collecting tubule","C":"Loss of SGLT2 function in the early proximal tubule","D":"Loss of NCC function in the distal convoluted tubule","E":"Excess aldosterone from an adrenal adenoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with hypomagnesemia, normal blood pressure, and low urinary calcium identifies Gitelman syndrome, a defect of the thiazide-sensitive NaCl cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule. Liddle syndrome and aldosteronism cause hypertension. Bartter syndrome causes hypercalciuria, whereas Gitelman syndrome causes hypocalciuria. Loss of NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb is the Bartter defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gitelman equals thiazide effect with hypomagnesemia. COMLEX: the presentation is later and milder than Bartter.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — NCC-mediated NaCl reabsorption in the early distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with low renin and low aldosterone. Spironolactone is ineffective but amiloride corrects the abnormalities. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Excess aldosterone secretion from an adrenal adenoma","B":"Loss of NCC function in the early distal tubule","C":"Loss of NKCC2 function in the thick ascending limb","D":"Generalized proximal reabsorption failure with phosphate wasting","E":"Overactive ENaC from impaired channel degradation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hypertension with hypokalemic alkalosis and suppressed renin and aldosterone that responds to amiloride, an ENaC blocker, identifies Liddle syndrome, in which a gain-of-function mutation impairs ENaC degradation and increases Na+ reabsorption in the collecting tubule. An adrenal adenoma would raise aldosterone, and the other defects cause hypotension or proximal wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liddle syndrome mimics hyperaldosteronism but with low aldosterone. COMLEX: treat with amiloride or triamterene, not spironolactone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — collecting tubule ENaC and principal cell transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes has pH 7.22 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 20 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), HCO3- 8 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), Na+ 132 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and Cl- 96 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","B":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a superimposed respiratory acidosis","C":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a superimposed respiratory alkalosis","D":"Normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","E":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a superimposed metabolic alkalosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His anion gap is 132 - (96 + 8) = 28 mEq/L, which is high. Winter's formula predicts an expected Pco2 of 1.5 x 8 + 8 = 20 mm Hg, matching the measured value, so compensation is appropriate. The delta HCO3- of 16 relative to the delta anion gap of 16 gives a ratio of 1, so there is no second metabolic disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: check the anion gap, then Winter's formula, then the delta ratio. COMLEX: DKA is the classic high-anion-gap acidosis with appropriate compensation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — Winter's formula and the delta ratio applied to a full blood gas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with persistent vomiting has pH 7.52 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 48 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 38 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic alkalosis with superimposed respiratory acidosis","B":"Metabolic alkalosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","C":"Metabolic alkalosis with superimposed respiratory alkalosis","D":"Respiratory alkalosis with appropriate renal compensation","E":"Respiratory acidosis with appropriate renal compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vomiting causes loss of gastric H+ and a rise in HCO3-, the primary disturbance, producing alkalemia. The respiratory compensation rule predicts a rise in Pco2 of about 0.6 mm Hg (Costanzo) to 0.7 mm Hg (BRS) per 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-, or about 8 to 10 mm Hg for a 14 mEq/L rise, giving about 48 to 50 mm Hg, which matches his measured 48 mm Hg. Compensation is therefore appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic alkalosis is compensated by hypoventilation, which raises Pco2. COMLEX: contraction alkalosis with hypokalemia and hypochloremia accompanies vomiting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — vomiting alkalosis is maintained by volume contraction and hypochloremia while hypoventilation compensates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old who ingested aspirin tablets has pH 7.46 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 22 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), HCO3- 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), Na+ 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and Cl- 104 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with compensation as Winter's predicts","B":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a concurrent respiratory acidosis","C":"Primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation","D":"Normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","E":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a concurrent respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The anion gap is 140 - (104 + 15) = 21 mEq/L, so there is a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a delta ratio of 1, and no second metabolic disorder. Winter's formula predicts a Pco2 of 1.5 x 15 + 8 = 30.5 mm Hg, plus or minus 2, but the measured Pco2 of 22 mm Hg is lower than predicted, indicating an additional respiratory alkalosis. Salicylates stimulate the respiratory center and also cause metabolic acidosis. The high anion gap and a delta ratio of 1 show the HCO3- fall is metabolic, not renal compensation for a primary respiratory alkalosis, and an acute ingestion leaves no time for days-long renal compensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: salicylate poisoning is the classic mixed respiratory alkalosis and high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: a Pco2 below the Winter's range signals a superimposed respiratory alkalosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — salicylate toxicity is the worked example of a mixed disorder found by checking compensation against Winter's formula","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man on chronic lithium therapy has polyuria and serum osmolarity of 310 mOsm/L. Urine osmolarity is 90 mOsm/L before and 95 mOsm/L after desmopressin. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus","B":"Primary polydipsia","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","D":"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion","E":"Osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperosmolar serum with persistently dilute urine that does not respond to desmopressin indicates that the collecting ducts are unresponsive to ADH, which is nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, a known complication of lithium. In central diabetes insipidus, desmopressin would raise urine osmolarity, and primary polydipsia would produce low serum osmolarity. SIADH gives low serum osmolarity with concentrated urine, and an osmotic diuresis is driven by unreabsorbed glucose rather than by failure to respond to ADH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: central DI responds to desmopressin, and nephrogenic DI does not. COMLEX: lithium and demeclocycline are classic causes of nephrogenic DI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — ADH-dependent water permeability of the collecting duct","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old smoker with small cell lung cancer has serum Na+ 121 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), serum osmolarity 255 mOsm/L (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/L), and urine osmolarity 550 mOsm/L (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/L), and is euvolemic. Which combination of free-water clearance and urine flow is expected?","choices":{"A":"Positive free-water clearance with high urine flow","B":"Negative free-water clearance with low urine flow","C":"Zero free-water clearance with isosmotic urine","D":"Negative free-water clearance with high urine flow","E":"Positive free-water clearance with low urine flow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion. Excess ADH increases water reabsorption in the collecting ducts, producing hyperosmotic urine with urine flow that is low relative to water intake and a negative free-water clearance, and dilutional hyponatremia with low serum osmolarity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SIADH gives hyponatremia, euvolemia, concentrated urine, and negative free-water clearance. COMLEX: small cell carcinoma is a classic ectopic ADH source.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — SIADH pathophysiology and its effect on urine concentration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with schizophrenia drinks about 10 L of water daily. Serum Na+ is 128 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), serum osmolarity is 265 mOsm/L (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/L), and urine osmolarity is 60 mOsm/L, rising to 600 mOsm/L (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/L) after water restriction. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus with low ADH and positive free-water clearance","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus with ADH resistance and positive free-water clearance","C":"SIADH with excess ADH and negative free-water clearance","D":"Primary polydipsia with suppressed ADH and positive free-water clearance","E":"Adrenal insufficiency with volume-driven ADH release and negative free-water clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Excess water intake suppresses ADH, producing dilute urine, positive free-water clearance, and low serum sodium and osmolarity. Because the kidney is normal, water restriction restores concentrating ability, which distinguishes primary polydipsia from both forms of diabetes insipidus, in which the urine stays dilute despite water restriction. A urine osmolarity of 60 is inconsistent with ADH excess.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low serum osmolarity with dilute urine points to water intake, and a response to water restriction confirms it. COMLEX: psychiatric patients are at risk for psychogenic polydipsia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — water deprivation testing and the ADH response","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man six months after a T6 spinal cord transection has involuntary bladder emptying at low volumes with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia. Which lesion pattern explains this?","choices":{"A":"Lower motor neuron injury of the S2-S4 roots","B":"Isolated pudendal injury at the pelvic floor","C":"Upper motor neuron injury above the sacral center","D":"Sympathetic chain injury below the L2 level","E":"Neuropathy of the pelvic splanchnic nerves"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After recovery from spinal shock, a lesion above the sacral micturition center leaves the sacral reflex arc intact but disconnected from pontine control, producing a spastic, hyperreflexic bladder with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia. Lower motor neuron lesions of S2-S4 produce a flaccid atonic bladder with overflow. An isolated pudendal injury affects only the external sphincter, sympathetic outflow to the bladder arises at T11-L2 and governs storage rather than reflex emptying, and pelvic splanchnic neuropathy is a lower motor neuron lesion that gives a flaccid bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UMN lesions give a spastic bladder, and LMN lesions give a flaccid bladder. COMLEX: the level of the lesion determines the pattern of voiding dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — spastic versus flaccid neurogenic bladder types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a large midline disc herniation has saddle anesthesia, a distended bladder, and dribbling incontinence. Which best describes her bladder dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Atonic bladder from a lower motor neuron lesion","B":"Spastic hyperreflexic bladder from an upper motor neuron lesion","C":"Stress incontinence from weakened pelvic floor support","D":"Urge incontinence from detrusor overactivity","E":"Detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia from a pontine lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cauda equina compression damages the S2-S4 roots, interrupting the sacral reflex arc and producing a flaccid, atonic bladder that fills without contracting, with overflow incontinence and loss of perineal sensation. Spastic bladder and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia result from upper motor neuron lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: saddle anesthesia with urinary retention points to cauda equina syndrome. COMLEX: this is a surgical emergency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — lower motor neuron lesions cause overflow incontinence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old multiparous woman leaks urine when she coughs or laughs, with no urgency, and a normal postvoid residual. Which mechanism best explains her incontinence?","choices":{"A":"Detrusor overactivity with involuntary contractions during filling","B":"Chronic outlet obstruction with an overfilled bladder","C":"Loss of the sacral reflex arc from a lower motor neuron lesion","D":"Loss of pontine inhibition from an upper motor neuron lesion","E":"Weak urethral support against abdominal pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leakage with increased intra-abdominal pressure and no urgency or retention is stress incontinence, caused by weakness of urethral sphincter or pelvic floor support so that abdominal pressure exceeds urethral closure pressure. Urge incontinence reflects detrusor overactivity, and overflow reflects outlet obstruction or an atonic bladder. A lower motor neuron lesion gives an atonic bladder with a large residual, and loss of pontine inhibition gives involuntary detrusor contractions, neither of which fits leakage only with coughing and a normal residual.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stress incontinence equals leakage with cough, urge equals detrusor overactivity, overflow equals a full bladder with dribbling. COMLEX: pelvic floor exercises are first-line therapy for stress incontinence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — clinical types of incontinence and their mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man taking isosorbide for angina takes sildenafil and develops severe hypotension. Which mechanism best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Sildenafil blocks nitrate conversion to nitric oxide and worsens angina","B":"Nitrates inhibit phosphodiesterase type 5 and lower cGMP","C":"Both drugs raise cGMP and cause additive vasodilation","D":"Sildenafil increases norepinephrine release and causes a hypertensive crisis","E":"Nitrates block alpha-1 receptors and potentiate sildenafil hypotension"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nitrates release nitric oxide and raise cGMP in vascular smooth muscle, and sildenafil blocks cGMP breakdown by PDE5, so together they cause exaggerated vasodilation and severe hypotension. This is why PDE5 inhibitors are contraindicated with nitrates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NO to cGMP to vasodilation is common to both drugs. COMLEX: ask about nitrate use before prescribing a PDE5 inhibitor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 4 — PDE5 inhibitor signaling and vascular dangers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has azoospermia 2 years after chemotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma. Serum FSH is 28 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.5–12.4 mIU/mL), LH is 5.0 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.7–8.6 mIU/mL), and testosterone is 550 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1,000 ng/dL). Which statement about normal testicular control best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"FSH stimulates Leydig testosterone synthesis, and LH supports Sertoli cell function","B":"Testosterone stimulates hypothalamic GnRH release through positive feedback","C":"Inhibin from Leydig cells suppresses pituitary LH release","D":"LH drives Leydig testosterone synthesis and FSH supports Sertoli cell function","E":"GnRH inhibits pituitary LH and FSH secretion in a pulsatile pattern"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pulsatile hypothalamic GnRH stimulates anterior pituitary LH and FSH secretion. LH stimulates Leydig cells to synthesize testosterone, and FSH supports Sertoli cells and spermatogenesis. Testosterone inhibits GnRH and LH by negative feedback, and Sertoli cell inhibin selectively inhibits FSH. Chemotherapy damaged the germinal epithelium but spared the Leydig cells, so inhibin B fell and FSH rose alone while LH and testosterone stayed normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LH acts on Leydig cells and FSH acts on Sertoli cells. COMLEX: use the axis to interpret hormone panels in hypogonadism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — dual negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with a complete T4 spinal cord transection asks about sexual function. Which type of erection is most likely to persist?","choices":{"A":"Reflex erections through the sacral parasympathetic pathway","B":"Psychogenic erections through descending cortical input","C":"Erections mediated by sympathetic celiac ganglion fibers","D":"Nocturnal erections triggered by vagal input","E":"Erections mediated by the somatic pudendal motor neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reflex erections depend on the sacral S2-S4 parasympathetic arc, which remains intact below a high spinal lesion, so tactile stimulation can still produce erection. Psychogenic erections require descending input that is interrupted by the transection, and the pudendal nerve controls expulsion, not erection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: erection is parasympathetic through S2-S4 and can be reflexive without brain input. COMLEX: level of the cord lesion predicts which components of sexual function remain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — S2-S4 autonomic and somatic pathways as applied to bladder dysfunction after cord injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl30_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman at 34 weeks' gestation has new-onset hypertension, with a blood pressure of 162/108 mm Hg, and proteinuria. Over the past week her serum creatinine has risen from 0.5 to 1.1 mg/dL. Her condition involves widespread injury to the vascular endothelium. Which vasoconstrictor peptide, released by damaged endothelial cells, may contribute to her renal vasoconstriction and fall in GFR?","choices":{"A":"Endothelin","B":"Bradykinin","C":"Urodilatin","D":"Prostacyclin","E":"Dopamine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton describes endothelin as a vasoconstrictor peptide released by damaged vascular endothelial cells; it may help limit blood loss when a vessel is severed, and its plasma levels rise in diseases with endothelial injury, such as toxemia of pregnancy, acute renal failure, and chronic uremia, where it may contribute to renal vasoconstriction and a lower GFR. Bradykinin is a vasodilator that tends to increase renal blood flow and GFR. Urodilatin is a natriuretic peptide secreted by the kidney that increases GFR. Prostacyclin (PGI2) is a vasodilating prostaglandin, not a peptide. Low-dose dopamine dilates renal arterioles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hormones that lower both GFR and RBF: norepinephrine, epinephrine, and endothelin; those that raise both: endothelial nitric oxide and prostaglandins; angiotensin II lowers RBF while protecting GFR. COMLEX: Endothelial injury in preeclampsia, acute kidney injury, or uremia releases endothelin, which can worsen renal vasoconstriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators adjust afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl30_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man follows a very high-salt diet for 2 weeks. NaCl delivery to his macula densa rises, yet his GFR is maintained rather than lowered by tubuloglomerular feedback, and he excretes the extra NaCl. Which change best explains why feedback constriction of his afferent arterioles is blunted?","choices":{"A":"Higher angiotensin II raising feedback sensitivity","B":"Lower angiotensin II reducing feedback sensitivity","C":"Loss of cotransporter expression in macula densa","D":"Sympathetic dilation of the afferent arterioles","E":"A higher plasma oncotic pressure in glomeruli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that angiotensin II enhances tubuloglomerular feedback sensitivity, whereas nitric oxide and some prostaglandins decrease it; increased NaCl intake and ECF expansion reduce renin release and angiotensin II formation, and the lower angiotensin II reduces feedback sensitivity, permitting more NaCl to reach the macula densa and distal tubule without feedback afferent constriction and a fall in GFR. Higher angiotensin II would increase feedback sensitivity, and salt loading lowers it. The macula densa cotransporter still senses NaCl; it is the sensitivity of the response that changes. Sympathetic nerves constrict rather than dilate renal arterioles. A higher plasma oncotic pressure would lower GFR, not explain its maintenance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tubuloglomerular feedback sensitivity is raised by angiotensin II and lowered by nitric oxide and some prostaglandins; a high-salt diet lowers angiotensin II, letting more NaCl pass distally without a fall in GFR. COMLEX: This adjustment helps the kidney excrete a salt load while keeping GFR stable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — the renin-angiotensin system responds to effective arterial blood volume","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl30_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes mellitus has a GFR of 100 mL/min by inulin clearance. At a plasma glucose of 500 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), he excretes glucose at 125 mg/min. Hours later his plasma glucose is 700 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) with GFR unchanged. His urinary glucose excretion rate is now closest to which value?","choices":{"A":"About 125 mg/min","B":"About 375 mg/min","C":"About 700 mg/min","D":"About 175 mg/min","E":"About 325 mg/min"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At 500 mg/dL the filtered load is 100 mL/min × 5 mg/mL = 500 mg/min and excretion is 125 mg/min, so reabsorption is 500 − 125 = 375 mg/min; because plasma glucose is far above the renal threshold of about 180–200 mg/dL, the carriers are saturated and this is his Tm, the lecture value of about 375 mg/min. At 700 mg/dL the filtered load is 100 mL/min × 7 mg/mL = 700 mg/min, reabsorption stays fixed at the Tm, and excretion becomes 700 − 375 = 325 mg/min. About 125 mg/min assumes excretion does not change although the filtered load rose by 200 mg/min. About 375 mg/min is the Tm, the reabsorption rate rather than the excretion rate. About 700 mg/min is the whole filtered load and ignores ongoing reabsorption. About 175 mg/min scales the old excretion rate in proportion to plasma glucose, which applies to filtration, not to saturated reabsorption. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 30, transport maximum, renal threshold, and splay).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Above Tm, reabsorption is constant, so every additional milligram of filtered glucose is excreted. COMLEX: Splay, glucose excretion beginning before Tm is fully reached, reflects nephron heterogeneity and the relatively low affinity of SGLT2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — glucose titration curves show filtration, reabsorption, and excretion around Tm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl30_q4","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with very low plasma renin and aldosterone. Genetic testing confirms hereditary deficiency of 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase. Besides a K+-sparing diuretic, which treatment reduces his mineralocorticoid receptor activation by suppressing his own cortisol production?","choices":{"A":"Dexamethasone","B":"Fludrocortisone","C":"Hydrochlorothiazide","D":"Furosemide","E":"Lisinopril"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In hereditary 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency, cortisol is not converted to inactive cortisone and activates renal mineralocorticoid receptors; First Aid lists K+-sparing diuretics or corticosteroids as treatment, because an exogenous corticosteroid such as dexamethasone suppresses endogenous cortisol production and so lowers receptor activation. Fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid agonist and would worsen the hypertension and hypokalemia. Hydrochlorothiazide and furosemide increase distal Na+ delivery and K+ secretion, worsening hypokalemia. Lisinopril lowers angiotensin II, but his renin and aldosterone are already suppressed, so it does not address cortisol acting on the receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apparent mineralocorticoid excess: cortisol activates mineralocorticoid receptors when 11-beta-HSD is deficient or blocked (licorice); low renin and aldosterone; treat with K+-sparing diuretics or exogenous corticosteroids. COMLEX: Low-renin hypertension with hypokalemia in a child points to a monogenic cause such as this.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — potassium-sparing diuretics block principal cell Na+ reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl30_q5","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with pulmonary edema is receiving high-dose intravenous furosemide. His serum osmolality is 290 mOsm/kg H2O (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/kg H2O), his urine osmolality is 295 mOsm/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsm/kg H2O), and his urine flow rate is 6 mL/min. Which best describes his free-water clearance?","choices":{"A":"Strongly positive with excretion of dilute urine","B":"Strongly negative with reabsorption of free water","C":"Positive from blocked ADH action on V2 receptors","D":"Near zero with urine isosmotic to plasma","E":"Negative from a preserved medullary gradient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osmolar clearance is (295 × 6)/290 = 6.1 mL/min, so free-water clearance is 6 − 6.1 = −0.1 mL/min, essentially zero. A loop diuretic blocks NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb, which both disables the diluting segment that produces free water and abolishes the corticopapillary gradient needed to reabsorb it, so the urine stays isosmotic to plasma, largely independent of ADH. Strongly positive free-water clearance requires dilute urine, as in high water intake or diabetes insipidus. Strongly negative free-water clearance requires concentrated urine, as in water deprivation or SIADH. Furosemide does not block V2 receptors, and his urine is not dilute. The medullary gradient is abolished, not preserved, by loop blockade. Sources: Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (free-water clearance and isosthenuric urine during loop diuretic treatment); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 30, free-water clearance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Free-water clearance is V minus osmolar clearance, positive with dilute urine, negative with concentrated urine, and zero with isosmotic urine. COMLEX: Loop diuretics leave patients unable to either dilute or concentrate their urine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl30_q6","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man is evaluated for infertility. Semen analysis shows no sperm, and both testes are small. His FSH is 28 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.5–12.4 mIU/mL), LH 6.0 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.7–8.6 mIU/mL), and testosterone 520 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1000 ng/dL). Loss of which feedback signal best explains his hormone pattern?","choices":{"A":"Testosterone acting on hypothalamic GnRH pulses","B":"Androgen-binding protein acting on gonadotropes","C":"Sertoli cell inhibin B acting on the pituitary","D":"Testosterone acting on pituitary gonadotropes","E":"Leydig cell inhibin B acting on the hypothalamus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Azoospermia with small testes points to failure of the seminiferous tubules, and the Sertoli cells that support spermatogenesis also secrete inhibin B, which acts directly on the anterior pituitary to suppress FSH selectively; losing it raises FSH while LH and testosterone stay normal. Testosterone feeds back on hypothalamic GnRH pulses and on pituitary LH secretion, and his testosterone and LH are normal, so neither testosterone loop has been lost. Androgen-binding protein concentrates testosterone inside the tubule and is not a pituitary feedback signal. Inhibin B comes from Sertoli cells, not Leydig cells, and acts on the pituitary rather than the hypothalamus. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 30, HPG axis negative feedback loops).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhibin B from Sertoli cells selectively suppresses FSH, so tubular damage raises FSH with a normal LH. COMLEX: An isolated rise in FSH in an infertile man suggests seminiferous tubule failure with preserved Leydig cell function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — testosterone and inhibin B form two separate negative feedback loops","isHighYield":true}],"31":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which three components make up the glomerular capillary wall filtration barrier?","choices":{"A":"Fenestrated endothelium, mesangial cells, and Bowman capsule epithelium","B":"Mesangial matrix, glomerular basement membrane, and parietal epithelial cells","C":"Fenestrated endothelium, glomerular basement membrane, and macula densa cells","D":"Fenestrated endothelium, glomerular basement membrane, and podocytes","E":"Podocytes, mesangial cells, and the tubular basement membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The glomerular capillary wall is composed of endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane, and podocytes, which together act as a size and charge barrier for the plasma filtrate. Mesangial cells and parietal epithelium are not layers of the filtration barrier, and the macula densa and tubular basement membrane lie outside it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to any of the three layers can produce proteinuria, and podocyte injury is the hallmark of primary nephrotic diseases. COMLEX: think of the barrier as size selective and charge selective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits highly selective proteinuria, as seen in minimal change disease?","choices":{"A":"Mostly albumin and other low-molecular-weight proteins","B":"Albumin together with abundant high-molecular-weight globulins","C":"Predominantly immunoglobulin light chains from plasma cells","D":"Predominantly red cell casts with mild albumin loss","E":"Predominantly tubular proteins with minimal albumin loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Selectivity refers to the ratio of low to high molecular weight proteins in the urine. A highly selective proteinuria consists mostly of albumin and transferrin, whereas poorly selective proteinuria also contains higher-molecular-weight globulins, indicating a more damaged barrier. Minimal change disease characteristically has highly selective proteinuria. Immunoglobulin light chains in the urine are the Bence-Jones overflow proteinuria of plasma cell disorders, not a selective glomerular leak.","boardPrep":"Step 1: selective proteinuria predicts steroid responsiveness, as in minimal change disease, and nonselective proteinuria predicts steroid resistance. COMLEX: FSGS and membranous nephropathy are typically nonselective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which change initiates progressive glomerulosclerosis in the remaining glomeruli after loss of nephron mass?","choices":{"A":"Deposition of subepithelial immune complexes that activate complement","B":"Autoantibodies directed against the slit diaphragm protein nephrin","C":"Hypertrophy with raised capillary pressure and hyperfiltration","D":"Fibrin thrombi that occlude the lumens of glomerular capillaries","E":"Reduced glomerular blood flow with falling intraglomerular pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After loss of renal tissue the remaining glomeruli hypertrophy, and glomerular blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure increase, causing endothelial and podocyte injury that leads to segmental and eventually global sclerosis. The other options describe membranous nephropathy, a slit diaphragm injury, thrombotic microangiopathy, and a hemodynamic change opposite to the adaptive response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adaptive FSGS follows nephron loss, obesity, or reflux nephropathy through hyperfiltration and glomerular hypertension. COMLEX: this is why ACE inhibitors slow progression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Which findings define nephrotic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Hematuria, hypertension, oliguria, and mild proteinuria","B":"Heavy proteinuria, hematuria, hypertension, and azotemia","C":"Pyuria, fever, flank pain, and white blood cell casts","D":"Heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia","E":"Hypercalcemia, polyuria, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nephrotic syndrome consists of massive proteinuria of 3.5 g or more per day, hypoalbuminemia with plasma albumin below 3 g/dL, generalized edema, and hyperlipidemia with lipiduria. Hematuria, hypertension, and reduced GFR characterize nephritic syndrome, and the other combinations describe pyelonephritis and tubular disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tetrad is proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia, all following from glomerular protein loss. COMLEX: distinguish nephrotic from nephritic on the basis of the urinary sediment and blood pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — loss of the barrier's charge and size selectivity produces the heavy proteinuria that defines this syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":1,"stem":"What best explains the generalized edema of nephrotic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure from failure of the right ventricle","B":"Obstruction of lymphatic drainage by interstitial protein deposition","C":"Elevated natriuretic peptide levels that widen the interstitial space","D":"Increased capillary permeability from circulating inflammatory mediators","E":"Low oncotic pressure with renal Na+ and water retention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heavy proteinuria lowers plasma albumin and therefore intravascular colloid osmotic pressure, causing fluid to leave the capillaries. Hypovolemia stimulates renin, aldosterone, and sympathetic activity, and primary renal sodium retention also contributes, so sodium and water retention aggravate the edema. The other mechanisms do not underlie nephrotic edema. Natriuretic peptide secretion is actually reduced in nephrotic syndrome, and these peptides promote Na+ excretion, which would oppose edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrotic edema is soft, pitting, and periorbital in children, and it is driven by low oncotic pressure plus salt retention. COMLEX: this differs from the hypertensive, hematuric edema of nephritic syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — loss of the barrier's charge and size selectivity produces the albumin loss behind this edema","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Why are patients with nephrotic syndrome prone to thromboembolic events?","choices":{"A":"Immune platelet destruction causing compensatory thrombocytosis","B":"Urinary loss of endogenous anticoagulants such as antithrombin III","C":"Urinary loss of vitamin K needed for clotting factor synthesis","D":"Overproduction of erythropoietin that raises the hematocrit","E":"Deposition of antiplatelet antibodies along the endothelium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nephrotic patients lose antithrombin III and other endogenous anticoagulants in the urine, producing a hypercoagulable state that predisposes to renal vein thrombosis, particularly in membranous nephropathy. They also lose immunoglobulins and are vulnerable to staphylococcal and pneumococcal infections. Vitamin K loss would cause bleeding rather than thrombosis, and platelet destruction or antiplatelet antibodies are not features of nephrotic syndrome. Erythropoietin overproduction is not a feature of nephrotic syndrome; the hypercoagulability comes from urinary loss of anticoagulants.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal vein thrombosis is usually a consequence, not a cause, of nephrotic syndrome. COMLEX: watch for flank pain and hematuria in a nephrotic patient as a clue to renal vein thrombosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the same barrier failure that leaks albumin also leaks low-molecular-weight anticoagulant proteins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which biopsy findings characterize minimal change disease?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse capillary wall thickening with subepithelial deposits on electron microscopy","B":"Segmental scarring of a subset of glomeruli with hyalinosis on light microscopy","C":"Normal light microscopy with diffuse foot process effacement on electron microscopy","D":"Crescents filling Bowman space with linear IgG on immunofluorescence","E":"Subendothelial deposits with wire-loop lesions on light microscopy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Minimal change disease shows glomeruli that appear normal by light microscopy, with diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes and loss of slit diaphragms on electron microscopy, and no electron-dense deposits. Membranous nephropathy shows subepithelial deposits, FSGS shows segmental sclerosis, anti-GBM disease shows crescents with linear IgG, and lupus class IV shows wire loops.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal light microscopy with effacement of foot processes on EM is the classic description of MCD. COMLEX: lipoid nephrosis refers to the lipid-laden proximal tubule cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — podocyte slit diaphragm and basement membrane integrity determine which barrier layer is injured","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding is characteristic of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Sclerosis of part of the tuft in a subset of glomeruli","B":"Uniform thickening of the capillary walls without hypercellularity","C":"Normal glomeruli with fine granular IgG over the podocytes","D":"Crescent formation in most glomeruli with linear IgG","E":"Mesangial IgA deposits with intermittent gross hematuria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"FSGS is characterized by sclerosis of a portion of the capillary tuft, segmental, in a subset of glomeruli, focal, with collapse of capillary loops, matrix deposition, and hyalinosis. It is a leading cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults, particularly in Black and Hispanic patients, and responds poorly to corticosteroids. Uniform wall thickening, granular or linear IgG with crescents, and mesangial IgA describe membranous nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, and IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: focal means some glomeruli and segmental means part of the tuft. COMLEX: biopsy sampling can miss FSGS because only a minority of glomeruli are affected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — podocyte slit diaphragm and basement membrane integrity determine which barrier layer is injured","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which antigen is the most frequent autoantibody target in primary membranous nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Nephrin of the slit diaphragm","B":"Apolipoprotein L1 in podocytes","C":"Alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","D":"M-type phospholipase A2 receptor","E":"Galactose-deficient IgA1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoantibodies to the M-type phospholipase A2 receptor on the surface of podocytes underlie about 60 percent of membranous nephropathy (Robbins; the course notes give about 70% of primary cases), leading to complement activation and subepithelial immune deposits. Nephrin defects cause congenital nephrotic syndrome, type IV collagen is involved in Alport syndrome, APOL1 variants confer FSGS risk, and galactose-deficient IgA1 underlies IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-PLA2R antibodies are a diagnostic and prognostic marker of primary membranous nephropathy. COMLEX: membranous nephropathy is a leading primary cause of nephrotic syndrome in older and white adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — podocyte slit diaphragm and basement membrane integrity determine which barrier layer is injured","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy with new-onset nephrotic syndrome has urine protein made up of albumin and transferrin with little IgG. Which mechanism best explains this selective albumin loss?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the fenestrated endothelium allows any plasma protein through","B":"Hypertrophy of podocyte foot processes widens the filtration slits","C":"Lost anionic charge removes repulsion of negatively charged albumin","D":"Increased Bowman space pressure forces albumin across the barrier","E":"Reduced tubular reabsorption allows more filtered albumin to escape"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anionic glycoproteins line the filtration barrier and repel negatively charged plasma proteins such as albumin. When these charges are lost, as in glomerular disease, albumin passes into the urine, producing proteinuria. Albumin loss is mainly a barrier defect, not a change in Bowman space pressure or tubular reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the charge barrier explains why albumin, an anionic protein, is lost early. COMLEX: in minimal change disease, anti-nephrin injury to the slit diaphragm is the leading current explanation, and loss of glomerular polyanions may contribute.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and proteinuria starts lisinopril, and over the next year his proteinuria falls and his GFR decline slows. Which mechanism best explains this benefit?","choices":{"A":"They lower intraglomerular pressure and blunt angiotensin-driven fibrosis","B":"They raise glomerular capillary pressure to increase filtration","C":"They remove circulating anti-nephrin antibodies from the plasma","D":"They replace lost podocytes through stimulation of progenitor cells","E":"They increase efferent arteriolar tone to maintain hyperfiltration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Angiotensin II constricts efferent arterioles, raising intraglomerular pressure, and has direct profibrotic effects. Inhibiting the renin-angiotensin system dilates the efferent arteriole, reduces glomerular hypertension, and blunts these effects, which ameliorates the progression of sclerosis in animal and human studies. ACE inhibitors are not an antibody-removing therapy and do not regenerate podocytes; their benefit is lowering intraglomerular pressure and blunting angiotensin's direct effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs are renoprotective in proteinuric and diabetic kidney disease. COMLEX: monitor potassium and creatinine after starting therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with primary FSGS has biopsy evidence of segmental sclerosis with hyaline deposits. Which initial event best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Endothelial proliferation that occludes the capillary lumen","B":"Neutrophil infiltration of the mesangium with capillary wall rupture","C":"Subendothelial immune deposits with basement membrane duplication","D":"Fibrin deposition in afferent arterioles that blocks inflow","E":"Podocyte loss that exposes denuded segments of glomerular basement membrane"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In primary FSGS the podocytopathy leads to podocyte loss that exceeds the ability of surviving podocytes to compensate. Denuded areas of basement membrane become hyperpermeable, plasma proteins are entrapped causing hyalinosis, and the capillary collapses with matrix accumulation, producing segmental sclerosis. Fibrinoid necrosis of afferent arterioles is a lesion of malignant hypertension, not the podocyte injury of FSGS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS is a primary podocytopathy with progressive podocyte loss. COMLEX: it lies on the same spectrum as minimal change disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has periorbital edema, urine protein of 4 g/day, serum albumin of 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and hypercholesterolemia after a respiratory infection. Which sequence best explains his edema?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac failure raises venous hydrostatic pressure and drives fluid into tissues","B":"Inflammatory mediators increase capillary permeability and let albumin escape into interstitium","C":"Albumin loss lowers plasma oncotic pressure and the kidney retains Na+ and water","D":"Lymphatic obstruction traps protein in tissues and pulls water into the interstitium","E":"Excess natriuretic peptide release widens the interstitial compartment through vasodilation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Massive proteinuria depletes serum albumin faster than the liver can replace it, reducing colloid osmotic pressure so fluid leaves the vessels. The resulting hypovolemia raises renin, aldosterone, and sympathetic tone, and primary renal sodium retention also contributes, so sodium and water retention worsen the edema, which is soft and pitting and most marked periorbitally. Natriuretic peptide secretion is reduced in nephrotic syndrome, and these peptides promote Na+ excretion rather than edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a child with nephrotic syndrome after an infection most often has minimal change disease. COMLEX: periorbital swelling in the morning is a classic presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — RAAS and sympathetic Na+ retention when effective arterial blood volume falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with advanced CKD has serum phosphate 7.2 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), calcium 7.8 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), and a markedly elevated PTH. Which sequence best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Rising GFR clears phosphate and low PTH raises calcium reabsorption from bone","B":"High calcitriol increases gut Ca2+ absorption and suppresses PTH secretion","C":"Excess dietary calcium lowers phosphate and raises the PTH set point","D":"GFR decline retains phosphate while low calcitriol lowers Ca2+ absorption","E":"Primary parathyroid adenoma raises calcium and lowers phosphate excretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As GFR falls the kidney retains phosphate, and renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity declines so calcitriol falls. Hyperphosphatemia and low calcitriol both lower serum calcium, and the hypocalcemia stimulates PTH secretion, producing secondary hyperparathyroidism. A primary adenoma would cause hypercalcemia, not hypocalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKD produces high phosphate, low calcium, low calcitriol, and high PTH. COMLEX: phosphate binders and vitamin D analogs treat this pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — PTH inhibits proximal Na-phosphate cotransport, the phosphaturic response that fails as GFR falls in CKD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with long-standing CKD has bone pain and fractures. Alkaline phosphatase and PTH are high, and calcium is low. Which process best explains her bone disease?","choices":{"A":"Primary hyperparathyroidism from an adenoma with hypercalcemia and high turnover","B":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism with high turnover and poor mineralization","C":"Excess calcitriol causing bone resorption with hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria","D":"Adynamic bone from suppressed osteoblast activity despite high circulating PTH","E":"Autoimmune destruction of osteoclasts causing dense sclerotic bone with low PTH"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal osteodystrophy results from secondary hyperparathyroidism, which increases bone resorption, together with calcitriol deficiency and phosphate retention that impair mineralization of osteoid, producing osteomalacia features. High alkaline phosphatase reflects increased osteoblastic turnover, and primary hyperparathyroidism would raise calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal osteodystrophy combines osteitis fibrosa from high PTH and osteomalacia from low calcitriol. COMLEX: this explains bone pain and fractures in dialysis patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — calcium and phosphate handling and PTH action in bone and kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl develops edema after a viral illness. Urine protein is 4+, albumin is 1.9 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), blood pressure is unremarkable, and there is no hematuria. Which biopsy and treatment response are most likely?","choices":{"A":"Normal-appearing glomeruli with foot process effacement and rapid steroid response","B":"Segmental sclerosis on light microscopy with a poor response to steroids","C":"Subepithelial deposits with silver-stain spikes and a variable steroid response","D":"Crescents and linear IgG deposits with rapidly declining renal function","E":"Diffuse mesangial IgA with episodic gross hematuria and preserved GFR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is minimal change disease, the most frequent cause of childhood nephrotic syndrome, with normal light microscopy, diffuse podocyte foot process effacement, and no deposits. Proteinuria is highly selective, renal function is stable, and more than 90 percent of children respond rapidly to corticosteroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pediatric nephrotic syndrome with normal blood pressure and no hematuria is minimal change disease until proven otherwise. COMLEX: empiric steroids are given without biopsy in typical cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — podocyte foot processes and slit diaphragms are the barrier layer effaced in minimal change disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old African American man with hypertension and HIV infection has nephrotic-range proteinuria and progressive azotemia. Biopsy shows collapse of the glomerular tuft with tubular microcysts. Which risk factor is most strongly associated?","choices":{"A":"Mutations in the COL4A5 gene","B":"High-risk APOL1 G1 or G2 alleles","C":"Anti-PLA2R autoantibodies","D":"Aberrant O-glycosylation of IgA1","E":"Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collapsing glomerulopathy, seen in HIV-associated nephropathy, is a severe FSGS variant, and high-risk APOL1 G1 and G2 alleles, common in people of African descent, greatly increase its risk. The other options describe Alport syndrome, membranous nephropathy, IgA nephropathy, and anti-GBM disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: APOL1 risk alleles confer trypanosome resistance and increase risk of FSGS and CKD in people of African descent. COMLEX: HIVAN and collapsing FSGS have a poor prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — podocyte injury leads to segmental sclerosis and protein entrapment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with a lung mass has nephrotic syndrome. Biopsy shows granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls, electron microscopy shows subepithelial deposits, and silver stain shows spikes between them. Which diagnosis and next step are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Minimal change disease with immediate high-dose corticosteroids","B":"Primary FSGS with evaluation for an inherited slit diaphragm defect","C":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis with antibiotic therapy","D":"Membranous nephropathy with a search for an underlying cancer","E":"IgA nephropathy with evaluation for a recent pharyngeal infection"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial deposits and basement membrane spikes are the hallmarks of membranous nephropathy. Because as many as 5 to 10 percent of adult cases are associated with malignancy, particularly lung and colon carcinoma, secondary causes must be excluded first, since treating the underlying cause can reverse the injury. Minimal change disease shows no deposits on electron microscopy, FSGS shows segmental sclerosis and hyalinosis rather than diffuse subepithelial deposits, and poststreptococcal disease is nephritic, with subepithelial humps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: membranous nephropathy is a leading primary cause of nephrotic syndrome in older and white adults, and it responds poorly to steroids. COMLEX: screen for cancer, drugs, infections, and lupus before calling it primary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — subepithelial deposits injure the podocyte layer of the barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl31_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with a body mass index of 47 kg/m2 has proteinuria of 2.6 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h), serum albumin of 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and no hematuria. Biopsy shows enlarged tufts, a minority with sclerosis and hyalinosis of part of the tuft, IgM and C3 confined to the scars, and no electron-dense deposits. Which change initiates this injury?","choices":{"A":"Reduced glomerular blood flow from sustained afferent arteriolar vasospasm","B":"Subepithelial immune complexes that fix complement in capillary walls","C":"Glomerular hypertension and hyperfiltration in hypertrophied glomeruli","D":"Afferent arteriolar thrombi that cause patchy cortical ischemia","E":"Mesangial IgA immune complexes that activate resident mesangial cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Obesity creates a relative insufficiency of nephron mass compared with body mass, and the glomeruli respond with hypertrophy accompanied by increased glomerular blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure (glomerular hypertension). This injures endothelial cells and podocytes; podocyte loss leaves denuded basement membrane, proteins accumulate, and segmental and eventually global sclerosis follows, matching the enlarged glomeruli with focal segmental scars. Reduced glomerular blood flow is the opposite of the adaptive hyperfiltration that drives this lesion. Subepithelial immune complexes would give granular IgG with electron-dense deposits, both absent. Afferent arteriolar thrombi are not part of the adaptive sequence, and none are described. Mesangial IgA complexes would show dominant IgA, whereas IgM and C3 limited to sclerotic segments is the FSGS pattern. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (adaptive FSGS, glomerular hypertension, obesity-related FSGS); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, single-nephron capillary hypertension and podocyte shear-stress injury).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adaptive FSGS follows any relative shortage of nephrons, from nephrectomy, unilateral renal agenesis, reflux nephropathy, or obesity. COMLEX: renin-angiotensin system inhibitors slow the sclerosis because they reduce intraglomerular hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — the same hyperfiltration and podocyte-loss sequence drives the common pathway of chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl31_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has 10 days of generalized edema, a normal blood pressure, 4+ proteinuria that is mostly albumin, and serum albumin of 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Biopsy shows normal glomeruli by light microscopy, fine granular podocyte IgG colocalizing with clustered nephrin, and flattened, retracted foot processes over a normal basement membrane. Disruption of which structure most directly causes her proteinuria?","choices":{"A":"The fenestrae of the capillary endothelium","B":"The capsular basement membrane lining Bowman capsule","C":"The mesangial matrix supporting the tuft","D":"The slit diaphragm across each filtration slit","E":"The parietal epithelium lining Bowman space"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normal glomeruli by light microscopy with diffuse foot process effacement, no deposits, and fine podocyte IgG colocalizing with nephrin indicate a podocytopathy in which circulating anti-nephrin antibodies interfere with nephrin crosslinking. Nephrin dimerizes across the filtration slit, so this attack causes rapid breakdown of the slit diaphragm, the size-selective distal barrier, producing massive, highly selective proteinuria. The endothelial fenestrae are not the site of antibody binding, which localizes to the podocyte. The capsular basement membrane underlies the parietal epithelium of Bowman capsule, outside the glomerular capillary wall filter. Mesangial matrix supports the tuft but is not one of the three layers of the capillary wall. The parietal epithelium lines the urinary space and is likewise outside the filtration barrier. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (structure of the glomerulus, slit diaphragm proteins, minimal change disease); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, structural barrier disruption).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrin links through podocin and CD2AP to the podocyte actin cytoskeleton, so attack on it produces heavy proteinuria with unremarkable light microscopy. COMLEX: highly selective proteinuria in a young child with normal blood pressure predicts a rapid corticosteroid response.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the slit diaphragm is the outermost, size-selective layer of the filtration barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl31_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has 2 months of leg and periorbital swelling. Urine protein is 6.8 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h), serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and total cholesterol is 362 mg/dL (reference range: <200 mg/dL). The urine sediment contains refractile oval bodies whose droplets take up a lipid stain. Which process best explains these urinary bodies?","choices":{"A":"Precipitation of uric acid crystals in acid urine","B":"Release of marrow fat globules from an occult fracture","C":"Leakage of chylomicrons from obstructed renal lymphatics","D":"Secretion of lipid vacuoles by injured podocytes","E":"Shed tubular cells laden with resorbed lipoprotein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In nephrotic syndrome, hepatic lipoprotein synthesis rises and lipid catabolism falls, and lipiduria follows the hyperlipidemia because lipoproteins also leak across the damaged glomerular capillary wall. Tubular epithelial cells resorb this filtered lipoprotein and are then shed with other injured tubular cells, appearing in the sediment as oval fat bodies. Uric acid crystals are not lipid droplets and would not take up a lipid stain. There is no trauma to suggest marrow fat release. Obstructed lymphatics are not part of the nephrotic mechanism, which begins with lipoprotein leakage across the filter. Podocytes lose foot process architecture in nephrotic disease but do not secrete lipid into the urine. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (nephrotic syndrome, hyperlipidemia and lipiduria); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, the nephrotic clinical tetrad).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the nephrotic tetrad is proteinuria of 3.5 g/day or more, albumin below 3 g/dL, generalized edema, and hyperlipidemia with lipiduria. COMLEX: nephrotic patients also lose immunoglobulins and antithrombin III, raising risks of infection and thrombosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — lipoproteins cross the same barrier whose size and charge selectivity normally exclude them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl31_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has a calcium of 7.9 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), phosphate of 6.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), and PTH of 290 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). Her 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 41 ng/mL (reference range: 30–100 ng/mL), but 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is 11 pg/mL (reference range: 18–72 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains her low active vitamin D?","choices":{"A":"High FGF23 with loss of renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase","B":"High PTH inhibiting proximal tubule hydroxylation","C":"Failed hepatic 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D","D":"Low sunlight synthesis of cholecalciferol in skin","E":"Hypercalcemic suppression of renal vitamin D activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Proximal tubule cells convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol through 1-alpha-hydroxylase. In chronic kidney disease, destruction of renal parenchyma and high circulating FGF23 impair this enzyme, so calcitriol falls; low calcitriol reduces intestinal calcium absorption, and together with phosphate retention and hypocalcemia it drives secondary hyperparathyroidism. PTH stimulates rather than inhibits 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so her high PTH would favor calcitriol synthesis. Her normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D shows that hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact. Low sunlight exposure would lower the 25-hydroxyvitamin D precursor, which is normal. She is hypocalcemic, not hypercalcemic. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, uremic osteodystrophy bone remodeling loop); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 9 (PTH and renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the mineral disorder of chronic kidney disease is high phosphate, low calcium, low calcitriol, and high PTH, leading to renal osteodystrophy. COMLEX: FGF23 keeps phosphate normal in early chronic kidney disease, so hyperphosphatemia appears late.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 2 — PTH acts on the same proximal tubule that both reabsorbs phosphate and activates vitamin D","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl31_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has urine protein of 8.2 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h), serum albumin of 2.2 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and a bland sediment. Biopsy shows uniform capillary wall thickening without hypercellularity, silver-stained spikes, granular IgG and C3 along capillary loops, and subepithelial deposits. A positive result for which serum test most supports a primary form of this disease?","choices":{"A":"High-titer antinuclear antibodies in serum","B":"Circulating antibodies to the podocyte PLA2 receptor","C":"Circulating antibodies to type IV collagen","D":"Elevated antistreptococcal antibody titers","E":"Antineutrophil antibodies against myeloperoxidase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uniform capillary wall thickening without hypercellularity, silver-staining spikes, granular IgG and C3, and subepithelial deposits define membranous nephropathy, and most primary cases are caused by autoantibodies to the M-type phospholipase A2 receptor on the basal surface of podocytes, the antigen in about 70% of primary cases (course notes; Robbins gives about 60% of all membranous nephropathy). A high-titer antinuclear antibody would instead raise concern for SLE, a recognized secondary cause of the membranous pattern. Antibodies to type IV collagen produce linear rather than granular IgG and crescentic disease. Elevated antistreptococcal titers accompany postinfectious GN, with hypercellular glomeruli and a nephritic picture. Antimyeloperoxidase antibodies mark pauci-immune crescentic GN with little or no immune deposition. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (membranous nephropathy, PLA2R, and secondary causes); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, membranous nephropathy and PLA2R autoantibodies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: granular capillary-loop IgG with subepithelial deposits and silver-stain spikes is the membranous pattern. COMLEX: malignancy is present in as many as 5% to 10% of adults with this pattern, so carcinoma, drugs, infection, and SLE must be excluded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 3 — class V lupus nephritis reproduces this subepithelial membranous pattern as a secondary cause","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl31_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":3,"stem":"A 32-year-old man of West African ancestry has 2 months of leg swelling and urine protein of 6.1 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h) that does not respond to 12 weeks of prednisone. Biopsy shows segmental sclerosis and hyalinosis in a subset of glomeruli, IgM and C3 confined to the scars, and diffuse foot process effacement. Which genetic finding most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"An X-linked COL4A5 collagen variant","B":"A heterozygous COL4A4 collagen variant","C":"Two high-risk APOL1 variant alleles","D":"A dominant PKD1 polycystin variant","E":"A recessive PKHD1 fibrocystin variant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Segmental sclerosis and hyalinosis in a subset of glomeruli, IgM and C3 trapped in the scars, diffuse foot process effacement, and steroid resistance define FSGS, and in individuals of African descent FSGS is strongly associated with the high-risk G1/G2 variants of APOL1, which protect against trypanosomal infection but promote podocyte death. COL4A5 variants cause X-linked Alport syndrome, with hematuria, deafness, and a laminated basement membrane. A heterozygous COL4A4 variant causes thin basement membrane disease with benign hematuria. PKD1 variants cause autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, and PKHD1 variants cause the recessive form; both enlarge the kidneys with cysts rather than causing a podocytopathy. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, FSGS genetic factors and APOL1; SDL 33, collagen IV variants; cystic renal disease comparison).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS shows segmental sclerosis with IgM and C3 in scars and responds poorly to corticosteroids; APOL1 G1/G2 risk variants explain much of its excess in people of African descent. COMLEX: At least 50% of patients with FSGS reach end-stage kidney disease within 10 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 2 — inherited collagen IV defects alter the basement membrane, whereas APOL1 risk variants drive podocyte death","isHighYield":true}],"32":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":1,"stem":"Which findings define nephritic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia","B":"Pyuria, fever, flank pain, and white blood cell casts","C":"Polyuria, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypotension","D":"Hematuria, hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and fever","E":"Hematuria with casts, oliguria, azotemia, and hypertension"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nephritic syndrome results from inflammatory glomerular injury that damages capillary walls, letting blood into the urine, and lowers GFR, causing oliguria, fluid retention, and azotemia. Hypertension results from fluid retention and renin release from ischemic kidneys. The other combinations describe nephrotic syndrome, pyelonephritis, a tubular disorder, and a thrombotic microangiopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephritic means hematuria, red cell casts, azotemia, oliguria, and hypertension, and the prototype is poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: compare with nephrotic syndrome, which is dominated by protein loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — the same filtration barrier that leaks protein in nephrotic disease is inflamed and ruptured here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern characterizes acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","B":"Coarsely granular deposits of IgG and C3","C":"Dominant mesangial IgA deposits with C3","D":"Little or no immune deposition on staining","E":"Full-house granular IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Poststreptococcal GN is an immune complex disease, and immunofluorescence shows discrete coarsely granular deposits of IgG and C3 in a starry-sky pattern along the basement membrane and mesangium. Linear IgG is seen in anti-GBM disease, mesangial IgA in IgA nephropathy, and absent staining in pauci-immune GN. Full-house staining points to lupus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: granular means immune complexes, linear means anti-GBM, and pauci-immune means none. COMLEX: C3 is low because complement is consumed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — the same filtration barrier that leaks protein in nephrotic disease is inflamed and ruptured here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":1,"stem":"Which ultrastructural finding is characteristic of acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Subendothelial deposits forming wire loops","B":"Basket-weave splitting of the basement membrane","C":"Diffuse foot process effacement without deposits","D":"Discrete subepithelial electron-dense humps","E":"Tram-track duplication of the basement membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Discrete amorphous electron-dense deposits on the epithelial side of the basement membrane, appearing as humps, are the characteristic electron microscopic finding of poststreptococcal GN. Wire loops indicate lupus nephritis, basket-weave splitting suggests Alport syndrome, effacement without deposits suggests minimal change disease, and tram-tracks suggest membranoproliferative disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: subepithelial humps are the classic EM description of poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: hypercellular glomeruli with neutrophils appear on light microscopy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — the same filtration barrier that leaks protein in nephrotic disease is inflamed and ruptured here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Crescents in rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis are formed predominantly by proliferation of which cells?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial cells within the glomerular tuft","B":"Epithelial cells lining Bowman capsule","C":"Endothelial cells of the glomerular capillaries","D":"Granular cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus","E":"Proximal tubule epithelial cells of the cortex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crescents are produced predominantly by proliferation of the epithelial cells lining Bowman capsule, together with monocytes and macrophages that have migrated into the urinary space. Fibrin strands are frequently prominent, and the crescents may obliterate the space and compress the tuft.","boardPrep":"Step 1: crescent means proliferating parietal epithelium and macrophages in Bowman space. COMLEX: crescents in most glomeruli define crescentic, or rapidly progressive, glomerulonephritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — rupture of the filtration barrier lets fibrin and proliferating epithelium fill Bowman space","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern defines type I rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Granular IgG and complement deposits in capillary walls","B":"Linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane","C":"Dominant mesangial IgA with variable IgG and C3","D":"Little or no deposition of immune reactants","E":"Dominant C3 with ill-defined electron-dense deposits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type I RPGN is anti-GBM antibody-mediated disease, marked by smooth linear deposits of IgG, and often C3, along the basement membrane. Type II shows granular deposits, type III is pauci-immune with little or no deposition, and C3 glomerulonephritis shows C3-dominant staining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type I is anti-GBM with linear IgG, type II is immune complex with granular deposits, and type III is pauci-immune. COMLEX: plasmapheresis is standard therapy for anti-GBM disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 2 — the collagen IV antigen targeted in anti-GBM disease is also mutated in Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which autoantibody is characteristic of type III, pauci-immune, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Antibody to neutrophil cytoplasmic PR3 or MPO","B":"Antibody to the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV","C":"Antibody to the M-type PLA2 receptor","D":"Antibody to double-stranded DNA","E":"Antibody to streptococcal exotoxin B"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most patients with pauci-immune crescentic GN have circulating ANCA, either cytoplasmic PR3-ANCA or perinuclear MPO-ANCA. Anti-GBM antibodies target the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV, anti-PLA2R defines membranous nephropathy, and anti-dsDNA is a lupus marker.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PR3-ANCA is c-ANCA and typical of granulomatosis with polyangiitis, while MPO-ANCA is p-ANCA and typical of microscopic polyangiitis. COMLEX: B-cell targeted therapy is important in treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — pauci-immune ANCA injury ruptures the glomerular capillary wall without immune deposits, unlike anti-GBM and immune-complex RPGN","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic class of lupus nephritis is the most common and most often associated with renal failure?","choices":{"A":"Minimal mesangial lupus nephritis","B":"Mesangial proliferative lupus nephritis","C":"Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","D":"Focal proliferative lupus nephritis","E":"Membranous lupus nephritis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Class IV, diffuse lupus nephritis, is the most common and severe form and involves more than 50 percent of glomeruli with proliferation of endothelial, mesangial, and epithelial cells, and crescents. Classes I, II, III, and V are less severe or have distinct patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class IV is diffuse, most common, and most severe. COMLEX: patients typically have hematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, and renal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — immune deposits at different layers of the filtration barrier define the lupus nephritis classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":1,"stem":"What do wire loop lesions in lupus nephritis represent?","choices":{"A":"Subendothelial deposits thickening the capillary wall","B":"Subepithelial immune complex deposits with basement membrane spikes","C":"Mesangial IgA deposits with mesangial expansion","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis of afferent arterioles with luminal thrombi","E":"Linear IgG deposits with crescents in Bowman space"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Wire loops are circumferential thickening of the capillary wall caused by subendothelial immune complex deposits, seen on light microscopy in class IV lupus nephritis and confirmed by electron microscopy as dense subendothelial deposits. Subepithelial deposits with spikes describe membranous disease. Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles with thrombi is the vascular lesion of malignant hypertension, not a capillary wall deposit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: wire loops are most typical of class IV lupus nephritis and reflect subendothelial deposits. COMLEX: immunofluorescence shows granular IgG with C3 and C1q.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — subendothelial immune deposits injure the endothelial layer of the filtration barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature characterizes class V lupus nephritis?","choices":{"A":"Involvement of fewer than half of glomeruli with segmental necrosis","B":"Mesangial cell proliferation without capillary involvement","C":"Global sclerosis of most glomeruli with fibrosis","D":"Diffuse capillary wall thickening from subepithelial immune complexes","E":"Immune complex deposition seen by immunofluorescence without any light changes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Class V, membranous lupus nephritis, features diffuse thickening of the capillary walls due to subepithelial immune complexes, similar to idiopathic membranous nephropathy, and typically presents with severe proteinuria or nephrotic syndrome. Focal necrosis defines class III, mesangial proliferation class II, and global sclerosis class VI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class V equals membranous pattern and nephrotic syndrome. COMLEX: it can coexist with class III or IV lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — class V lupus nephritis mimics primary membranous nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has cola-colored urine and periorbital edema two weeks after a sore throat. Blood pressure is 145/92 mm Hg, and urinalysis shows red cell casts. Which serologic pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Normal C3 with a positive ANCA test","B":"Low C3 with high antistreptococcal titers","C":"Low C3 with positive anti-GBM antibodies","D":"Normal C3 with an elevated serum IgA level","E":"Elevated C3 with positive cryoglobulin studies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, an immune complex disease that follows pharyngitis after a latent period compatible with antibody production. Complement is consumed, so serum C3 falls, and titers of antistreptococcal antibodies are elevated. ANCA, anti-GBM antibodies, and cryoglobulins point to other glomerular diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low C3 with recent strep infection and a nephritic picture is poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: the low C3 reflects complement consumption by the deposited immune complexes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — glomerular capillary wall injury and altered filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy develops cola-colored urine and periorbital edema two weeks after streptococcal pharyngitis. Biopsies in this disease show subendothelial deposits early that later appear on the subepithelial side of the basement membrane. Which process is proposed to explain this shift?","choices":{"A":"Podocytes phagocytose subendothelial complexes and secrete them subepithelially","B":"Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV redirect deposits across the membrane","C":"Complexes dissociate and reassemble on the outer epithelial face of the membrane","D":"Neutrophils carry the deposits across the basement membrane into the urinary space","E":"Complement lysis of the basement membrane creates subepithelial spaces for deposits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antigens are initially planted in subendothelial locations, where they elicit an inflammatory response. Later, through poorly understood mechanisms, antigen-antibody complexes dissociate, migrate across the GBM, and re-form on the subepithelial side, producing the characteristic humps. The other options do not explain the subendothelial-to-subepithelial shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: early subendothelial deposits give way to subepithelial humps that later resolve. COMLEX: the exotoxin SpeB is a candidate nephritogenic antigen localized to humps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — the endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte layers define subendothelial versus subepithelial deposit sites","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":2,"stem":"A pediatrician counsels the parents of a 6-year-old with poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Which statement about prognosis is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Most children progress to end-stage kidney disease within five years despite therapy","B":"Most children need long-term plasmapheresis to remove circulating antibodies","C":"Most children develop steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome after recovery","D":"Most children regain renal function with conservative supportive therapy","E":"Most children develop crescentic disease with rapid loss of renal function"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In children, more than 95 percent recover renal function with conservative therapy aimed at maintaining sodium and water balance. A small minority, perhaps less than 1 percent, develop a rapidly progressive form, and the disease is less benign in adults, of whom only about 60 percent of sporadic cases recover promptly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pediatric poststreptococcal GN is usually self-limited. COMLEX: treatment is supportive, with salt restriction and antihypertensives.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — maladaptive progressive glomerular injury contrasts with the self-limited course of acute nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old smoker has hemoptysis, hematuria, and a rapidly rising creatinine. Renal biopsy shows crescents with linear IgG along the basement membrane. Which mechanism and treatment are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Anti-alpha-3 collagen IV antibodies treated with plasmapheresis and immunosuppression","B":"PR3-ANCA vasculitis treated with B-cell targeted therapy and no plasmapheresis","C":"Streptococcal antigen complexes treated with antibiotics and salt restriction","D":"Deposition of galactose-deficient IgA1 treated with dietary sodium restriction","E":"Complement-mediated alternative pathway injury treated with plasmapheresis without immunosuppression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pulmonary hemorrhage with renal failure and linear IgG is Goodpasture syndrome, in which anti-GBM antibodies cross-react with the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV in the alveolar basement membrane. Plasmapheresis removes the pathogenic antibodies, and therapy to suppress the underlying immune response is added. Streptococcal immune complexes give granular deposits with subepithelial humps, and IgA deposits sit in the mesangium; neither gives linear IgG.","boardPrep":"Step 1: linear IgG with lung and kidney involvement is Goodpasture syndrome. COMLEX: smoking and hydrocarbon exposure can trigger alveolar exposure of the antigen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV also underlies Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic sinusitis, hemoptysis, and a rapidly rising creatinine has a positive c-ANCA. Biopsy shows segmental fibrinoid necrosis with crescents and no immune deposits. Which type of glomerulonephritis is this?","choices":{"A":"Anti-GBM crescentic glomerulonephritis with linear IgG","B":"Immune complex crescentic glomerulonephritis with granular IgG","C":"C3 glomerulonephritis with alternative pathway dysregulation","D":"Pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis with PR3-ANCA","E":"Postinfectious glomerulonephritis with subepithelial humps"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The absence of immune deposits with segmental necrosis and crescents defines pauci-immune crescentic GN, and PR3-ANCA, previously called c-ANCA, is characteristic of granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which involves the upper respiratory tract and lungs. Anti-GBM disease shows linear IgG, and immune complex disease shows granular deposits. C3 glomerulonephritis shows C3 deposits and postinfectious disease shows subepithelial humps, whereas this biopsy has no immune deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pauci-immune RPGN is the most common type of crescentic GN. COMLEX: c-ANCA points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis and p-ANCA to microscopic polyangiitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — classification of glomerular disease by immune pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis has a renal biopsy that shows crescents containing fibrin strands. Which event initiates crescent formation?","choices":{"A":"Podocyte loss from adaptive hyperfiltration with segmental sclerosis","B":"GBM rupture letting proteins and leukocytes enter Bowman space","C":"Glucose-derived matrix accumulation in the mesangium with nodule formation","D":"Thrombosis of the afferent arteriole with patchy cortical infarction","E":"Cast obstruction of the proximal tubules with backpressure on the tuft"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regardless of the underlying type, electron microscopy may show ruptures in the GBM that allow leukocytes, plasma proteins such as coagulation factors and complement, and inflammatory mediators to reach the urinary space, where they trigger proliferation of parietal epithelial cells and macrophages, forming crescents rich in fibrin. Podocyte loss from hyperfiltration leads to segmental sclerosis, mesangial nodules are diabetic glomerulosclerosis, afferent thrombosis causes wedge-shaped infarcts, and tubular casts are myeloma cast nephropathy; none of them breaks the GBM to start a crescent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fibrin and GBM breaks in Bowman space drive crescent formation. COMLEX: early aggressive therapy can prevent scarring.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — the parietal epithelium of Bowman capsule is the layer that proliferates into crescents after capillary wall rupture","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with SLE has hematuria, proteinuria of 2.5 g/day, hypertension, and creatinine of 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Biopsy shows proliferation in most glomeruli, wire loops, and granular immune deposits. Which class and immunofluorescence pattern are most likely?","choices":{"A":"Class II with granular mesangial IgA deposits and no C1q staining","B":"Class III with linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","C":"Class V with fine granular deposits over podocytes and no complement","D":"Class VI with global sclerosis and no immune deposits present","E":"Class IV with granular multi-immunoglobulin and C1q staining"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Proliferation in more than 50 percent of glomeruli with wire loops is diffuse, class IV, lupus nephritis, the most common and severe form. Immune complexes are usually reactive for IgG, often accompanied by IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q, in a granular pattern. Linear IgG would indicate anti-GBM disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class IV with wire loops and a full immunofluorescence pattern is the classic severe lupus nephritis. COMLEX: induction immunosuppression is required.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — immunofluorescence patterns distinguish glomerular diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with SLE has new hematuria with red cell casts and 1.5 g/day of proteinuria. Renal biopsy shows that 7 of 20 sampled glomeruli have segmental endocapillary proliferation with leukocytes and focal necrosis, two with small crescents; the remaining glomeruli show mesangial deposits only. Which class of lupus nephritis is this?","choices":{"A":"Class III focal lupus nephritis","B":"Class II mesangial proliferative","C":"Class IV diffuse lupus nephritis","D":"Class V membranous lupus nephritis","E":"Class VI advanced sclerosing lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Focal lupus nephritis (class III) is defined by involvement of fewer than 50% of glomeruli; lesions may be segmental or global, with endothelial and mesangial proliferation, leukocytes, capillary necrosis, and often crescents, and red cell casts are common when the disease is active. Here 7 of 20 glomeruli (35%) are involved. Class II shows mesangial proliferation and mesangial deposits without capillary involvement or endocapillary inflammation. Class IV has similar lesions in more than 50% of glomeruli. Class V is diffuse capillary wall thickening from subepithelial immune complexes with nephrotic-range proteinuria. Class VI is global sclerosis of 90% or more of glomeruli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lupus nephritis: I minimal mesangial, II mesangial proliferative, III focal (<50% of glomeruli), IV diffuse (>50%, most common and severe, wire loops), V membranous (subepithelial, nephrotic), VI advanced sclerosing (≥90% sclerosed). COMLEX: Classes III and IV can progress, so the percentage of involved glomeruli on biopsy guides treatment intensity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — crescents form within active proliferative glomerular lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with long-standing lupus nephritis has advanced CKD, and a repeat biopsy shows scarring of most glomeruli. Which class best describes this final histologic state?","choices":{"A":"Class I with immune deposits and no structural changes","B":"Class II with mesangial proliferation and deposits","C":"Class VI with 90 percent or more of glomeruli sclerosed","D":"Class III with fewer than half of glomeruli involved","E":"Class V with subepithelial deposits and GBM thickening"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Advanced sclerosing lupus nephritis, class VI, is characterized by global sclerosis of 90 percent or more of glomeruli. Lesions can evolve from one class to another over time, and active proliferative lesions in classes III and IV can heal with scarring that culminates in this state.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class VI is end-stage sclerosis and is not responsive to immunosuppression. COMLEX: class transitions determine renal survival, so early treatment of proliferative classes matters.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — nephron loss and hyperfiltration drive the global glomerulosclerosis that defines class VI","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl32_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy has cola-colored urine, periorbital edema, and a blood pressure of 150/96 mm Hg 2 weeks after impetigo. Creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), C3 is 42 mg/dL (reference range: 90–180 mg/dL), and urinalysis shows red cell casts with protein of 0.6 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h). Which process best explains his reduced glomerular filtration?","choices":{"A":"Podocyte effacement with massive albumin leakage","B":"Antibodies binding type IV collagen in capillary walls","C":"Proliferating cells and leukocytes blocking capillaries","D":"Subepithelial spikes thickening the capillary wall","E":"Mesangial IgA deposits after a mucosal infection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nephritic syndrome 1 to 4 weeks after a streptococcal skin infection, with low C3, is postinfectious GN, in which leukocyte infiltration and proliferation of endothelial and mesangial cells follow the infection. Proliferation, endothelial swelling, and neutrophil and monocyte infiltration obliterate the capillary lumens and injure the capillary walls, lowering GFR and causing oliguria, fluid retention, and azotemia, while hypertension reflects fluid retention and renin release from the ischemic kidneys. Podocyte effacement with massive albumin loss produces nephrotic, not nephritic, syndrome, and his proteinuria is mild. Antibodies binding type IV collagen are not tied to a preceding streptococcal infection with complement consumption. Subepithelial spikes characterize membranous nephropathy, a nephrotic lesion. Mesangial IgA deposits produce hematuria concurrently with or within 1 to 2 days of a mucosal infection, not after a 2-week latent period. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (nephritic syndrome; acute postinfectious GN morphology and clinical features); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, synpharyngitic hematuria vs. postinfectious latency).","boardPrep":"Step 1: postinfectious GN follows pharyngitis or impetigo by 1 to 4 weeks (Robbins; First Aid 2025 gives about 2 to 4 weeks) with low C3 and elevated antistreptococcal titers. COMLEX: more than 95% of affected children recover renal function with conservative salt and water management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — the same capillary wall that leaks protein in nephrotic disease is inflamed and occluded here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl32_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man being treated for Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis develops dark urine with red cell casts, and his creatinine rises to 2.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Biopsy shows enlarged, hypercellular glomeruli with neutrophils in capillary lumens, granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls, and discrete hump-shaped deposits on the epithelial side of the basement membrane. Which mechanism best explains his glomerular injury?","choices":{"A":"Septic emboli of bacteria lodged in glomerular capillaries","B":"Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","C":"ANCA activation of neutrophils without deposits","D":"Podocyte loss from adaptive glomerular hypertension","E":"Immune complexes containing microbial antigens"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Glomerulonephritis like the poststreptococcal form occurs with other infections, including staphylococcal endocarditis, and is caused by deposition or formation of immune complexes containing microbial antigens that recruit leukocytes and stimulate glomerular cell proliferation. Granular immunoglobulin and complement with subepithelial humps are the signature of immune complex nephritis. Septic emboli would not produce granular IgG and C3 deposits or subepithelial humps. Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen do not fit this granular, hump-forming deposit pattern. ANCA-associated injury is pauci-immune, with little or no immune staining. Adaptive glomerular hypertension produces segmental sclerosis with podocyte loss rather than an acute proliferative, deposit-rich lesion. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (acute postinfectious and infection-associated GN; granular deposits and subepithelial humps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: subepithelial humps with granular IgG and C3 indicate immune complex GN, whether the antigen is streptococcal, staphylococcal, viral, or parasitic. COMLEX: in adults, poststreptococcal GN is less benign, and only about 60% of sporadic cases recover promptly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — immune complex deposition is one route to crescentic, rapidly progressive GN","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl32_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has 3 weeks of dark urine and falling urine output, with no hemoptysis. Creatinine has risen from 0.9 to 5.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and urine shows red cell casts. Biopsy shows crescents in 85% of glomeruli and smooth linear IgG and C3 along every capillary loop. Which serum finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies to neutrophil myeloperoxidase","B":"Antinuclear antibodies at high titer","C":"Elevated antistreptococcal antibody titers","D":"Antibodies to type IV collagen alpha-3 chains","E":"Antibodies to the podocyte PLA2 receptor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crescents in most glomeruli with smooth linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane and no deposits are type I, anti-GBM antibody-mediated crescentic GN, in which antibodies bind a peptide in the noncollagenous portion of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen. Renal-limited anti-GBM disease occurs without pulmonary hemorrhage, so the absence of hemoptysis does not exclude it. Antimyeloperoxidase antibodies mark pauci-immune crescentic GN, which has little or no immune staining. Antinuclear antibodies point to lupus nephritis, an immune complex disease with granular deposits. Elevated antistreptococcal titers accompany postinfectious GN, which also gives granular rather than linear staining. Antibodies to the PLA2 receptor cause membranous nephropathy with granular subepithelial deposits and a nephrotic course. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (crescentic GN classification; anti-GBM antibody-mediated disease and Goodpasture syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: linear IgG means antibody bound to an intrinsic basement membrane antigen, granular staining means immune complexes, and absent staining means pauci-immune disease. COMLEX: plasmapheresis to remove the pathogenic antibodies, with immunosuppression, is the mainstay of treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain targeted here belongs to the collagen IV network mutated in Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl32_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has 3 weeks of fatigue and dark urine, and his creatinine has risen to 4.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Urine shows red cell casts. Biopsy shows fibrinoid necrosis and crescents in 70% of glomeruli, little or no immunoglobulin or complement on immunofluorescence, and no deposits on electron microscopy. Which mechanism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcal immune complexes in the capillary wall","B":"Galactose-deficient IgA1 complexes in the mesangium","C":"Autoantibodies to basement membrane collagen","D":"Lupus immune complexes with full-house staining","E":"ANCA-mediated neutrophil injury of the tuft"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Crescents in more than 50% of glomeruli with fibrinoid necrosis define RPGN, and a pauci-immune pattern, with little or no immunoglobulin or complement and no electron-dense deposits, is type III RPGN, associated with circulating ANCA against PR3 or MPO that activate neutrophils against the glomerular tuft. Streptococcal immune complexes give coarse granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial humps (type II). Galactose-deficient IgA1 complexes give granular mesangial IgA (type II). Autoantibodies to basement membrane collagen give continuous linear IgG and C3 (type I). Lupus immune complexes give granular full-house staining with deposits (type II). Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 32, immunologic classification of RPGN).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Immunofluorescence sorts RPGN into linear (type I, anti-GBM), granular (type II, immune complex), and pauci-immune (type III, ANCA) patterns. COMLEX: Pauci-immune RPGN is treated with high-dose corticosteroids plus cyclophosphamide or rituximab.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — leukocyte-mediated GBM rupture is the nephritic injury that crescents follow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl32_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with SLE has new hypertension, a creatinine of 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), proteinuria of 2.9 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h), and red cell casts. Biopsy shows endocapillary proliferation in 19 of 24 glomeruli, several with crescents, rigid circumferential capillary wall thickening, full-house granular staining, and subendothelial deposits. Which class of lupus nephritis is this?","choices":{"A":"Class IV diffuse proliferative lesion","B":"Class II mesangial proliferative lesion","C":"Class III focal proliferative lesion","D":"Class V membranous lesion","E":"Class VI advanced sclerosing lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Proliferative lesions in 19 of 24 glomeruli, about 79%, several with crescents, exceed the 50% threshold that defines diffuse, class IV, lupus nephritis, and circumferential capillary wall thickening from subendothelial deposits forms the wire loops typical of this class. Class II does not fit because this biopsy shows capillary-loop proliferation. Class III has the same active lesions but involves fewer than 50% of glomeruli. Class V shows diffuse capillary wall thickening from subepithelial deposits and presents with severe proteinuria or nephrotic syndrome. Class VI requires global sclerosis of 90% or more of glomeruli, not active proliferation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (lupus nephritis classes); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 32, wire-loop lesions in class IV lupus nephritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: classes III and IV share the same proliferative lesions and differ by extent, so counting involved glomeruli decides the class. COMLEX: class IV is the most common and most severe form, with hematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, and renal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the cellular crescents of diffuse lupus nephritis make it a cause of immune complex crescentic GN","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl32_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with newly diagnosed SLE has a normal creatinine and blood pressure, no hematuria, and proteinuria of 0.4 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h). Light microscopy is normal, immunofluorescence shows granular IgG, C3, and C1q in the mesangium, and electron microscopy shows small mesangial deposits. Which class of lupus nephritis is this?","choices":{"A":"Class II mesangial proliferative lesion","B":"Class I minimal mesangial lesion","C":"Class III focal proliferative lesion","D":"Class IV diffuse proliferative lesion","E":"Class V membranous lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mesangial immune complexes detected by immunofluorescence and electron microscopy, with no structural change by light microscopy, define minimal mesangial, class I, lupus nephritis, the least common class, which typically causes minimal or no proteinuria and preserved renal function. Class II does not fit because the light microscopy shows no mesangial hypercellularity. Classes III and IV do not fit because the biopsy shows no endocapillary proliferative lesions. Class V does not fit because the capillary walls are normal and free of subepithelial deposits. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (lupus nephritis classes); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 32, lupus nephritis classes and clinical features).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lupus immune complexes usually contain IgG with IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q, the full-house pattern. COMLEX: lesions can evolve from one class to another over time, so mild lupus nephritis still needs follow-up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 1 — the mesangium is also the defining deposit site of IgA nephropathy","isHighYield":true}],"33":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most common type of glomerulonephritis worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Membranous nephropathy","B":"Minimal change disease","C":"Lupus nephritis","D":"Anti-GBM disease","E":"IgA nephropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IgA nephropathy, characterized by prominent mesangial IgA deposits and recurrent hematuria, is the most common type of glomerulonephritis worldwide. Membranous nephropathy, minimal change disease, lupus nephritis, and anti-GBM disease are less common causes of glomerular disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA nephropathy is also called Berger disease and is the leading cause of primary glomerulonephritis worldwide. COMLEX: think of young adults with recurrent gross hematuria after infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — poststreptococcal GN is the classic contrast case, with a delayed onset and low C3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern characterizes IgA nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial IgA deposits with C3 and properdin","B":"Linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","C":"Coarsely granular IgG and C3 along the capillary walls","D":"Subepithelial IgG deposits separated by spikes","E":"Absent deposits with segmental fibrinoid necrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The characteristic immunofluorescence finding in IgA nephropathy is mesangial deposition of IgA, often with C3 and properdin and lesser amounts of IgG or IgM. Early complement components are usually absent. Linear IgG indicates anti-GBM disease, granular capillary wall deposits indicate poststreptococcal GN, and subepithelial deposits indicate membranous nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mesangial IgA on immunofluorescence is characteristic, and diagnostic once secondary causes are excluded; light microscopy can be variable. COMLEX: electron microscopy shows dense deposits mainly in the mesangium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — poststreptococcal GN is the classic contrast case, with a delayed onset and low C3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which abnormality is thought to initiate IgA nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV","B":"Aberrant O-glycosylation of the IgA1 hinge region","C":"Antibodies against the M-type phospholipase A2 receptor","D":"Mutations in the COL4A5 gene encoding collagen IV","E":"Excess IgG4 deposition along the subepithelial surface"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A key facet of IgA nephropathy is a hereditary or acquired defect in the formation or attachment of galactose-containing O-linked glycans to the hinge region of IgA1. This aberrantly glycosylated IgA is deposited or elicits IgG autoantibodies, forming mesangial immune complexes. The other options describe anti-GBM disease, membranous nephropathy, Alport syndrome, and the subepithelial IgG4 deposition of membranous disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA nephropathy is a multi-hit disease beginning with galactose-deficient IgA1. COMLEX: mucosal exposure to viruses, bacteria, or food proteins can drive production of abnormal IgA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — poststreptococcal GN is the classic contrast case, with a delayed onset and low C3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene is mutated in the X-linked form of Alport syndrome?","choices":{"A":"COL4A3","B":"COL4A4","C":"COL4A5","D":"NPHS1","E":"TRPC6"},"correct":"C","explanation":"About 85 percent of Alport syndrome is X-linked and results from mutations in COL4A5, which encodes the alpha-5 chain of type IV collagen. COL4A3 and COL4A4 are autosomal Alport genes, NPHS1 encodes nephrin, and TRPC6 encodes a podocyte calcium channel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COL4A5 on the X chromosome means males have full disease and female carriers have milder hematuria. COMLEX: autosomal forms involve COL4A3 and COL4A4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV is also the target antigen in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":1,"stem":"Which electron microscopic finding is characteristic of Alport syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Discrete electron-dense subepithelial humps","B":"Basket-weave splitting and lamination of the lamina densa","C":"Uniform thinning of the basement membrane to 150 to 225 nm","D":"Subendothelial deposits producing wire loops","E":"Diffuse foot process effacement over a normal basement membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fully developed Alport syndrome shows irregular thickening alternating with attenuation of the GBM and pronounced splitting and lamination of the lamina densa, producing a basket-weave appearance. Subepithelial humps indicate poststreptococcal GN, uniform thinning suggests thin basement membrane nephropathy, wire loops indicate lupus, and effacement over a normal membrane suggests minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: basket-weave GBM equals Alport syndrome. COMLEX: light microscopy is normal early, and antibodies to collagen IV chains fail to stain the membranes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV is also the target antigen in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of extrarenal features accompanies Alport syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Conductive deafness and retinal detachment","B":"Optic neuritis and vestibular schwannoma","C":"Cataracts and polycystic liver disease","D":"Sensorineural deafness and lens abnormalities","E":"Hearing loss and intracranial aneurysms"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Type IV collagen is crucial to the GBM, the lens of the eye, and the cochlea, so Alport syndrome causes sensorineural deafness and eye disorders such as lens dislocation, anterior lenticonus, posterior cataracts, and corneal dystrophy. Each other pair combines a feature Alport syndrome does not produce, such as retinal detachment, schwannoma, polycystic liver disease, or aneurysms, with conductive loss or an unrelated finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cannot see, cannot pee, cannot hear a bee, meaning eye, kidney, and ear involvement. COMLEX: audiometry may be needed because deafness can be subtle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV is also the target antigen in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts in the urine indicate which source of hematuria?","choices":{"A":"A glomerular source","B":"A lower urinary tract source","C":"A renal papillary source","D":"A ureteral stone source","E":"A bladder mucosal source"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dysmorphic red cells, distorted by passage through the damaged glomerular capillary wall, and red cell casts, molded in the tubules, point to a glomerular source of bleeding. Lower urinary tract bleeding typically produces normal-shaped red cells without casts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: red cell casts are highly specific for glomerular bleeding. COMLEX: a glomerular source prompts evaluation for nephritic disease, whereas non-glomerular bleeding prompts imaging and cystoscopy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts are the urinary signature of glomerular inflammation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding in a patient with IgA nephropathy predicts a greater risk of progression?","choices":{"A":"Recurrent gross hematuria after infections","B":"Onset in older childhood with normal blood pressure","C":"Heavy proteinuria with hypertension","D":"Isolated microscopic hematuria with no proteinuria","E":"Preserved renal function at initial presentation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Onset in old age, heavy proteinuria, hypertension, and the extent of glomerulosclerosis on biopsy are clues to an increased risk of progression in IgA nephropathy. Recurrent gross hematuria, isolated microscopic hematuria, and childhood onset with normal pressure are associated with a more benign course. Preserved function at presentation is likewise a favorable feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteinuria and hypertension are important bedside predictors of progression. COMLEX: about 15 to 40 percent progress to chronic renal failure over 20 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — progression to glomerulosclerosis follows the same injury pathways described there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which description best fits the usual course of thin basement membrane nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Progressive renal failure by age 40 in most affected males","B":"Nephrotic syndrome with hyperlipidemia and lipiduria","C":"Rapidly progressive crescentic glomerulonephritis","D":"Familial hematuria with preserved renal function typically","E":"Recurrent gross hematuria with nephrotic-range proteinuria"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy is associated with mutations in the alpha-3 or alpha-4 chains of type IV collagen, usually heterozygous and autosomal, and typically causes familial hematuria with attenuation of the GBM to 150 to 225 nm and normal or only mildly affected renal function. Progressive failure characterizes classic X-linked Alport syndrome in males.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thin basement membrane disease is the benign end of the type IV collagen spectrum. COMLEX: genetic counseling and follow-up are advised because relatives may have classic Alport syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the collagen IV network is defective in thin basement membrane disease as in Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man notes cola-colored urine within one to two days of each upper respiratory infection, which clears in several days. Serum C3 is normal. Which pathogenic sequence best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Galactose-deficient IgA1 forms complexes with IgG that deposit in the mesangium","B":"Streptococcal antigens form subepithelial complexes that activate the classical pathway","C":"Autoantibodies to the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV bind the glomerular basement membrane","D":"ANCA activate neutrophils that injure the glomerular capillary walls","E":"Anti-nephrin antibodies disrupt the slit diaphragm and efface foot processes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Recurrent gross hematuria coinciding with mucosal infection in a young adult with normal C3 fits IgA nephropathy. Aberrantly O-glycosylated IgA1 elicits IgG autoantibodies, and the resulting immune complexes deposit in the mesangium, activating mesangial cells. Poststreptococcal GN has a longer latent period and low C3, and the other mechanisms describe anti-GBM, ANCA, and minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hematuria during or within days of infection suggests IgA nephropathy, whereas one to four weeks later suggests poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: C3 is normal in IgA nephropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — comparison with poststreptococcal GN and its delayed onset","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has gross hematuria one day after an upper respiratory infection. Renal biopsy shows mesangial IgA and C3 deposits, but C1q and C4 are absent. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Immune complexes containing IgG activate the classical complement pathway","B":"The findings indicate lupus nephritis because complement is present","C":"The findings indicate anti-GBM disease because C3 is present","D":"The findings favor alternative-pathway complement activation by deposited IgA","E":"The findings indicate poststreptococcal GN because C3 is present"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deposited IgA and IgA-containing complexes may activate the alternative complement pathway, so C3 is present while C1q and C4 are absent, which is typical of IgA nephropathy. Lupus nephritis usually shows C1q as well, anti-GBM disease shows linear IgG, and poststreptococcal GN shows subepithelial humps with granular IgG and C3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C3 present with C1q and C4 absent points to alternative pathway activation, as in IgA nephropathy. COMLEX: lupus shows a full-house immunofluorescence pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — lupus full-house staining with C1q marks classical pathway activation, unlike the alternative pathway in IgA nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with celiac disease develops recurrent episodes of gross hematuria, and biopsy shows mesangial IgA deposits. Which link between her conditions is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Gluten directly binds the glomerular basement membrane and triggers linear IgG staining","B":"Celiac disease deposits IgG4 in subepithelial locations through anti-PLA2R antibodies","C":"Intestinal mucosal defects increase exposure and production of aberrant IgA","D":"Malabsorption depletes vitamin D and leads to mesangial calcification","E":"Villous atrophy raises circulating antistreptolysin O titers with low complement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IgA nephropathy occurs with increased frequency in patients with gluten enteropathy, in whom intestinal mucosal defects are seen, and in liver disease, in which hepatobiliary clearance of IgA complexes is defective. Increased synthesis of abnormal IgA may occur in response to mucosal exposure to environmental agents. The biopsy shows IgA deposits, not calcification, and the described link is aberrant IgA production after mucosal exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease and liver disease are recognized associations of secondary IgA nephropathy. COMLEX: Henoch-Schonlein purpura shows the same mesangial IgA deposits in children.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — immune complex nephritis and its infectious or mucosal triggers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has hematuria, high-frequency hearing loss, and a maternal uncle on dialysis. Electron microscopy shows basement membrane lamination. Which genetic defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"X-linked mutation in COL4A5 that impairs type IV collagen assembly","B":"Autosomal recessive mutation in NPHS1 that disrupts nephrin","C":"Autosomal dominant mutation in ACTN4 that disrupts podocyte actin","D":"X-linked mutation in the androgen receptor that blocks its action","E":"Autosomal dominant mutation in PKD1 that disrupts polycystin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hematuria with sensorineural hearing loss and an affected maternal uncle, with a lamellated basement membrane, is X-linked Alport syndrome. Mutations in COL4A5 interfere with assembly of the alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 collagen IV network, and large deletions are linked to earlier end-stage disease. Males express the full syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked inheritance explains affected males and hematuria in female carriers. COMLEX: most affected males reach ESRD in early to middle adulthood.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — the collagen IV network is the target in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has persistent microscopic hematuria found on routine urinalysis. Her creatinine is normal, she has no hearing loss, and several relatives have the same finding. Electron microscopy shows diffuse GBM thinning to about 200 nm. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Classic X-linked Alport syndrome","B":"IgA nephropathy with mesangial deposits","C":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy","D":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","E":"Anti-GBM disease with crescents"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Familial hematuria with normal renal function and diffuse attenuation of the GBM to 150 to 225 nm, compared with 300 to 400 nm in healthy adults, is thin basement membrane nephropathy, associated with heterozygous alpha-3 or alpha-4 collagen IV mutations. Alport syndrome shows lamination, deafness, and progressive failure, and IgA nephropathy shows mesangial deposits. Poststreptococcal disease is nephritic with subepithelial humps, and anti-GBM disease is rapidly progressive with crescents and linear IgG; neither gives familial hematuria with normal creatinine and a thin GBM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thin GBM with familial hematuria and normal function is benign. COMLEX: follow blood pressure and proteinuria over time.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — type IV collagen chains in health and disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy has hematuria and sensorineural hearing loss, and two maternal uncles had kidney failure. X-linked Alport syndrome is suspected, but renal electron microscopy is not available. Which alternative finding would support the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Linear IgG staining of the skin basement membrane","B":"Absent alpha-5 collagen IV staining in skin","C":"Mesangial IgA staining in a renal biopsy","D":"Anti-PLA2R antibodies in the patient serum","E":"Positive PR3-ANCA in the patient serum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Immunohistochemistry can help in classic Alport syndrome, since antibodies to the alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 collagen chains fail to stain glomerular and tubular basement membranes in the classic X-linked form, and alpha-5 staining is also absent in skin biopsy specimens. PR3-ANCA marks pauci-immune vasculitis such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis, not a collagen IV defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an abnormal skin stain supports the diagnosis, and a normal stain does not exclude it. COMLEX: genetic testing is the definitive confirmation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — the alpha-3 collagen IV chain targeted in anti-GBM disease belongs to the same network that is defective in Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has persistent microscopic hematuria with dysmorphic red cells, proteinuria of 1.5 g/day, hypertension, high-frequency hearing loss, and anterior lenticonus. Which conclusion best summarizes his prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Low risk because isolated hematuria carries a benign long-term outlook","B":"Low risk because thin basement membrane disease seldom causes hearing loss","C":"Uncertain risk because a nonglomerular source of bleeding is more likely","D":"High risk because extrarenal Alport features accompany renal markers","E":"High risk because recurrent gross hematuria after infections predicts renal failure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensorineural hearing loss and ocular abnormalities point to Alport syndrome rather than thin basement membrane disease, and proteinuria and hypertension add to the risk. Dysmorphic red cells indicate a glomerular source, and in affected males the course is progressive, with renal failure in early to middle adulthood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: extrarenal features, proteinuria, hypertension, and reduced GFR raise the risk in hematuria. COMLEX: hearing loss and eye findings separate Alport from IgA nephropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — proteinuria and hypertension drive progression to glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has isolated microscopic hematuria with dysmorphic red cells on repeated testing, with normal blood pressure, no proteinuria, a normal GFR, and no family history. Which approach is most appropriate initially?","choices":{"A":"Immediate high-dose corticosteroids for presumed glomerulonephritis","B":"Periodic reassessment of renal risk markers over time","C":"Urgent plasmapheresis for suspected anti-GBM disease","D":"Cyclophosphamide induction for presumed lupus nephritis","E":"Immediate kidney biopsy to establish a tissue diagnosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A low-risk profile of isolated microscopic hematuria with preserved GFR, normal blood pressure, and no proteinuria supports monitoring for the appearance of proteinuria, hypertension, or falling GFR, which would signal progression. Aggressive immunosuppression or plasmapheresis is not indicated without evidence of active inflammatory disease, and biopsy is reserved for proteinuria, hypertension, or falling GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk stratify hematuria by proteinuria, blood pressure, GFR, and extrarenal features. COMLEX: persistent glomerular hematuria still warrants long-term follow-up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — progressive glomerular injury is what proteinuria and GFR follow over time","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A biopsy in a 55-year-old with IgA nephropathy is reviewed for prognostic features. Which finding most strongly indicates a risk of progression?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial IgA deposition with C3","B":"Minor focal mesangial expansion","C":"Electron-dense mesangial deposits","D":"Preserved tubules without interstitial change","E":"Extensive glomerulosclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The extent of glomerulosclerosis on biopsy is a clue to increased risk of progression in IgA nephropathy, along with old age at onset, heavy proteinuria, and hypertension. Mesangial IgA deposition, minor mesangial expansion, and mesangial deposits are diagnostic or active features, whereas established sclerosis is irreversible and is a major indicator of progression. Preserved tubules and interstitium point away from progression, since tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis are hallmarks of progressive renal damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sclerosis and tubulointerstitial scarring indicate irreversible damage. COMLEX: active lesions may respond to treatment, but sclerotic glomeruli do not recover.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — secondary global glomerulosclerosis in progressive injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl33_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":3,"stem":"A 21-year-old man has had three episodes of cola-colored urine, each starting 1 to 2 days after a sore throat. Biopsy shows dominant mesangial IgA with C3 and properdin but no C1q, and his circulating IgA1 lacks galactose on its hinge-region O-glycans. Which event converts these molecules into the complexes that lodge in his mesangium?","choices":{"A":"Planting of streptococcal exotoxin B in capillaries","B":"Binding of IgG autoantibodies to the altered IgA1","C":"Binding of C1q to start the classical pathway","D":"Binding of anti-nephrin IgG to the slit diaphragm","E":"Hepatic uptake of the polymeric IgA complexes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IgA nephropathy is a multi-hit disease: a hereditary or acquired defect in attaching galactose-containing O-linked glycans to the IgA1 hinge region produces aberrant IgA, which elicits IgG (or IgA) autoantibodies directed against the abnormal molecules, and binding of these antibodies forms immune complexes that deposit in the mesangium and activate mesangial cells. Streptococcal exotoxin B is the planted antigen of poststreptococcal GN, which follows infection by 1 to 4 weeks and lowers serum C3, unlike his synpharyngitic episodes. C1q is absent from his deposits because deposited IgA activates the alternative rather than the classical pathway. Anti-nephrin IgG targets the podocyte slit diaphragm in minimal change disease. Hepatic uptake catabolizes polymeric IgA, removing rather than forming the complexes. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (IgA nephropathy pathogenesis and complement activation); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, multi-hit pathogenesis and synpharyngitic hematuria).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hematuria within 1 to 2 days of a mucosal infection points to IgA nephropathy, whereas a 1- to 4-week latent period with low C3 points to poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: IgA nephropathy is the most common glomerulonephritis worldwide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — poststreptococcal GN is the contrast case, with a 1- to 4-week latent period and low C3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl33_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has 5 days of raised purpura over the buttocks and the extensor surfaces of his legs, colicky abdominal pain with guaiac-positive stools, and painful knees, beginning a week after an upper respiratory infection. Urinalysis shows red blood cells and 1+ protein. Skin biopsy shows necrotizing vasculitis of small dermal vessels with subepidermal hemorrhage. Which immunofluorescence pattern is most likely in his glomeruli?","choices":{"A":"Linear IgG along capillary basement membranes","B":"Coarse granular IgG and C3 in capillary walls","C":"Little or no immunoglobulin or complement","D":"Mesangial IgA with variable IgG and C3","E":"Full-house granular staining including C1q"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Purpura on the buttocks and extensor surfaces, abdominal pain with intestinal bleeding, and arthralgia in a young child after an upper respiratory infection, with a small-vessel necrotizing vasculitis in the skin, is Henoch-Schönlein purpura, and its pathognomonic glomerular immunofluorescence finding is mesangial IgA, sometimes with IgG and C3. The distribution matches IgA nephropathy, supporting the view that the two are manifestations of the same disease. Linear IgG is the anti-GBM pattern. Coarse granular IgG and C3 in capillary walls is the postinfectious GN pattern, which follows infection by 1 to 4 weeks without purpura or vasculitis. Little or no immune staining defines pauci-immune ANCA-associated GN. Full-house granular staining with C1q points to lupus nephritis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (Henoch-Schönlein purpura; IgA nephropathy immunofluorescence).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA vasculitis is the systemic counterpart of IgA nephropathy, and IgA with IgG and C3 is also found in the dermal vessels. COMLEX: most children have an excellent prognosis, but diffuse lesions, crescents, or nephrotic syndrome predict a poorer outcome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — IgA vasculitis is one of the immune complex causes of crescentic GN","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl33_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl and her 13-year-old brother both have hematuria, progressive proteinuria, and sensorineural hearing loss; the girl's creatinine is 1.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Their parents are first cousins with at most isolated microscopic hematuria. The girl's biopsy shows a split, basket-weave lamina densa. Which genetic defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"X-linked COL4A5 variant affecting the alpha-5 chain","B":"Recessive NPHS2 variant affecting podocin","C":"Recessive COL4A3 variant affecting the alpha-3 chain","D":"Recessive NPHS1 variant affecting nephrin","E":"Dominant ACTN4 variant affecting alpha-actinin 4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Basket-weave splitting of the lamina densa with hematuria and sensorineural deafness is Alport syndrome, and equally severe disease in a girl and her brother born to consanguineous parents with at most mild findings indicates autosomal recessive inheritance, in which males and females are equally susceptible. The autosomal forms arise from mutations in COL4A3 or COL4A4, which encode the alpha-3 and alpha-4 collagen IV chains on autosomes. In the X-linked COL4A5 form, males express the full syndrome, so an affected father would have full disease rather than isolated hematuria, and a girl would typically be less severely affected than her brother. NPHS2 and NPHS1 encode the slit diaphragm proteins podocin and nephrin, whose mutations cause nephrotic syndrome with foot process effacement rather than basement membrane lamination and deafness. ACTN4 mutations cause autosomal dominant FSGS. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (Alport syndrome genetics and morphology; inherited FSGS); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, COL4A3 and COL4A4 autosomal variants).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 85% of Alport syndrome is X-linked through COL4A5, but autosomal forms through COL4A3 or COL4A4 affect males and females equally. COMLEX: the auditory defect may be subtle and require sensitive testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain is also the antigen targeted in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl33_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has persistent hematuria and subtle high-frequency hearing loss, and his maternal uncle reached end-stage kidney disease at 34. Genetic testing finds a pathogenic COL4A5 variant only. Immunostaining shows absent alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 collagen IV in glomerular and tubular basement membranes. Which mechanism best explains the loss of the alpha-3 and alpha-4 chains?","choices":{"A":"Complement-mediated degradation of the basement membrane","B":"Autoantibody destruction of basement membrane collagen","C":"Replacement of the membrane by mesangial matrix","D":"Failed assembly into a shared trimeric network","E":"Replacement of collagen by amyloid fibril deposits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The basement membrane collagen network is built from trimeric molecules composed of alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 chains, so a mutation in any one chain causes defective assembly of the whole network, which is why antibodies to all three chains fail to stain glomerular and tubular basement membranes in classic X-linked Alport syndrome even though only COL4A5 is mutated. Complement-mediated degradation would require immune deposits, whereas immunofluorescence in Alport syndrome is initially negative or nonspecific. Autoantibody destruction of collagen characterizes anti-GBM disease with linear IgG, not an inherited structural defect. Mesangial matrix replacement could not explain loss of staining in tubular basement membranes, which have no mesangium. Amyloid replacement describes an acquired deposition disease, not an inherited collagen chain defect, and no fibrillar deposits are described. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (Alport syndrome pathogenesis and immunohistochemistry).","boardPrep":"Step 1: one mutated collagen IV chain removes its partners from the basement membrane because the alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 chains assemble as one trimeric network. COMLEX: absent alpha-5 staining in a skin biopsy can support classic X-linked Alport syndrome when electron microscopy is unavailable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — type IV collagen forms the basement membrane layer of the glomerular filtration barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl33_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with biopsy-proven IgA nephropathy had isolated microscopic hematuria 3 years ago. He takes no medications. He now has proteinuria of 1.4 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h), a blood pressure of 146/94 mm Hg, and a creatinine that has risen gradually from 1.0 to 1.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Which process best explains the change in his course?","choices":{"A":"New anti-GBM antibodies binding the membrane","B":"Superimposed PLA2R-driven subepithelial deposits","C":"Interstitial nephritis from drug hypersensitivity","D":"Ischemic tubular injury from hypotension","E":"Secondary segmental sclerosis from podocyte loss"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In IgA nephropathy, healing of focal proliferative lesions can lead to secondary focal segmental sclerosis, and the course framework treats proteinuria rising above 0.5 to 1.0 g/day as a major indicator of secondary FSGS with podocyte detachment and accelerated chronic kidney disease; heavy proteinuria and hypertension are also recognized clues to progression. New anti-GBM antibodies would cause rapidly progressive crescentic disease over weeks with linear IgG, not a gradual rise over years. PLA2R-driven subepithelial deposits define a separate membranous disease presenting with nephrotic syndrome, which his course does not suggest. Drug hypersensitivity nephritis is unlikely because he takes no medications. Ischemic tubular injury follows an acute hypoperfusion event with an abrupt fall in function, whereas his creatinine rose gradually over years without any hypotensive event and he is hypertensive. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (IgA nephropathy morphology and clinical course); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, microscopic hematuria prognostic framework).","boardPrep":"Step 1: onset in old age, heavy proteinuria, hypertension, and the extent of glomerulosclerosis predict progression of IgA nephropathy. COMLEX: slow progression to chronic renal failure occurs in 15% to 40% of patients over 20 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — podocyte loss and glomerular hypertension drive progressive glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl33_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":3,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy has persistent microscopic hematuria with dysmorphic red cells, protein of 120 mg/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h), and a creatinine of 0.5 mg/dL (reference range for age: 0.3–0.7 mg/dL). His maternal uncle began dialysis at age 32, and his mother has microscopic hematuria. Which additional result would most increase his predicted risk of early end-stage kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"A large deletion within the COL4A5 gene","B":"A heterozygous COL4A4 variant on testing","C":"Uniform membrane thinning without lamination","D":"Normal hearing on formal audiometry","E":"Red cell casts on urine microscopy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Glomerular hematuria in a boy whose maternal uncle needed dialysis and whose mother has hematuria suggests X-linked Alport syndrome, and in X-linked disease large deletions in COL4A5 are associated with end-stage disease at an earlier age, placing him in a high-risk category. A heterozygous COL4A4 variant is the typical genotype of thin basement membrane nephropathy, the milder end of the collagen IV spectrum. Uniform basement membrane thinning without lamination is the thin basement membrane pattern, which usually carries normal renal function. Normal hearing on formal audiometry would lower rather than raise concern, because sensorineural hearing loss points toward Alport syndrome. Red cell casts confirm a glomerular source of bleeding but do not stratify risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (Alport syndrome genetics; thin basement membrane nephropathy); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, family history and genetic stratification of hematuria).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 90% of males with X-linked Alport syndrome progress to ESRD by 40 years of age. COMLEX: formal audiometry and slit-lamp examination for anterior lenticonus belong in the evaluation of familial glomerular hematuria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — Alport progression ends in the same podocyte loss and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true}],"34":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common route by which bacteria reach the kidney in acute pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Hematogenous seeding from infective endocarditis","B":"Lymphatic spread from the intestinal tract","C":"Direct extension from the peritoneal cavity","D":"Retrograde spread from the seminal vesicles","E":"Ascent from the lower urinary tract"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ascending infection from the lower urinary tract is the most common cause of pyelonephritis, requiring colonization of the urethra, entry into the bladder, and movement up the ureter, usually in the presence of reflux or obstruction. The hematogenous route is less common and occurs with distant foci such as endocarditis. Spread from the peritoneal cavity or the seminal vesicles is not a recognized route; bacteria reach the kidney by ascent or, less often, the bloodstream.","boardPrep":"Step 1: most pyelonephritis is ascending from the bladder, so predisposing factors are reflux, obstruction, instrumentation, and pregnancy. COMLEX: infections are more frequent in females except in infancy and in older men with prostatic obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — bladder dysfunction and incomplete emptying predispose to ascending infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which organism causes the majority of urinary tract infections and pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Escherichia coli","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","D":"Candida albicans","E":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"More than 85 percent of urinary tract infections are caused by enteric gram-negative bacilli, and Escherichia coli is by far the most common, followed by Proteus, Klebsiella, and Enterobacter. Staphylococci and fungi are more often involved in hematogenous or immunosuppressed settings, and mycobacteria cause granulomatous disease. Pseudomonas is not among the common enteric causes, which after E. coli are Proteus, Klebsiella and Enterobacter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. coli uses P-fimbriae to adhere to urothelial cells. COMLEX: Proteus produces urease and is associated with struvite stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — bladder dysfunction and incomplete emptying predispose to ascending infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic finding is the hallmark of acute pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomatous interstitial inflammation with central caseation","B":"Interstitial eosinophils and mast cells with lymphocytic tubulitis","C":"Coagulative papillary tip necrosis with preserved tubule outlines","D":"Patchy suppurative interstitial inflammation with tubular neutrophils","E":"Coarse polar corticomedullary scars with thyroidization of tubules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hallmarks of acute pyelonephritis are patchy interstitial suppurative inflammation, intratubular aggregates of neutrophils, neutrophilic tubulitis, and tubular injury, sometimes forming abscesses or wedge-shaped areas. Glomeruli are relatively resistant. Granulomas suggest fungal or mycobacterial disease, eosinophils suggest hypersensitivity nephritis, and scars with thyroidization indicate chronic pyelonephritis. Coagulative necrosis of the papillary tips with preserved tubule outlines is papillary necrosis, a complication rather than the hallmark of acute pyelonephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neutrophil casts and cortical microabscesses point to bacterial pyelonephritis. COMLEX: white blood cell casts indicate renal, not bladder, involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — bladder dysfunction and incomplete emptying predispose to ascending infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":1,"stem":"Which gross finding characterizes chronic pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse fine granularity of the cortical surface","B":"Wedge-shaped pale infarcts with hemorrhagic borders","C":"Coarse corticomedullary scars overlying blunted calyces","D":"Bilaterally enlarged kidneys replaced by cysts","E":"Small smooth kidneys with pinpoint surface hemorrhages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chronic pyelonephritis produces coarse, discrete corticomedullary scars overlying dilated, blunted, or deformed calyces with flattening of the papillae, and if bilateral, the involvement is asymmetric. Most scars are in the upper and lower poles. Chronic glomerulonephritis instead scars both kidneys diffusely and symmetrically. Wedge-shaped pale infarcts with a hyperemic rim are the lesion of arterial occlusion, not of chronic infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pelvocalyceal damage with asymmetric scarring separates chronic pyelonephritis from glomerular disease. COMLEX: among the classic chronic tubulointerstitial diseases, chronic pyelonephritis and analgesic nephropathy involve the calyces.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — loss of nephron mass drives adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":1,"stem":"What is meant by thyroidization in chronic pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Follicular hyperplasia of lymphoid tissue within the interstitium","B":"Hyaline thickening of afferent arterioles with narrowed lumens","C":"Nodules of foamy macrophages mixed with plasma cells and giant cells","D":"Dilated atrophic tubules with colloid-like proteinaceous casts","E":"Lipid-laden proximal tubular cells with cytoplasmic vacuoles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thyroidization describes dilated tubules with flattened epithelium filled with proteinaceous casts that resemble thyroid colloid, a typical microscopic feature of chronic pyelonephritis. The other options describe lymphoid follicles, hyaline arteriolosclerosis, xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, and lipoid change in minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thyroidization of tubules plus corticomedullary scars is chronic pyelonephritis. COMLEX: glomeruli are relatively spared until late, when periglomerular fibrosis and secondary FSGS appear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — loss of nephron mass drives adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":1,"stem":"Why does scarring in chronic pyelonephritis favor the upper and lower renal regions?","choices":{"A":"Polar glomeruli carry a higher filtration fraction than midzone glomeruli","B":"Flattened or concave papillae there permit retrograde urine entry","C":"Polar arterioles lack collateral flow and infarct during infection","D":"Polar calyces are longer and hold more residual urine after voiding","E":"Polar cortex has a lower density of tubules and heals with scarring"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vesicoureteral reflux can propel infected urine deep into the parenchyma through open ducts at the papillary tips, called intrarenal reflux, and this is most common at the upper and lower poles where papillae tend to be flattened or concave rather than convex and pointed as in the midzone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary shape determines susceptibility to intrarenal reflux and hence polar scarring. COMLEX: voiding cystourethrography demonstrates reflux in a substantial minority of children with UTI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — loss of nephron mass drives adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":1,"stem":"Which group of conditions predisposes to renal papillary necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, obesity, and smoking","B":"Nephrotic syndrome, lupus, amyloidosis, and HIV infection","C":"Diabetes, sickle cell disease, obstruction, and analgesic abuse","D":"Polycystic kidney disease, tuberous sclerosis, and nephronophthisis","E":"Gout, hypercalcemia, hyperoxaluria, and cystinuria"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Papillary necrosis is seen mainly in diabetics, patients with sickle cell disease or trait, those with urinary tract obstruction, and in analgesic nephropathy. In each of these settings ischemia from compression or obstruction of small medullary vessels is the final common pathway. The other groups describe risk factors for vascular, glomerular, cystic, and stone diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the classic causes of papillary necrosis are diabetes, sickle cell disease, analgesics, and obstruction. COMLEX: necrotic papillae may be passed and cause colic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — medullary oxygen dynamics and prostaglandin buffers explain the vulnerability of the papillae","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":1,"stem":"What is the pathogenic mechanism of papillary necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Ischemia from small medullary vessel obstruction","B":"Direct bacterial invasion of the papillary tips","C":"Immune complex deposition in papillary capillaries","D":"Toxin-induced necrosis of the proximal tubule epithelium","E":"Thrombosis of the main renal artery with cortical infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Papillary necrosis is caused by ischemia resulting from compression or obstruction of small blood vessels in the medulla. Compression may result from interstitial edema, as in inflammation and obstruction, and vascular compromise from microvascular disease, as in diabetes. Main renal artery thrombosis infarcts the cortex rather than selectively the papillae.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the papillae are the most poorly perfused region of the kidney. COMLEX: necrosis is usually bilateral but may be unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — medullary oxygen dynamics and prostaglandin buffers explain the vulnerability of the papillae","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":1,"stem":"Which microscopic finding characterizes renal papillary necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Caseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells","B":"Eosinophilic infiltrate with lymphocytic tubulitis","C":"Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles with onion-skin thickening","D":"Neutrophil casts with cortical microabscesses","E":"Ischemic coagulative necrosis with preserved outlines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"On microscopic examination, necrotic papillary tissue shows ischemic coagulative necrosis with preservation of the outlines of tubules, and the leukocytic response is limited to the junctions between preserved and destroyed tissue. The other options describe tuberculosis, malignant nephrosclerosis, acute pyelonephritis, and drug-induced interstitial nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: coagulative necrosis is the pattern of ischemic infarcts in solid organs. COMLEX: gross examination shows gray-white to yellow necrosis of the distal two-thirds of the pyramids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — medullary oxygen dynamics and prostaglandin buffers explain the vulnerability of the papillae","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has her second febrile urinary tract infection. A voiding cystourethrogram shows dye refluxing into both ureters and filling the calyces. Which anatomic defect most likely explains her repeated infections?","choices":{"A":"Absent ureteral peristalsis from a neurogenic bladder disorder","B":"Urethral stricture causing high-pressure bladder outflow obstruction","C":"Short intravesical ureter that is not compressed on micturition","D":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction with unilateral pelvic dilation","E":"A vesicovaginal fistula draining bladder urine into the vagina"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The normal ureteral insertion into the bladder acts as a one-way valve that is compressed when intravesical pressure rises during micturition. Reflux most often results from congenital absence or shortening of the intravesical portion of the ureter, allowing bladder urine to flow back into the ureters and carry bacteria to the renal pelvis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vesicoureteral reflux is the leading cause of pyelonephritis in children and it affects 1 to 2 percent of normal children. COMLEX: reflux is demonstrated by voiding cystourethrography.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — impaired emptying and reflux predispose to infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has fever, dysuria, and costovertebral angle tenderness. Which urinary finding best indicates that the infection involves the kidney rather than the bladder?","choices":{"A":"White blood cell casts","B":"Pyuria on microscopy","C":"Bacteriuria on culture","D":"A positive nitrite dipstick","E":"Microscopic hematuria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pyuria does not differentiate upper from lower urinary tract infection, but leukocyte casts, typically rich in neutrophils, form only in the tubules and therefore indicate renal involvement. Bacteriuria, nitrite positivity, and hematuria can all occur with cystitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: casts form only in renal tubules, so any cast localizes disease to the kidney. COMLEX: white blood cell casts point to pyelonephritis or interstitial nephritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — lower tract disease versus upper tract disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with long-standing diabetes develops recurrent pyelonephritis. Which factor most directly promotes ascent of bacteria in his case?","choices":{"A":"Increased antibacterial activity of prostatic fluid","B":"Hyperfiltration that flushes bacteria from the tubules","C":"A longer urethra than in females that traps bacteria","D":"Neurogenic bladder with residual urine after voiding","E":"Alkaline urine from glucosuria that inhibits growth"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diabetes predisposes to infection through impaired host defenses, neurogenic bladder dysfunction that causes incomplete emptying and residual urine, and more frequent instrumentation. Stasis allows bacteria introduced into the bladder to multiply unhindered and ascend, particularly if reflux or obstruction is present. The antibacterial activity of prostatic fluid protects against infection rather than promoting it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: residual urine favors bacterial growth in any obstruction or bladder atony. COMLEX: diabetic patients are also prone to papillary necrosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — neurogenic bladder and incomplete emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with reflux and recurrent childhood urinary infections now has hypertension, asymmetrically small kidneys, and new proteinuria of 3 g/day despite no active infection. Which mechanism best explains the proteinuria?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing suppurative infection that is destroying glomeruli","B":"Anti-GBM antibodies produced after scarring exposes new antigens","C":"Secondary FSGS from adaptive hyperfiltration after nephron loss","D":"Subepithelial immune complexes formed against bacterial antigens","E":"Mesangial IgA deposition triggered by recurrent mucosal infection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Some individuals with pyelonephritic scars develop secondary FSGS with significant proteinuria, even in the nephrotic range, usually several years after scarring and often without ongoing infection or persistent reflux. The glomerulosclerosis reflects adaptive glomerular alterations secondary to loss of renal mass, and the onset of proteinuria is a poor prognostic sign. Subepithelial immune complexes describe membranous or postinfectious disease, not the adaptive sclerosis that follows nephron loss in a scarred kidney.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reflux nephropathy is a classic cause of adaptive FSGS. COMLEX: proteinuria in a patient with scarred kidneys signals progression toward ESRD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with long-standing kidney disease dies, and her autopsy shows tubulointerstitial scarring with damage to the calyces and pelvis. Which pair of diagnoses best fits this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis and minimal change disease","B":"Acute tubular injury and acute interstitial nephritis","C":"IgA nephropathy and membranous nephropathy","D":"Hypertensive nephrosclerosis and Alport syndrome","E":"Chronic pyelonephritis and analgesic nephropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Although several diseases produce chronic tubulointerstitial alterations, chronic pyelonephritis and analgesic nephropathy are the classic pair that affect the calyces, making pelvocalyceal damage an important diagnostic clue. The other pairs include diseases that do not involve the calyces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calyceal involvement is the discriminating feature of these two diseases. COMLEX: analgesic nephropathy also causes papillary necrosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 3 — papillary necrosis from medullary ischemia is the pelvicalyceal lesion of analgesic nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old with reflux nephropathy has polyuria, nocturia, and hypertension. Which additional finding is the poorest prognostic sign for progression to end-stage disease?","choices":{"A":"Persistent leukocyturia","B":"Occasional flank discomfort","C":"A single small polar scar in one kidney","D":"New onset of proteinuria","E":"Bacteriuria on culture"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic pyelonephritis, proteinuria is usually mild, but in some patients secondary FSGS develops, and the onset of proteinuria is a poor prognostic sign since it indicates an increased likelihood of progression to ESRD. Leukocyturia, bacteriuria, flank discomfort, and a single scar are manifestations or markers of the disease but do not predict progression in the same way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteinuria signals secondary FSGS in scarred kidneys. COMLEX: loss of tubular concentrating ability produces polyuria and nocturia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — podocyte injury and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old diabetic woman with acute pyelonephritis develops gross hematuria, flank colic, and a sudden rise in creatinine. She passes gray-white tissue fragments in her urine. Which complication has occurred?","choices":{"A":"Pyonephrosis of the collecting system","B":"Renal papillary necrosis","C":"Perinephric abscess in the fat","D":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","E":"Renal vein thrombosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sloughed necrotic papillae can be excreted and cause gross hematuria or renal colic if they obstruct the ureter. Papillary necrosis is a complication of acute pyelonephritis in diabetics, and its superimposition may lead to acute renal failure. Pyonephrosis and perinephric abscess are suppurative complications, and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis is a rare chronic form.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sloughed papillae with hematuria and colic in a diabetic patient means papillary necrosis. COMLEX: it is usually bilateral.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — medullary ischemia and susceptibility to injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with sickle cell trait has painless gross hematuria. Which explanation best accounts for the vulnerability of the renal medulla?","choices":{"A":"High oxygen tension drives free radical injury of medullary capillaries","B":"Rapid blood flow through the vasa recta damages sickled cells","C":"Alkaline medullary pH prolongs the survival of sickled red cells","D":"Hypoxia and hypertonicity promote sickling and vessel occlusion","E":"Dilute interstitial fluid causes red cell lysis within the vasa recta"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The medulla has low oxygen tension and a hypertonic environment, conditions that promote red cell sickling even in sickle cell trait, and sickled cells obstruct the slow-flowing small vessels of the medulla. This causes ischemia and papillary necrosis, which may present with hematuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the medulla is uniquely hypoxic and hypertonic, so sickle cell trait can cause hematuria and papillary necrosis. COMLEX: unlike other tissues, sickling in the medulla occurs even in the trait.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — low medullary oxygen tension and susceptibility to ischemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with years of daily use of an aspirin-containing analgesic has sterile pyuria and radiographs suggesting papillary necrosis. Which mechanism best links her drug exposure to the papillary injury?","choices":{"A":"Loss of vasodilatory prostaglandins worsens medullary ischemia","B":"Direct immune complex deposition in the papillary capillaries","C":"Gram-negative infection of the papillae from the bladder","D":"Uric acid crystal obstruction of the collecting ducts","E":"Osmotic swelling of tubular cells that compresses vessels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Many nonsteroidal analgesics inhibit cyclooxygenase-dependent prostaglandin synthesis, which removes vasodilatory prostaglandins that protect medullary blood flow, and chronic analgesic use damages the papillae by ischemia. Papillary necrosis results from ischemia due to compression or obstruction of small medullary vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: analgesic nephropathy causes chronic interstitial nephritis and papillary necrosis, and it affects the calyces. COMLEX: sterile pyuria and hematuria are clues in chronic analgesic users.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — prostaglandins protect medullary flow and NSAIDs remove this buffer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl34_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man who injects drugs has 2 weeks of fever and a new heart murmur, and blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. He has bilateral flank tenderness without dysuria, and CT shows small abscesses scattered through both renal cortices. By which route did the bacteria most likely reach his kidneys?","choices":{"A":"Ascent of urethral coliforms into the bladder","B":"Retrograde flow through an incompetent ureter","C":"Hematogenous seeding from an infected cardiac focus","D":"Direct spread from an infected perinephric space","E":"Lymphatic spread from adjacent pelvic lymph nodes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The two routes described for renal infection are ascent from the lower urinary tract or the bloodstream, and the less common bloodstream route results from seeding by bacteria from distant foci such as infective endocarditis; bilateral cortical abscesses with Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia and a quiet lower tract fit this route. Ascending infection begins with colonization of the distal urethra by coliform bacteria and entry into the bladder, often with instrumentation, none of which is present. Retrograde flow through an incompetent ureterovesical junction carries infected bladder urine upward, an ascending pattern that does not fit bacteremia with bilateral cortical abscesses. A perinephric abscess forms when suppuration extends outward through the renal capsule, the opposite direction. Lymphatic spread is not one of the two routes described. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (pathways of renal infection, hematogenous seeding); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, conversion from cystitis to pyelonephritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ascending infection by enteric gram-negative rods, most often E. coli, is by far the most common route, so a staphylococcal pattern of bilateral cortical abscesses should prompt a search for a bloodstream source. COMLEX: hematogenous renal infection is more likely with ureteral obstruction and in debilitated patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1 — renal infection is the neutrophilic, culture-positive contrast to drug-induced hypersensitivity nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl34_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman has 3 days of dysuria and frequency, followed by fever and right flank pain. Urinalysis shows many neutrophils and gram-negative rods, and culture grows Escherichia coli. Which additional urine finding would most specifically show that the infection involves the kidney?","choices":{"A":"Positive nitrite on the dipstick","B":"Leukocyte esterase on the dipstick","C":"Squamous cells in the urine sediment","D":"Gross blood in the voided urine","E":"Leukocyte casts in the sediment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Neutrophils that reach the renal tubules become trapped in a matrix of uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) secreted by tubular cells, forming leukocyte (pus) casts; because cellular casts can form only within renal tubules, their presence in the urine is the diagnostic hallmark of pyelonephritis rather than cystitis. A positive nitrite shows gram-negative bacteriuria anywhere in the tract. Leukocyte esterase shows pyuria anywhere in the tract. Squamous cells indicate contamination of the sample. Gross hematuria occurs with hemorrhagic cystitis and does not localize infection to the kidney. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, neutrophilic cast formation in acute pyelonephritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: White cell casts localize a urinary infection to the kidney, since casts form only in tubules. COMLEX: Flank pain, fever, and costovertebral angle tenderness with white cell casts call for treatment as pyelonephritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1 — neutrophil casts and a positive culture separate pyelonephritis from drug-induced interstitial nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl34_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has hypertension, nocturia, and a creatinine of 1.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). She had several febrile urinary infections in early childhood. Imaging shows asymmetric contracted kidneys with coarse scars over blunted calyces at the upper and lower poles, sparing the midzones. Which abnormality best explains this scar distribution?","choices":{"A":"Urate crystal deposition in the collecting ducts","B":"Intrarenal reflux through polar compound papillae","C":"Accelerated red cell sickling in the hypoxic medulla","D":"Main renal artery narrowing with diffuse atrophy","E":"Calcium deposition from chronic hypercalcemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vesicoureteral reflux propels infected bladder urine into the renal pelvis, and at the upper and lower poles compound papillae have open ducts that permit intrarenal reflux deep into the parenchyma, so recurrent childhood infection leaves asymmetric coarse polar scars over blunted, deformed calyces; nocturia reflects the early loss of concentrating ability. Urate deposition places crystals in the collecting ducts rather than producing polar scars over calyces. Sickling in the hypoxic medulla causes hyposthenuria and patchy papillary necrosis, not polar cortical scars. Main renal artery narrowing makes one kidney diffusely atrophic rather than focally scarred at the poles. Hypercalcemia causes nephrocalcinosis and stones but not coarse polar scars. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, polar scarring through compound papillae and impaired concentrating ability).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyelonephritic scars favor the poles because papillae there have flattened or concave tips that allow intrarenal reflux, whereas midzone convex papillae resist it. COMLEX: reflux nephropathy is a classic hidden cause of hypertension in children, and polyuria with nocturia reflects early loss of concentrating ability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — nephron loss from reflux scarring drives the hyperfiltration of the common pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl34_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":3,"stem":"A pathologist examines an end-stage nephrectomy specimen from a 41-year-old man with recurrent febrile urinary infections in childhood and no analgesic exposure; his creatinine before dialysis was 6.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). She wants the single finding that best separates infection-related scarring from end-stage scarring of glomerular, vascular, or other tubulointerstitial origin. Which finding best serves this purpose?","choices":{"A":"Deformed calyces beneath the cortical scars","B":"Interstitial fibrosis with tubular atrophy","C":"Global sclerosis of scattered glomeruli","D":"Hyaline thickening of afferent arterioles","E":"Chronic lymphocytic interstitial infiltrate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Among chronic tubulointerstitial diseases, calyceal deformity points chiefly to chronic pyelonephritis or analgesic nephropathy, so scars overlying dilated, blunted, or deformed calyces are the key diagnostic clue, and analgesic exposure has been excluded. Interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy belong to the nonspecific constellation of chronic kidney disease from any glomerular, tubulointerstitial, or vascular injury. Glomerulosclerosis develops after many types of renal injury, even when the primary disease is nonglomerular. Hyaline arteriolar thickening is the lesion of nephrosclerosis, which tracks with hypertension and advanced age, and does not point to an infectious process. A chronic lymphocytic infiltrate accompanies pyelonephritic scars but also many other chronic tubulointerstitial diseases. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (chronic pyelonephritis, pelvocalyceal damage as a diagnostic clue; chronic kidney disease); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, deformation of the pelvocalyceal system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: all chronic kidney disease converges on glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and arteriosclerosis, so a site-specific change is needed to name the original disease. COMLEX: radiographs showing asymmetric contracted kidneys with coarse scars and deformed calyces support the diagnosis without tissue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1 — benign nephrosclerosis produces diffuse fine granular scarring rather than coarse focal scars","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl34_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with 20 years of poorly controlled diabetes is treated for acute pyelonephritis. She develops gross hematuria and colic and passes gray-white tissue fragments, and CT shows cavities at the tips of several pyramids. Which factor most directly predisposes her medulla to this injury?","choices":{"A":"Prostaglandin loss from chronic analgesic use","B":"Sickling of red cells in the hypertonic medulla","C":"Urate crystal obstruction of collecting ducts","D":"Microvascular disease limiting medullary flow","E":"Immune complex deposition in medullary capillaries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Necrosis of the pyramid tips with sloughed papillae is papillary necrosis, which is caused chiefly by ischemia of the medulla; in diabetes the vascular compromise comes from microvascular disease, and diabetics with superimposed pyelonephritis are among the classic patients affected. Prostaglandin loss from analgesics causes medullary vasoconstriction, but her history points to diabetic microangiopathy instead. Sickling in the hypoxic, hypertonic medulla affects patients with sickle cell disease or trait, which she is not described as having. Urate crystals obstruct collecting ducts in urate nephropathy but do not explain ischemic necrosis of the pyramid tips. Immune complex deposition is a mechanism of glomerular disease and is not implicated in papillary necrosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (papillary necrosis in diabetes and its ischemic basis); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, medullary vascular vulnerability in diabetes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: causes of papillary necrosis include diabetes, sickle cell disease or trait, analgesics, obstruction, and acute pyelonephritis. COMLEX: a diabetic with pyelonephritis who passes tissue and develops colic and rising creatinine needs urgent imaging for sloughed papillae.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — the medulla's low oxygen tension makes it intolerant of reduced blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl34_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has had a stone impacted in the right ureter for 6 days with fever and an inflamed, obstructed collecting system. He then passes a gray-white tissue fragment with gross hematuria, and his creatinine rises to 2.3 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He takes no analgesics. Which mechanism best explains injury to his right medullary pyramid tips?","choices":{"A":"Prostaglandin loss from chronic analgesic use","B":"Amyloid deposition in medullary vessels","C":"Cholesterol emboli lodged in arcuate arteries","D":"Light-chain casts in the collecting ducts","E":"Pelvic pressure compressing medullary vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The sloughed papillary tissue reflects ischemic necrosis of the papillae. In urinary tract obstruction, raised intrapelvic pressure compresses the delicate medullary vasa recta, and stagnant suppurative exudate adds to the ischemia, so the pyramid tips of the obstructed kidney necrose. Prostaglandin loss from analgesics is excluded by his history. Amyloid deposits in vessel walls and glomeruli in systemic amyloidosis, not acutely after obstruction. Cholesterol emboli lodge in arcuate and interlobular arteries after aortic procedures and are not a feature of an obstructed, infected kidney. Light-chain casts obstruct distal tubules in myeloma kidney and do not explain papillary necrosis in an obstructed, infected kidney. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, urinary tract obstruction and medullary vascular compression); Robbins Ch. 20 (papillary necrosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sloughed papillae can obstruct the ureter and cause gross hematuria and renal colic. COMLEX: relieve obstruction promptly in an infected kidney, because papillary necrosis superimposed on pyelonephritis or obstruction can trigger acute renal failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — raised pressure behind an obstruction compresses the renal parenchyma and its vessels","isHighYield":true}],"35":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which group of drugs most commonly causes acute interstitial nephritis?","choices":{"A":"Aminoglycosides, radiographic contrast, cisplatin, and amphotericin B","B":"Statins, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, and nitrates","C":"Heparin, warfarin, aspirin, and clopidogrel","D":"Synthetic penicillins, rifampin, thiazides, and NSAIDs","E":"Levothyroxine, insulin, metformin, and glipizide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute interstitial nephritis most frequently occurs with sulfonamides, synthetic penicillins such as methicillin and ampicillin, rifampin, thiazide diuretics, NSAIDs, and drugs such as allopurinol, cimetidine, proton pump inhibitors, and immune checkpoint inhibitors. Aminoglycosides, contrast agents, and cisplatin typically cause acute tubular injury instead. The cardiovascular, antithrombotic and diabetes or thyroid drug groups are not the classic culprit classes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: antibiotics, diuretics, and NSAIDs are the classic culprits, and they may present with rash, fever, and eosinophilia, although the full triad is seen in a minority. COMLEX: withdrawal of the drug is followed by recovery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — suppurative pyelonephritis is the infectious contrast to this hypersensitivity interstitial disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":1,"stem":"When does antibiotic-induced acute interstitial nephritis typically begin after drug exposure?","choices":{"A":"Within minutes of the first dose","B":"Six to twelve hours after exposure","C":"Two to 40 days after exposure","D":"Two to three weeks after stopping the drug","E":"Two to three years after exposure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis begins 2 to 40 days after drug exposure, a latent period consistent with a hypersensitivity reaction. Onset is not dose-related, and recurrence follows re-exposure to the same or a chemically related drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the latent period, rash, eosinophilia, and lack of dose relationship all point to hypersensitivity. COMLEX: the triad of fever, rash, and eosinophilia is present in a minority of patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — suppurative pyelonephritis is the infectious contrast to this hypersensitivity interstitial disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most likely immunologic mechanism of drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis?","choices":{"A":"Drug binding modifies tubular antigens and provokes hypersensitivity","B":"Osmotic injury from drug crystals precipitating in the tubule lumen","C":"Complement lysis triggered by anti-GBM autoantibodies","D":"Bacterial invasion of the renal interstitium from the bladder","E":"Ischemia from sustained constriction of the afferent arteriole"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The most likely sequence is that drugs bind to a plasma membrane or extracellular component of tubular cells and create modified self antigens that evoke an immune response, through IgE-mediated or T cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at the tubular cells or their basement membranes. Crystal precipitation is a direct, non-immune injury, and anti-GBM antibodies, bacterial ascent and afferent constriction are the mechanisms of anti-GBM disease, pyelonephritis and ischemic tubular injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hapten mechanism means the drug alters self proteins so that the immune system attacks them. COMLEX: the response can be type I or type IV hypersensitivity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — suppurative pyelonephritis is the infectious contrast to this hypersensitivity interstitial disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"Which nephron segments are especially vulnerable to acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular capillary loops and Bowman capsule epithelium","B":"Proximal straight tubule and thick ascending limb","C":"Cortical collecting duct and the papillary tip urothelium","D":"Early distal convoluted tubule and macula densa cells","E":"Thin descending limb and the ascending vasa recta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The straight portion of the proximal tubule and the thick ascending limb in the outer medulla are especially vulnerable, because they have high metabolic and oxygen demands from active transport and receive limited oxygen delivery. Glomeruli and collecting ducts are relatively resistant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high transport activity and low medullary oxygen tension make these segments the most susceptible. COMLEX: injury is patchy with skip areas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in the thick ascending limb explains its susceptibility to ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"Which morphologic pattern characterizes ischemic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Extensive necrosis along the proximal convoluted tubule","B":"Uniform necrosis of the glomerular capillary loops","C":"Wedge-shaped cortical infarcts with preserved tubule outlines","D":"Patchy necrosis of short tubular segments with skip areas","E":"Diffuse tubular atrophy with interstitial fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In ischemic ATI, tubular necrosis is patchy and affects relatively short lengths of tubule, with large skip areas, most often in the proximal straight segments and thick ascending limbs. Toxic ATI instead produces extensive necrosis along the proximal convoluted tubule with many toxins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic ATI is patchy and toxic ATI is diffuse in the PCT. COMLEX: toxic ATI typically spares the tubular basement membrane, whereas ischemic ATI can show focal basement membrane disruption, and both regenerate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in the thick ascending limb explains its susceptibility to ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is a well-recognized cause of nephrotoxic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Gentamicin","B":"Amlodipine","C":"Metoprolol","D":"Levothyroxine","E":"Ondansetron"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aminoglycosides such as gentamicin, radiographic contrast agents, heavy metals, and organic solvents are classic causes of nephrotoxic ATI, in which proximal tubular cells are injured because they reabsorb and concentrate the toxin. The other agents are not recognized tubular toxins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aminoglycoside toxicity is dose dependent and shows muddy brown casts. COMLEX: monitor peak and trough levels and creatinine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in the thick ascending limb explains its susceptibility to ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which are the three clinical phases of classic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Prerenal, intrinsic, and postrenal stages","B":"Oliguric, anuric, and polyuric stages","C":"Acute, subacute, and chronic stages","D":"Injury, fibrosis, and scarring stages","E":"Initiation, maintenance, and recovery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The classic course of ATI is divided into an initiation phase of about 36 hours dominated by the inciting event, a maintenance phase with sustained oliguria and uremic complications, and a recovery phase heralded by a steady rise in urine volume. The other sets describe etiologic categories or unrelated staging systems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the phases and the hazards of each. COMLEX: most survivors of the precipitating event recover substantial function, but some are left with chronic kidney disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback links tubular injury to the depressed GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"What urine output characterizes the maintenance phase of classic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Polyuria of 2 to 3 L per day","B":"Normal output of 1 to 1.5 L per day","C":"Oliguria of 40 to 400 mL per day","D":"Anuria of less than 10 mL per day","E":"Nocturia with 800 to 1000 mL per day"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The maintenance phase is characterized by sustained oliguria of 40 to 400 mL per day with salt and water overload, rising BUN, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and other manifestations of uremia. The recovery phase is marked by polyuria that can reach up to 3 L per day. Normal output, nocturia with near-normal volumes, and anuria below 10 mL/day are not the described pattern, which is sustained oliguria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: oliguria in the maintenance phase and polyuria in recovery. COMLEX: hyperkalemia is the most immediately dangerous complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback links tubular injury to the depressed GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism helps maintain a depressed GFR during the maintenance phase of acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Restored polarity of tubular cells increases proximal reabsorption","B":"Sloughed cells obstruct tubules and raise intratubular pressure","C":"Dilation of afferent arterioles reduces glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Increased plasma oncotic pressure raises the filtration coefficient","E":"Loss of ureteral peristalsis distends the renal pelvis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Injured cells detach from their basement membranes and cause luminal obstruction, raising intratubular pressure and decreasing GFR, and filtrate can also leak back through damaged tubular epithelium into the interstitium, causing edema and ischemic damage. Tubuloglomerular feedback from increased distal sodium delivery adds afferent constriction. Ureteral peristalsis is not involved; the obstruction is inside the tubules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: casts, backleak, and vasoconstriction combine to sustain oliguria. COMLEX: muddy brown granular casts are the urinary hallmark.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback links tubular injury to the depressed GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man treated with ampicillin ten days ago develops fever, a rash, and a rising creatinine. Urinalysis shows eosinophiluria and mild proteinuria. Which biopsy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil casts with cortical microabscesses and normal glomeruli","B":"Interstitial edema with mononuclear and eosinophil infiltrate","C":"Diffuse foot process effacement with a normal interstitium","D":"Subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes on silver stain","E":"Granular capillary wall IgG and C3 with glomerular hypercellularity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fever, rash, and eosinophiluria beginning days after starting a synthetic penicillin indicate drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis. The interstitium shows pronounced edema and infiltration by lymphocytes and macrophages, with eosinophils, and lymphocytic infiltration of tubules called tubulitis. Glomeruli are usually normal. Subepithelial deposits with spikes are membranous nephropathy, a glomerular lesion, whereas glomeruli are usually normal in this drug reaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils and tubulitis on biopsy point to a hypersensitivity interstitial nephritis. COMLEX: the treatment is to stop the drug, with steroids sometimes added.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — contrast with the neutrophilic suppurative inflammation of pyelonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has a rising creatinine and pyuria. Which finding favors drug-induced interstitial nephritis over acute pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophiluria with rash and sterile culture","B":"Neutrophil casts with a positive urine culture","C":"Costovertebral angle tenderness with bacteriuria","D":"Nitrite-positive urine with pus casts","E":"Fever with pyuria and gram-negative bacteremia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis is a hypersensitivity reaction with T cells and eosinophils, rash, fever, and eosinophiluria, and the urine culture is sterile because no infection is present. Pyelonephritis is a suppurative bacterial infection with neutrophil casts, bacteriuria, and a positive urine culture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sterile pyuria with eosinophils suggests interstitial nephritis. COMLEX: a positive culture with neutrophil casts and flank pain suggests pyelonephritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — ascending bacterial pyelonephritis gives neutrophil casts and positive cultures, the findings absent in drug-induced AIN","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man develops fever, a rash, and a rising creatinine 12 days after starting ampicillin. Urinalysis shows leukocytes, including eosinophils, and mild proteinuria. His serum IgE is elevated, and a kidney biopsy shows interstitial edema with mononuclear cells and eosinophils, IgE-containing plasma cells, and basophils, without granulomas. Which immune mechanism do these findings most support?","choices":{"A":"Direct dose-dependent toxicity to tubular epithelial cells","B":"Antibodies against the native tubular basement membrane","C":"Late phase of an IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity","D":"Circulating immune complexes trapped in the glomeruli","E":"Granulomatous T cell-mediated type IV hypersensitivity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins notes that in some patients with drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis, serum IgE is increased and IgE-containing plasma cells and basophils are present in the lesions, suggesting the late-phase reaction of an IgE-mediated (type I) hypersensitivity; drugs binding tubular cell components create modified self antigens that provoke the response. The injury is not dose-related and recurs on re-exposure, arguing against direct toxicity. The antigens are drug-modified, not native basement membrane components. The glomeruli are normal in drug-induced interstitial nephritis, apart from some NSAID cases with minimal change disease. A mononuclear or granulomatous reaction with positive drug skin tests suggests the type IV pattern instead, and this biopsy has no granulomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis begins 2–40 days after exposure with fever, eosinophilia, rash, and renal abnormalities; mechanisms are IgE-mediated (type I late phase) or T cell-mediated (type IV, granulomas with methicillin or thiazides). COMLEX: Withdrawing the drug leads to recovery, although it may take several months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — drugs can also injure tubules directly as acute tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has prolonged hypotension during cardiac surgery. Which early ischemic change in tubular cells contributes to his falling GFR?","choices":{"A":"Loss of polarity with pump redistribution raising distal Na+ delivery","B":"Greater proximal Na+ reabsorption that lowers macula densa delivery","C":"Afferent arteriolar dilation from a surge of prostacyclin release","D":"Rapid hyperfiltration from a rising ultrafiltration coefficient","E":"Myogenic afferent dilation from lowered arterial pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"One early reversible result of ischemia is loss of cell polarity, with redistribution of Na+/K+-ATPase from the basolateral to the luminal surface, causing abnormal ion transport and increased sodium delivery to the distal tubule, which incites afferent vasoconstriction through tubuloglomerular feedback and lowers GFR. Ischemia is thought to reduce rather than raise the glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient, so hyperfiltration does not occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemia disrupts the tubular cytoskeleton and pump polarity. COMLEX: this early phase is reversible if perfusion is restored.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — active transport, oxygen consumption, and ischemic susceptibility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man develops acute kidney injury after ingesting antifreeze. Which biopsy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Large acidophilic inclusions in proximal tubule cells that later calcify","B":"Neutrophil casts with cortical microabscesses and interstitial edema","C":"Wedge-shaped cortical infarcts with preservation of tubular outlines","D":"Eosinophils and lymphocytes infiltrating the interstitium with tubulitis","E":"Vacuolar degeneration of proximal tubules with oxalate crystals in lumens"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ethylene glycol poisoning produces marked ballooning and hydropic or vacuolar degeneration of proximal convoluted tubules, and calcium oxalate crystals are often found in the tubular lumens, an example of toxic acute tubular injury. Acidophilic inclusions occur with mercuric chloride, and the other options describe pyelonephritis, infarction, and interstitial nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ethylene glycol produces calcium oxalate crystals and a high anion gap, high osmolar gap metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: fomepizole is the antidote.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — proximal tubule brush border and high mitochondrial density","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man develops acute kidney injury after aminoglycoside therapy. Which set of proximal tubule properties best explains its vulnerability to this drug?","choices":{"A":"Small surface area with passive transport and rapid toxin excretion","B":"Absent transport proteins with low metabolism and high oxygen supply","C":"Thick basement membrane with low oxygen consumption and slow flow","D":"Large reabsorptive surface with active transport concentrating toxins","E":"Impermeability to solutes with high tonicity and abundant antioxidants"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Several factors predispose proximal tubular cells to toxic injury, including an increased surface area for tubular reabsorption, active transport systems for ions and organic acids, and the ability to absorb and concentrate toxins. High metabolic and oxygen demand also make them sensitive to ischemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal tubule is the primary target of nephrotoxins because it reabsorbs and concentrates them. COMLEX: hydration and dose adjustment reduce aminoglycoside toxicity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in transporting segments","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man is on day 12 of acute tubular injury after septic shock. His urine output is 3 L per day, his BUN is falling, serum Na+ is 128 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and K+ is 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which hazard is most consistent with this phase?","choices":{"A":"Volume overload and hyperkalemia from persistent oliguria","B":"Volume depletion with hypokalemia from urinary loss of solutes and water","C":"Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis from impaired acid excretion","D":"Worsening uremic pericarditis from a rapidly rising BUN and creatinine","E":"Severe hypertension from excessive renin release by ischemic cortex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The recovery phase brings a steady increase in urine volume, but the tubules are still damaged, so large amounts of water, sodium, and potassium are lost in the flood of urine, causing volume depletion, hyponatremia, and hypokalemia, and patients have a peculiar vulnerability to infection. Oliguria, hyperkalemia, and rising BUN belong to the maintenance phase. Renin-driven severe hypertension is not a described hazard of this phase, which is dominated by water and electrolyte losses and infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: polyuria of recovery demands careful replacement of fluid and electrolytes. COMLEX: watch for infection during this phase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — high distal flow and Na+ delivery in the polyuric recovery phase drive principal cell K+ secretion, causing hypokalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man is in the maintenance phase of acute tubular injury after cardiac surgery, with a urine output of 250 mL per day. Which laboratory pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Falling BUN with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis","B":"Falling creatinine with hypernatremia and dilute urine","C":"Rising BUN with hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis","D":"Rising BUN with hypokalemia and respiratory alkalosis","E":"Stable creatinine with hyperkalemia and normal bicarbonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The maintenance phase is characterized by sustained oliguria with salt and water overload, rising BUN concentrations, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and other manifestations of uremia. Hypokalemia and dilute urine characterize the recovery phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced GFR retains potassium, acid, and nitrogenous waste. COMLEX: hyperkalemia may require urgent treatment or dialysis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — intrinsic renal azotemia from ATN raises BUN and creatinine together (ratio below 15:1), unlike prerenal azotemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man develops ischemic acute tubular injury after prolonged hypotension during surgery, and his GFR falls sharply. Which mechanism links his tubular damage to the depressed GFR through tubuloglomerular feedback?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced NaCl reabsorption lowers distal delivery and dilates the afferent arteriole","B":"Direct tubular renin secretion lets angiotensin II dilate the efferent arteriole","C":"Tubular nitric oxide release causes vasodilation and lowers glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Poor NaCl reabsorption raises distal delivery and constricts the afferent arteriole","E":"Raised Bowman space pressure from tubular injury lowers the filtration coefficient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Defective sodium reabsorption in injured tubules increases the concentration of sodium reaching the macula densa, which causes constriction of the afferent arteriole through tubuloglomerular feedback and decreases GFR; cast obstruction and backleak add to the reduction. Raised Bowman space pressure from obstruction lowers GFR by opposing filtration, not through tubuloglomerular feedback or a change in the filtration coefficient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in ATI, high macula densa NaCl delivery triggers tubuloglomerular feedback that constricts the afferent arteriole and lowers GFR. COMLEX: cast obstruction and backleak of filtrate add to the fall in GFR.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback from macula densa sensing to afferent constriction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl35_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman develops fever and a creatinine of 2.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) 3 weeks after starting hydrochlorothiazide. Her serum IgE is normal, and a drug patch test is positive. Biopsy shows lymphocytes, macrophages, tubulitis, and nonnecrotizing granulomas, with normal glomeruli. Which mechanism best explains her renal injury?","choices":{"A":"Delayed-type T cell response to altered self antigens","B":"IgE-triggered late-phase mast cell and basophil reaction","C":"Direct dose-related toxicity to tubular epithelium","D":"Granular immune complex deposits in the glomeruli","E":"Uncontrolled alternative complement pathway activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The drug most likely binds a plasma membrane or extracellular component of tubular cells and creates a modified self antigen, and a mononuclear or granulomatous reaction together with a positive drug skin test read days later points to a T cell-mediated, type IV, delayed-type hypersensitivity response; thiazides are among the drugs that can produce interstitial nonnecrotizing granulomas. An IgE-mediated late-phase reaction is suggested by increased serum IgE with IgE-containing plasma cells and basophils in the lesions, but her IgE is normal. Direct toxicity produces acute tubular injury, whereas the latent period and lack of dose relationship typical of this disease favor an immune mechanism. Immune complexes, which can deposit in glomeruli or along tubular basement membranes, would show a granular pattern of deposition on immunofluorescence and do not explain granulomas with a late-read skin test. Alternative complement overactivation drives atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome with microvascular thrombi, not a granulomatous interstitial infiltrate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (pathogenesis and morphology of drug-induced interstitial nephritis); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, type IV and type I hypersensitivity mechanisms).","boardPrep":"Step 1: drug-induced interstitial nephritis can reflect either a late-phase IgE reaction or T cell-mediated delayed-type hypersensitivity, and granulomas with a positive drug patch test tip the balance to type IV. COMLEX: withdrawal of the offending drug is followed by recovery, though it may take several months and irreversible damage can occur.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 2 — complement dysregulation injures endothelium in TMA rather than producing an interstitial infiltrate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl35_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with a neurogenic bladder started rifampin 10 days ago and now has fever, left flank tenderness, no rash, and creatinine of 2.3 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Urine shows many neutrophils, neutrophil-rich leukocyte casts, and no eosinophils, and urine culture grows Escherichia coli. Which process best explains his kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Hapten-driven hypersensitivity to the new drug","B":"Nephrotoxic necrosis of tubular epithelial cells","C":"Ascending bacterial infection from incomplete voiding","D":"Coagulative necrosis of the medullary pyramid tips","E":"Light-chain casts obstructing the distal tubules"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Neurogenic bladder dysfunction leaves residual urine in which bacteria multiply and ascend, neutrophil-rich leukocyte casts localize the process to the kidney because casts form only in tubules, and the culture grows E. coli. A hapten-driven reaction to the newly started rifampin produces an infiltrate of lymphocytes, macrophages, and often eosinophils, sometimes with rash (about 25%) and often with eosinophils in the urine, whereas his urine has neutrophil casts, no eosinophils, and a positive culture. Tubular epithelial necrosis sheds necrotic epithelium into casts rather than producing neutrophil casts with bacteriuria. Necrosis of the pyramid tips presents with excreted papillary tissue causing gross hematuria or colic, which he does not have. Light-chain casts arise from Bence-Jones proteinuria in multiple myeloma, and nothing suggests myeloma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (acute pyelonephritis morphology and leukocyte casts; drug-induced interstitial nephritis); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, contrast with suppurative pyelonephritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neutrophils and pus casts point to bacterial infection, whereas lymphocytes, macrophages, and eosinophils with rash and eosinophilia point to drug hypersensitivity. COMLEX: pyuria alone does not distinguish infection from a drug reaction, so casts, culture, and the infiltrating cell type must be weighed together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — urinary stasis lets bacteria multiply and ascend to the kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl35_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man becomes oliguric 2 days after deliberately ingesting a mercuric chloride solution; he was never hypotensive. His creatinine is 4.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Biopsy shows extensive necrosis along long, continuous tubular lengths, injured cells with large acidophilic inclusions, desquamated necrotic cells that are partly calcified, and casts in more distal segments. Which nephron segment shows the most extensive necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Thin limb of Henle in the inner medulla","B":"Inner medullary collecting duct near papilla","C":"Medullary thick ascending limb of Henle","D":"Proximal convoluted tubule of the cortex","E":"Cortical collecting duct lined by principal cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Toxic acute tubular injury is most obvious in the proximal convoluted tubules, where many toxins such as mercury cause extensive necrosis, and mercuric chloride characteristically leaves large acidophilic inclusions in injured cells that later desquamate and may calcify; this segment's large reabsorptive surface, active transport systems, and ability to concentrate toxins make it the prime target. The thin limb of Henle is not among the segments described as injured in the toxic pattern. The inner medullary collecting duct is a terminal segment whose lumen receives casts rather than being the main site of necrosis. The medullary thick ascending limb is vulnerable to ischemic injury because of its high oxygen use in the hypoxic outer medulla, whereas toxic injury concentrates in the proximal convoluted tubule. The cortical collecting duct likewise contains casts in toxic injury rather than primary necrosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (patterns of toxic acute tubular injury, mercuric chloride inclusions); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, toxin reabsorption and concentration by the PCT); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, selective vulnerability of the PCT and TAL).","boardPrep":"Step 1: toxic injury produces extensive necrosis along the proximal convoluted tubule, the segment whose large reabsorptive surface and active transport concentrate toxins. COMLEX: recovery is expected with nephrotoxic tubular injury when the toxin has not seriously damaged other organs, such as the liver or heart.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 3 — the sloughed necrotic cells form the casts that obstruct tubules during the maintenance phase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl35_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old man becomes oliguric after repair of a ruptured aortic aneurysm; he also received contrast and gentamicin. On day 4 his creatinine is 3.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Biopsy shows patchy necrosis of short tubular segments with skip areas and ruptured basement membranes, without inclusions or crystals. Which event most likely caused this injury?","choices":{"A":"Gentamicin given during postoperative care","B":"Prolonged hypotension from major blood loss","C":"Radiographic contrast given for the preoperative CT","D":"Carbon tetrachloride solvent exposure","E":"Ethylene glycol with intratubular oxalate crystals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patchy necrosis of short tubular segments separated by skip areas, with rupture of basement membranes (tubulorrhexis), is the ischemic pattern of acute tubular injury, which follows inadequate perfusion with severe hypotension or shock, as expected after a ruptured aneurysm. Gentamicin and radiographic contrast are nephrotoxic causes, and nephrotoxic injury produces extensive, continuous necrosis along long stretches of the proximal convoluted tubule with basement membranes generally intact. Carbon tetrachloride is also a nephrotoxin that would give the toxic proximal pattern. Ethylene glycol causes vacuolar degeneration of proximal tubular cells with calcium oxalate crystals in the lumens, both absent. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, ischemic vs. nephrotoxic ATI differences).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic tubular injury is patchy, affecting relatively short tubular lengths with skip areas, whereas nephrotoxic injury follows drugs such as gentamicin and radiographic contrast, heavy metals, and organic solvents. COMLEX: in shock from sepsis, burns, or multiorgan failure, mortality from acute tubular injury can exceed 50%.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — the outer medulla's high oxygen use and limited supply make it the first zone to fail in hypotension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl35_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":3,"stem":"In a rat model of ischemic tubular injury during the oliguric phase, a filtered marker that is neither reabsorbed nor secreted is microinjected into injured tubules. Much of it never reaches the final urine and appears in the peritubular space, and interstitial pressure is raised. Which mechanism do these findings demonstrate?","choices":{"A":"Afferent constriction from high macula densa NaCl","B":"Luminal obstruction by casts of sloughed cells","C":"Reduced glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient","D":"Endothelin-driven constriction of renal arterioles","E":"Leak of filtrate back across damaged tubule walls"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the maintenance phase, glomerular filtrate in the lumen of damaged tubules can leak back into the interstitium through injured epithelium and ruptured basement membranes, causing interstitial edema, increased interstitial pressure, further ischemic damage, and a fall in effective GFR, exactly what the lost marker demonstrates. Afferent constriction from increased distal sodium delivery is tubuloglomerular feedback, which lowers filtration but would not move a filtered marker into the interstitium. Cast obstruction raises intratubular pressure, but the marker would be retained in the lumen rather than appearing in the peritubular space. A reduced ultrafiltration coefficient lowers filtration at the glomerulus and does not explain loss of already filtered marker. Endothelin-driven vasoconstriction reduces glomerular blood flow but likewise leaves filtered marker in the tubule. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (pathogenesis of acute tubular injury, back-leak and decreased GFR); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, intrarenal feedback loops maintaining depressed GFR).","boardPrep":"Step 1: depressed GFR in acute tubular injury is maintained by tubuloglomerular feedback, cast obstruction, filtrate back-leak, and intrarenal vasoconstriction. COMLEX: the maintenance phase brings oliguria of 40 to 400 mL per day with salt and water overload, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback is the intrinsic loop that also lowers GFR in tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl35_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":3,"stem":"On day 16 after ischemic acute tubular injury, a 56-year-old woman's urine output has risen steadily to 3 L/day. Her creatinine has fallen to 1.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), sodium is 132 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and potassium is 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which feature best explains her urinary losses of water, sodium, and potassium?","choices":{"A":"Immature regenerating tubules with poor reabsorption","B":"Persistent cast obstruction of the collecting ducts","C":"Global glomerulosclerosis reducing filtration","D":"Afferent constriction from tubuloglomerular feedback","E":"Excess ADH acting on collecting duct principal cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The recovery phase is ushered in by a steady rise in urine volume that may reach up to 3 L/day, and because the regenerating tubules are still damaged and functionally immature, large amounts of water, sodium, and potassium are lost in the flood of urine until tubular function and concentrating ability are restored. Persistent cast obstruction would lower urine output, not raise it. Global glomerulosclerosis reduces filtration and does not produce a solute diuresis with a falling creatinine. Tubuloglomerular feedback constricts the afferent arteriole and lowers GFR, which belongs to the oliguric phase. ADH acting on principal cells increases water reabsorption and would concentrate the urine rather than cause polyuria. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (clinical phases of acute tubular injury, recovery phase); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, hazards of the diuretic recovery phase); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (ADH action on principal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the recovery phase brings polyuria, falling BUN and creatinine, and risk of hypokalemia and other electrolyte losses. COMLEX: patients in the recovery phase also have a peculiar vulnerability to infection, so watch for sepsis as well as volume depletion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — segmental potassium handling explains why damaged tubules waste potassium","isHighYield":true}],"36":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":1,"stem":"Which arteriolar lesion characterizes benign nephrosclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","B":"Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles","C":"Cholesterol atheroemboli in arcuate arteries","D":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","E":"Transmural vasculitis of interlobular arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Benign nephrosclerosis is characterized by thickening and hyalinization of arteriolar walls, hyaline arteriolosclerosis, with narrowed lumens, plus fibroelastic hyperplasia of larger arteries. The resulting ischemia causes glomerulosclerosis and tubulointerstitial scarring. Onion-skin thickening and fibrinoid necrosis characterize malignant hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyaline arteriolosclerosis is the lesion of benign hypertension and diabetes. COMLEX: uncomplicated nephrosclerosis is usually slowly progressive, and renal failure is more likely in African American patients and with severe hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II and renin regulate arteriolar tone and glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":1,"stem":"What is the gross appearance of the kidneys in benign nephrosclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Coarse polar scars overlying blunted dilated calyces","B":"Normal to moderately small kidneys with fine leathery cortex","C":"Wedge-shaped pale infarcts with hyperemic borders","D":"Markedly enlarged kidneys filled with multiple cysts","E":"Symmetrically small pale kidneys with smooth cortices"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In benign nephrosclerosis the kidneys are normal or moderately reduced in size, weighing 110 to 130 g, and the cortical surfaces have a fine, even granularity resembling grain leather from microscopic subcapsular scars. Coarse polar scars suggest chronic pyelonephritis, and wedge-shaped infarcts suggest embolic occlusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fine granular leathery cortex with hyaline arteriolosclerosis identifies benign nephrosclerosis. COMLEX: the loss of mass is mainly from cortical scarring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II and renin regulate arteriolar tone and glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":1,"stem":"Which findings define the renal lesion of malignant hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Onion-skin arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis","B":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis with subcapsular glomerular scars","C":"Atheromatous plaque at the origin of the renal artery","D":"Medial fibrous thickening with a string-of-beads appearance","E":"Glomerular crescents with linear IgG along the basement membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Malignant hypertension shows proliferation of intimal cells producing an onion-skin appearance, hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, along with fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles and glomeruli, sometimes with intraglomerular microthrombi. It is defined by severe elevations of pressure, renal failure, and retinal hemorrhages with or without papilledema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: onion skin equals hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, and it is seen in malignant hypertension. COMLEX: the renal lesions overlap with thrombotic microangiopathy but the molecular defects differ.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II and renin regulate arteriolar tone and glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":1,"stem":"What is the usual trigger of typical, diarrhea-positive hemolytic uremic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies that inhibit ADAMTS13","B":"Mutation of complement factor H","C":"Antiphospholipid antibodies","D":"Heparin-dependent antibodies to platelet factor 4","E":"Shiga-like toxin from E. coli O157:H7"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Typical HUS follows intestinal infection with Shiga-like toxin-producing strains of E. coli, most commonly O157:H7, and less commonly Shigella dysenteriae. The toxin causes endothelial injury, platelet activation, and microvascular thrombosis. ADAMTS13 inhibitors cause TTP, and complement mutations and antiphospholipid antibodies are associated with atypical HUS or other secondary microangiopathies. Heparin-dependent antibodies cause platelet consumption and thrombosis, not a diarrhea-associated microangiopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef followed by anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure in a child is typical HUS. COMLEX: antibiotics are avoided in suspected E. coli O157:H7 infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 3 — thrombotic microangiopathy can also complicate lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":1,"stem":"Which abnormality underlies thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura?","choices":{"A":"Deficient ADAMTS13 protease activity","B":"Deficient complement regulatory factor H","C":"Deficient endogenous antithrombin III","D":"Deficient platelet glycoprotein Ib receptors","E":"Deficient plasma coagulation factor VIII"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TTP is characterized by inherited or acquired deficiency of ADAMTS13, the plasma metalloprotease that cleaves large multimers of von Willebrand factor. Ultralarge multimers accumulate and activate platelets spontaneously, forming microthrombi. Most cases are due to inhibitory autoantibodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TTP means ADAMTS13 deficiency and platelet-rich microthrombi. COMLEX: plasma exchange removes autoantibodies and supplies functional enzyme.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 3 — thrombotic microangiopathy can also complicate lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination is characteristic of hemolytic uremic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Warm autoimmune hemolysis, leukocytosis, and acute hepatic failure","B":"Hemolytic anemia with target cells, neutropenia, and splenomegaly","C":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and kidney injury","D":"Iron deficiency anemia, thrombocytosis, and nephrotic syndrome","E":"Macrocytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and a peripheral neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thrombotic microangiopathy leads to deposition of thrombi in the microvasculature with red cell hemolysis, tissue ischemia and organ dysfunction, and a consumptive thrombocytopenia. In HUS the kidney is the dominant organ affected, whereas in TTP central nervous system involvement is dominant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schistocytes on smear indicate microangiopathic hemolysis. COMLEX: HUS causes renal failure and TTP causes neurologic symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 3 — thrombotic microangiopathy can also complicate lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"How is chronic kidney disease defined?","choices":{"A":"GFR below 90 mL/min/1.73 m2 for at least 1 month or transient proteinuria","B":"GFR below 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 for 3 months or persistent albuminuria","C":"GFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 for at least 6 months with a rising creatinine","D":"Serum creatinine above 1.0 mg/dL for at least 6 weeks with hematuria","E":"BUN above 20 mg/dL with hypertension lasting at least 3 months"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic kidney disease is defined by a diminished GFR persistently less than 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 for at least 3 months and, or, persistent albuminuria. It is the end result of a variety of renal diseases, most commonly diabetic and vascular kidney disease. A lower GFR threshold held for 1 month or a creatinine cutoff over 6 weeks does not meet the 3-month definition, and transient proteinuria is not persistent albuminuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duration of at least 3 months distinguishes CKD from acute kidney injury. COMLEX: diabetic and vascular kidney disease are the most common causes of CKD (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — hyperfiltration and podocyte injury drive progressive glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"Which are the most common underlying causes of chronic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Polycystic kidney disease and Alport syndrome","B":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis and pyelonephritis","C":"Obstructive uropathy and nephrolithiasis","D":"Diabetic and vascular kidney disease","E":"Lupus nephritis and IgA nephropathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic kidney disease is the end result of a variety of renal diseases, most commonly diabetic kidney disease and vascular kidney disease, and it is particularly common in people over age 60. Inherited, infectious, obstructive, and autoimmune diseases account for a smaller share.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes and hypertension lead the list of causes of CKD and ESKD. COMLEX: screen with urine albumin and eGFR in these patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — hyperfiltration and podocyte injury drive progressive glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins' classic description of terminal uremia, what GFR defines end-stage kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Less than 50 percent of normal","B":"Less than 35 percent of normal","C":"Less than 5 percent of normal","D":"Less than 25 percent of normal","E":"Less than 60 percent of normal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In end-stage kidney disease the GFR is less than 5 percent of normal, the terminal stage of uremia, at which dialysis or transplantation is required to sustain life. The other thresholds correspond to earlier stages of chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins gives terminal ESKD as a GFR below 5 percent of normal, the point at which dialysis or transplantation is required. COMLEX: manifestations include anemia, hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalemia, and acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — hyperfiltration and podocyte injury drive progressive glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with long-standing benign hypertension dies of a stroke. At autopsy, the kidneys are moderately small with a finely granular cortical surface. Besides hyaline thickening of the arterioles, the interlobular and arcuate arteries show medial hypertrophy, reduplication of the internal elastic lamina, and increased myofibroblastic tissue in the intima. Which term describes the change in these arteries?","choices":{"A":"Fibroelastic hyperplasia","B":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","C":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","D":"Fibrinoid arteriolar necrosis","E":"Fibromuscular dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In nephrosclerosis, the interlobular and arcuate arteries show medial hypertrophy, replication of the internal elastic lamina, and increased myofibroblastic tissue in the intima, which narrow the lumen; Robbins calls this fibroelastic hyperplasia, which often accompanies hyaline arteriolosclerosis and increases with age and hypertension. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is the glassy thickening of arteriolar walls from leaked plasma proteins, the companion lesion described separately. Hyperplastic (onion-skin) arteriolosclerosis and fibrinoid necrosis are the lesions of severe (malignant) hypertension. Fibromuscular dysplasia is a cause of renal artery stenosis, not the small-artery change of nephrosclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Benign nephrosclerosis: normal to moderately small kidneys with a fine leathery granular surface, hyaline arteriolosclerosis, and fibroelastic hyperplasia of interlobular and arcuate arteries; malignant hypertension: onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis and fibrinoid necrosis. COMLEX: Patients of African descent and those with severe blood pressure elevations are at higher risk of renal failure from nephrosclerosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — vascular narrowing feeds the common pathway of nephron loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with severe hypertension has unilateral atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis. Which pair of findings in the two kidneys is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Small ischemic kidney with severe arteriolosclerosis and contralateral mild arteriolosclerosis","B":"Enlarged ischemic kidney with hydronephrosis and contralateral cortical atrophy","C":"Small ischemic kidney with fibrinoid necrosis and contralateral normal vessels","D":"Normal ischemic kidney with hyaline change and contralateral onion-skin lesions","E":"Small ischemic kidney with mild arteriolosclerosis and contralateral severe arteriolosclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ischemic kidney is reduced in size with diffuse atrophy, crowded glomeruli, and atrophic tubules, but its arterioles are usually protected from high pressure and show only mild arteriolosclerosis. The contralateral nonischemic kidney is exposed to the elevated pressure driven by renin from the ischemic side and may show more severe arteriolosclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal artery stenosis raises renin, angiotensin II, and blood pressure, which then damages the unprotected contralateral kidney. COMLEX: atherosclerosis causes 70 percent of stenosis and fibromuscular dysplasia most often affects young women.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — renin and angiotensin II as the cause of renovascular hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with bilateral renal artery stenosis starts lisinopril, and his creatinine rises sharply. Which mechanism best explains the fall in GFR?","choices":{"A":"Afferent constriction from unopposed prostaglandins lowers filtration pressure","B":"Increased Bowman space pressure from ureteral obstruction opposes filtration","C":"Efferent dilation removes angiotensin II support of glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Direct immune injury to podocytes reduces the filtration coefficient","E":"Volume expansion raises plasma oncotic pressure and reduces net filtration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With bilateral stenosis, perfusion pressure is low, and angiotensin II constricts efferent arterioles to preserve glomerular capillary pressure and GFR. ACE inhibitors dilate the efferent arteriole, abolishing this compensation, so GFR falls, which can precipitate acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs are contraindicated in bilateral renal artery stenosis. COMLEX: a sharp creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor should prompt a search for renal artery stenosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — efferent constriction by angiotensin II and its inhibition by ACE inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy had bloody diarrhea five days ago and now has pallor, oliguria, schistocytes on the smear, thrombocytopenia of 40,000/mm3 (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm3), and a creatinine of 3.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.3–0.7 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains his kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies inhibit ADAMTS13 and ultralarge vWF multimers form","B":"An inherited factor H defect causes uncontrolled alternative pathway activation","C":"Immune complexes deposit in the mesangium and activate the classical pathway","D":"Shiga-like toxin injures endothelium and drives platelet microthrombi","E":"Antiphospholipid antibodies activate endothelium and promote thrombosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diarrhea followed by microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure in a child is typical HUS. Shiga-like toxin activates endothelial cells, increases adhesion molecules and endothelin, decreases nitric oxide, and may cause endothelial apoptosis, leading to platelet activation and microvascular thrombi. ADAMTS13 inhibition, factor H defects, and antiphospholipid antibodies are the mechanisms of other TMAs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: typical HUS follows O157:H7 diarrhea, and recovery is expected in most children. COMLEX: supportive care with dialysis when necessary is the mainstay.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — severe hypertension also produces endothelial injury and microvascular disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has fever, confusion, schistocytes, a platelet count of 15,000/mm3 (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm3), and a creatinine of 1.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Activity of the von Willebrand factor-cleaving protease is below 10 percent. Which diagnosis and treatment are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"TTP from ADAMTS13 autoantibodies, treated with plasma exchange","B":"Typical HUS from Shiga-like toxin, treated with antibiotics","C":"Atypical HUS from factor H mutation, treated with platelet transfusion","D":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation, treated with heparin infusion","E":"Malignant hypertension, treated with intravenous vasodilators"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fever, neurologic symptoms, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal dysfunction form the pentad of TTP, and severely deficient ADAMTS13 activity confirms it. Autoantibodies are the usual cause, and plasma exchange removes them and supplies functional enzyme, which is effective in most patients. Central nervous system involvement dominates while severe renal injury is uncommon. Typical HUS follows Shiga-like toxin diarrhea and atypical HUS reflects complement regulator defects, neither defined by absent ADAMTS13, and disseminated intravascular coagulation and malignant hypertension are not caused by this protease deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal ADAMTS13 levels distinguish atypical HUS from TTP. COMLEX: platelet transfusion is generally avoided in TTP because it can worsen thrombosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — malignant hypertension also injures endothelium and causes fibrinoid arteriolar necrosis, but ADAMTS13 activity is normal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman without diarrhea has recurrent microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and renal failure, with normal ADAMTS13 activity and negative Shiga toxin testing. Which mechanism best explains her disease?","choices":{"A":"Shiga-like toxin binding to endothelial receptors in the glomerulus","B":"Alternative pathway overactivation from a defective regulator","C":"Inhibitory antibodies that block ADAMTS13 activity","D":"Immune complex deposition with consumption of C1q","E":"Autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Atypical, diarrhea-negative HUS is most often complement-mediated TMA, in which mutations of regulatory proteins such as factor H, factor I, and CD46, or autoantibodies against them, permit uncontrolled alternative pathway activation and endothelial injury. Normal ADAMTS13 excludes TTP, and patients fare worse than in typical HUS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: factor H protects cells by breaking down the alternative pathway C3 convertase. COMLEX: complement inhibitors treat complement-mediated TMA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — immune complexes activate complement in lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with CKD has 3 g/day of proteinuria and progressive scarring of the interstitium. Which mechanism best links filtered protein to fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Direct collagen deposition by leaked protein in the interstitium","B":"Luminal protein casts block tubules and cause hydronephrosis","C":"Lower plasma oncotic pressure causes ischemia of the renal cortex","D":"Tubular protein uptake activates inflammatory and TGF-beta pathways","E":"Protein-stimulated natriuretic peptide release causes volume loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein that leaks through a damaged glomerular barrier is taken up by tubular cells, which activate inflammatory pathways and profibrotic mediators such as TGF-beta, driving interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy. Interstitial fibrosis correlates with progression to end-stage disease. Hypoalbuminemia lowers plasma oncotic pressure and causes edema, not cortical ischemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TGF-beta stimulates extracellular matrix production and is the central profibrotic cytokine in CKD. COMLEX: reduce proteinuria with RAAS blockade to slow progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — loss of barrier size and charge selectivity delivers protein to tubules, driving secondary tubulointerstitial injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with diabetic kidney disease and CKD stage 3 has surviving glomeruli under increased workload. Which change most directly drives further nephron loss?","choices":{"A":"Falling glomerular flow that lowers capillary pressure and filtration","B":"Hypertrophy with capillary hypertension that injures podocytes","C":"Regression of glomerular size with normal capillary pressure","D":"Increased podocyte number that thickens the filtration barrier","E":"Reduced single-nephron GFR that spares the remaining glomeruli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When the number of functional nephrons falls, surviving glomeruli hypertrophy and experience increased blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure. The resulting hyperfiltration injures podocytes, leading to glomerulosclerosis and further nephron loss in a vicious cycle toward end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperfiltration is the common pathway of progression in CKD. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors slow this process by lowering glomerular capillary pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — single-nephron capillary hypertension and podocyte injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with long-standing diabetic nephropathy has a steadily falling GFR despite good glycemic and blood pressure control. Which sequence best describes the common pathway of his progressive kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Nephron loss, hypoperfusion, tubular hypertrophy, podocyte hyperplasia, and regeneration","B":"Podocyte hypertrophy, mesangial atrophy, capillary dilation, filtration decline, and sclerosis","C":"Nephron loss, hyperfiltration, podocyte injury, glomerulosclerosis, and TGF-beta-mediated fibrosis","D":"Interstitial fibrosis, tubular hypertrophy, glomerular hyperplasia, hypertension, and nephron gain","E":"Hyperfiltration, nephron gain, podocyte proliferation, capillary regeneration, and reversal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Progression begins with loss of nephrons, which causes compensatory hyperfiltration and capillary hypertension in surviving glomeruli. This injures podocytes, causing glomerulosclerosis, and TGF-beta-mediated interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy follow, driving the kidney toward end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the final common pathway is nonspecific and includes glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and arteriosclerosis. COMLEX: this explains why renal function can decline even after the original insult is removed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — maladaptive hyperfiltration and secondary global glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl36_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with 25 years of mild, treated hypertension has a creatinine of 1.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His kidneys are symmetrically small with a finely granular cortical surface. Biopsy shows homogeneous pink thickening of afferent arteriolar walls with narrowed lumens. Which process produced the arteriolar lesion?","choices":{"A":"Concentric intimal smooth muscle proliferation","B":"Fibrin insudation with smooth muscle necrosis","C":"Lipid plaque formation at the arterial origin","D":"Plasma protein leakage into arteriolar walls","E":"Immune complex deposition in arteriolar walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-standing mild hypertension in an older adult with small, finely granular (\"leather-grain\") kidneys is benign nephrosclerosis, whose defining lesion is hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent arterioles: endothelial injury and hemodynamic pressure drive extravasation of plasma proteins into the vessel wall, forming homogeneous pink hyaline deposits that narrow the lumen and cause patchy ischemic atrophy. Concentric intimal smooth muscle proliferation is the onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis of malignant hypertension. Fibrin insudation with smooth muscle necrosis is fibrinoid necrosis, also a malignant-hypertension lesion. A lipid plaque at the arterial origin is atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis of the main artery, not an arteriolar lesion. Immune complex deposition is a glomerular and vasculitic mechanism, not the basis of hyaline change. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, benign vs. malignant nephrosclerosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Benign nephrosclerosis shows hyaline arteriolosclerosis and a leather-grain cortex; malignant nephrosclerosis shows fibrinoid necrosis, onion-skinning, and a flea-bitten surface. COMLEX: Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is also typical of diabetes, so control both blood pressure and glucose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — chronic ischemia from narrowed renal vessels adds to the nephron loss of progressive kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl36_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has headache, a blood pressure of 230/140 mm Hg, and a creatinine that has risen to 3.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Biopsy shows fibrinoid necrosis of afferent arterioles and onion-skin thickening of interlobular arteries. Which process most directly sustains the rise in his blood pressure?","choices":{"A":"ANP release from stretched atrial myocytes","B":"Aldosterone escape with sodium diuresis","C":"Ischemia-driven renin release by the kidney","D":"Adenosine-mediated efferent arteriolar dilation","E":"Baroreceptor reset in the carotid sinus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fibrinoid necrosis and onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis mark malignant nephrosclerosis. The narrowed vessels make the parenchyma ischemic, ischemia drives the juxtaglomerular apparatus to secrete renin, and the resulting angiotensin II and aldosterone raise systemic pressure, which injures the vessels further and worsens the ischemia: a self-perpetuating renovascular vicious cycle. ANP release from stretched atria lowers rather than raises pressure. Aldosterone escape is a natriuresis that limits volume expansion, not a driver of the cycle. Adenosine acts on afferent A1 receptors to constrict them in tubuloglomerular feedback and does not sustain systemic hypertension. Baroreceptor resetting permits chronic hypertension but is not the renal process the lecture describes. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, renin-mediated renovascular vicious cycle).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In malignant hypertension, renal ischemia drives renin release, which raises pressure and injures vessels further in a vicious cycle. COMLEX: Malignant hypertension with acute kidney injury needs prompt, controlled blood pressure reduction to break the cycle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II from this cascade adjusts afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl36_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has 2 days of fever, headache, and intermittent confusion. Hemoglobin is 8.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with schistocytes, platelets are 9,000/μL (reference range: 150,000–400,000/μL), and creatinine is 1.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). She has no diarrhea, her blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg, and ADAMTS13 activity is markedly reduced with an inhibitory autoantibody. Which event initiates her microthrombi?","choices":{"A":"Primary injury to the glomerular endothelium","B":"Alternative complement pathway overactivation","C":"Immune complex deposition in arteriolar walls","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis from severe hypertension","E":"Platelet clumping on very large vWF multimers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fever, neurologic symptoms, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, severe thrombocytopenia, and renal dysfunction with an autoantibody against ADAMTS13 is thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, in which very large von Willebrand factor multimers accumulate, bind platelet surface glycoproteins, and activate platelets spontaneously, so platelet aggregation is the initiating event. Endothelial injury is not the primary event when ADAMTS13 activity is markedly reduced, because the oversized multimers activate platelets directly. Complement overactivation is not the mechanism of a protease-deficient disorder. Immune complex deposition is a mechanism of glomerulonephritis, not of microthrombus formation. Fibrinoid necrosis belongs to severe hypertension, and her blood pressure is normal. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (thrombotic microangiopathies, pathogenesis of TTP); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, ADAMTS13-deficient TTP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in TTP, very large vWF multimers left uncleaved by a deficient ADAMTS13 bind and activate platelets spontaneously, which explains the profound thrombocytopenia and widespread arteriolar microthrombi. COMLEX: plasma exchange removes the autoantibodies and supplies functional ADAMTS13, allowing successful treatment in more than 80% of patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1 — severe hypertension can mimic TMA through endothelial injury and microangiopathic hemolysis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl36_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has bloody diarrhea from Escherichia coli O157:H7. Five days later he has schistocytes, platelets of 41,000/μL (reference range: 150,000–400,000/μL), and creatinine of 3.4 mg/dL (reference range for age: 0.2–0.5 mg/dL). He dies, and the kidneys show patchy cortical necrosis, capillary loops occluded by thrombi, and mesangial destruction. Injury to which renal cell type initiates the microangiopathy?","choices":{"A":"Visceral podocytes on the glomerular basement membrane","B":"Mesangial cells within the glomerular stalk","C":"Proximal tubular cells that reabsorb filtrate","D":"Endothelial cells of the small blood vessels","E":"Interstitial fibroblasts of the renal cortex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diarrhea-positive disease after infection with E. coli O157:H7 is typical hemolytic uremic syndrome, and the Shiga-like toxin is thought to activate endothelial cells, which increase leukocyte adhesion molecules and endothelin and decrease nitric oxide, with apoptosis in the presence of cytokines such as TNF; endothelial injury is the primary event, and platelets are activated only secondarily, with vasoconstriction. Visceral podocytes are the target in podocytopathies such as minimal change disease, not the first cell injured by this toxin. Mesangial destruction is a common finding in HUS and TTP, often resulting from disruption of the mesangial matrix and damage to mesangial cells, but it follows the microvascular injury rather than initiating it. Tubular epithelium is not the primary target of Shiga toxin's microangiopathy. Interstitial fibroblasts respond later to chronic injury and are not the toxin's target. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (typical hemolytic uremic syndrome, endothelial injury, TMA morphology); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, Shiga-toxin-mediated HUS).","boardPrep":"Step 1: typical HUS is classically triggered by Shiga-like toxin from E. coli O157:H7, and the morphology of HUS and TTP is indistinguishable, so the initiating mechanism must come from the clinical story. COMLEX: most patients with less severe renal failure recover function within weeks, but the long-term outlook is guarded, with only 10 of 25 patients in one study having normal renal function 15 to 25 years later.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 2 — nephrotoxic tubular injury targets tubular epithelium rather than the microvasculature","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl36_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with IgA nephropathy diagnosed 12 years ago has a creatinine of 2.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), an estimated GFR of 29 mL/min/1.73 m2 (reference range: >90 mL/min/1.73 m2), and proteinuria of 3.1 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h). His biopsy shows changes in all four renal compartments. Which biopsy feature correlates best with the decline in his renal function?","choices":{"A":"Percentage of globally sclerosed glomeruli","B":"Extent of tubulointerstitial fibrosis","C":"Severity of hyaline thickening in arterioles","D":"Intensity of mesangial IgA on immunofluorescence","E":"Density of eosinophils in the interstitium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the lecture's final common pathway, damaged tubular cells undergo epithelial-mesenchymal transition and secrete TGF-beta, and tubulointerstitial fibrosis shows the strongest correlation with long-term decline in renal function, because it reflects ischemia of tubules downstream of sclerotic glomeruli, loss of peritubular capillaries, and direct injury of tubular cells by filtered proteins. The percentage of globally sclerosed glomeruli measures glomerular injury, which correlates less well (Robbins Ch. 20 notes that tubulointerstitial damage often correlates better than glomerular injury). Hyaline arteriolar thickening reflects hypertensive or diabetic vascular injury and tracks function less closely. The intensity of mesangial IgA reflects immune deposits rather than accumulated damage. Interstitial eosinophils mark a hypersensitivity reaction, not chronic progression. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, TGF-beta-mediated fibrosis and ESKD); Robbins Ch. 20 (tubular injury and interstitial fibrosis in progression).","boardPrep":"Step 1: filtered proteins such as cytokines, complement, and immunoglobulins activate tubular cells, which release proinflammatory and growth factors that drive interstitial fibrosis. COMLEX: on a biopsy from chronic kidney disease, judge prognosis from the tubules and interstitium rather than from glomerular counts alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — proteinuria from barrier injury is itself toxic to tubular cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl36_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman had crescentic glomerulonephritis 4 years ago that is now inactive, with a bland sediment. Over 2 years her eGFR has fallen from 45 to 31 mL/min/1.73 m2 (reference range: >90 mL/min/1.73 m2), and proteinuria has risen to 1.8 g/24 h (reference range: <150 mg/24 h). Biopsy shows large glomeruli with segmental scars and no crescents or deposits. Which mechanism best explains her continued decline?","choices":{"A":"Capillary hypertension in the surviving nephrons","B":"Smoldering crescent formation in Bowman space","C":"Recurrent immune complex deposition in capillaries","D":"Afferent arteriolar constriction lowering filtration","E":"Renal artery stenosis with renin-driven ischemia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once disease destroys enough nephrons to reduce GFR to about 30% to 50% of normal, progression proceeds at a steady rate independent of the original insult; the surviving glomeruli hypertrophy with increased blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure, and this glomerular hypertension injures podocytes and endothelium, producing segmental and eventually global sclerosis. Smoldering crescent formation is excluded by the bland sediment and the absence of crescents on biopsy. Recurrent immune complex deposition is excluded by the absence of deposits on biopsy and the bland sediment. Afferent arteriolar constriction would lower glomerular pressure, the opposite of the adaptive afferent dilation that drives this injury. Renal artery stenosis causes diffuse ischemic atrophy of one kidney, not hypertrophied glomeruli with segmental scars. Sources: Robbins Ch. 20 (mechanisms of progression, FSGS in the setting of adaptive changes); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, the hyperfiltration model of progressive CKD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adaptive glomerulosclerosis follows any major loss of nephron mass, including reflux nephropathy and unilateral renal agenesis. COMLEX: proteinuria that appears years after the original injury, without ongoing disease activity, fits adaptive glomerulosclerosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — crescentic injury is a common way to lose enough nephrons to start the common pathway","isHighYield":true}]};
